      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>KAVARIS</head>
        <p>KAVARIS</p>
        <p>The new Food for Poultry and Young Animals. (Hargreave's Patent.) Makes Birds strong and healthy; eggs plentiful and fertile; lightens trouble and risk in rearing young stock; ensures good constitution; remarkable results. TRY IT.</p>
        <p>KAVARIS FOOD CO., Hutchinson Street, Widnes. Ask your Corn Dealer for " Kavaris."</p>
        <p>AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON, BRIDGE ST., WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>EXHIBITION POULTRY.</head>
        <p>EXHIBITION POULTRY.</p>
        <p>The Vital Importance of Proper Feeding and Grooming.</p>
        <p>HARD AND SOFT FEATHERED SECTIONS.</p>
        <p>[BY REDCAP.]</p>
        <p>Last week I dealt with this subject in a very general way. We must now get rnorej into the details. The right feeding of exhibition stock is very important. As a matter of fact, it matters little how good a specimen may be, unless it is shown in good condition it would stand a poor chance in the show pen. The kind of food given depends entirely on the breed or variety with which you are dealing. In the fancy all breeds come under two heads, namely, hard- leathered and soft-feathered varieties. All varieties of game fowls would be classified under the hard- feathered section, and such breeds as Orpingtons, Brahma 3, Cochins, Faverolles and the like are known as soft-feathered varieties. You would not, therefore, give the same kind of food to a game fowl as you would to a Cochin when feeding for show purposes. Then again, a special course of feeding is requisite with large-combed varieties, such as Minorcas, Leghorns and Anconas. With the ever popular little Bantams you are again confronted with the problem of right feeding.</p>
        <p>Let us now deal with the different sections. It takes longer to get a game fowl into show condition than most other breeds. Feather colouring takes longer in development. A well-known breeder of Old English Game (brown-breasted reds) of my acquaint- mace used to reckon six months at least to get a bird into good show condition. It can, however, be managed in less time than this. If you feed game fowls on hard foods, such as hard red wheat, Indian corn, white Canadian peas for a regular diet they are kept more or less in good hard condition. Avoid soft foods in all forms, and then with a little extra attention a few weeks prior to the show, you soon have them in tip-top condition. Give plenty of clean water, green food and grit. Continue the pains and give them each day just a little raw meat. It is a good plan when giving the meat to do so from your hand. Hold it from the top of the training pen. This method helps them to get used to strangers and also to show themselves to advantage. The feeding of the soft-feathered varieties should be on the following lines. A morning mash of the best biscuit meal dried off with best thirds. Dinner time, give a feed of stewed rice to which has been added a sprinkling of " Kavaris," this latter is a rare conditioner.* For the evening meal give a feed of wheat oats and buckwheat mixed. On alternate days a little meat will help matters wonderfully. With large combed and white lobed varieties, the aforesaid feed- ing menu will serve the purpose very well. Special attention, however, should be given for about two weeks prior to the show. The only alteration should be the motning meal. Substitute the biscuit meal mash for bread and milk. I used to show Minorcas and the method of feeding for condition was as follows: I got the top crust off a loaf, placed it on a plate and poured over it over as much boiling milk as the bread would soak up. Give this when cool enough. For the development of comb and lobe I know of no better diet. For the rest of the diet I used the foods already mentioned, with the exception of Kavaris, which was then unknown.</p>
        <p>When feeding exhibition stock, be careful not to leave any food lying about. See that every meal is cleared up and, should any be left, clear it away. Once a week add a little Epsom salts to the drinking water, this will help to keep the stock in good health. Avoid forcing condiments in all forms. If you have a decent specimen that is not just ready for the show you have selected, don't be tempted to unduly force it into condition. Be content to wait and try your luck elsewhere. I have seen many a good bird utterly ruined by this practice.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>PREPARING FOR EXHIBITION.</head>
        <p>PREPARING FOR EXHIBITION.</p>
        <p>We touched on this subject last week but only in so far as just training a bird was concerned. This is by no means all that remains to be done in preparation for show. Befcre a bird is dispitched for the exhibi- tion it needs a toilet. It is not necessary to wash dark- 'coloured birds, but all white and light plumaged birds must be washed. The successful washing of a white fowl for show purposes is not altogether an easy matter. It is so difficult that the mere novice should never make experiments on the ones intended for com- petition. He should always try his hand with a white fowl not intended for show purposes. Then, of course, if successful, he may venture a trial with his more valuable specimens. A fowl bath should be carried out on the following lines. Take four ordinary tin baths. In the first you must put the hot water and white soap; in the second hot water, the third warm water, and in the fourth tepid water. The first bath is intended for the real wash. Take the fowl and gently but -firmly immerse it into the water. Then rub the hot soapy water well into the feathers, let the soap be well disolved in the water so that it does not clog on the feathers. When you ate satisfied that ar the dirt is removed from the feathers, lift the bird out and place it into the second bath and thoroughly rinse out all the soap. The third bath is intended for a more finished rinsing. Now in the third bath, the tepid water should contain a slight addition of ordinary washing blue. This bleaches the feathers a little and helps to remove any yellowness of the feathers caused by the soap. Give the bird a thorough rinsing in the blue water. After this ordeal, for such it is, place the fowl in an open show basket before a hot fire. Let it be free from draughts but not too near the fire. This process is best when done at night time, because by morning the fowl is dry and ready to be put into the training pen awaiting the show. About two days before the show is the best time for the bath.</p>
        <p>All that the dark-coloured varieties need is just a wash of the legs and face, with a slight grooming of the feathers with an old silk handkerchief. Next week I will deal with some of the best breeds for an amateur to take up for exhibition purposes. (To be continued.)</p>
        <p>Old CHICALA A DRY FEED FOR CHICKERS AGENTS:</p>
        <p>CARTER &amp; SON,</p>
        <p>BRIDGE ST. WARRINGTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0025" n="art0025">
        <p>PATENT SELF RAISING FLOUR GROCERIOFT(/ MoLIOUCALL'S</p>
        <p>Eggs, Poultry, eze.</p>
        <p>WAR IRINGTOPI Itar ch.o. 141/. RELIC/01/S e-SOC/AL</p>
        <p>of the Association at the recent action of the University of Cambridge in abolishing tests of religions belief ia connection with its divinity degrees, deploring the refusal of Convocation of the University of Oxford to pass similar statutes of emancipation, and reiterating the Association's strong and unalterable convic- tion that no solution of the question would be satisfactory which stopped short of the complete abolition of religious tests in connection with university degrees and the appointment of professors and teachers. Mr. Drummond said that to impose tests of this kind in connection with university degrees was contrary to public policy. Oxford had no right to refuse theologians proper degrees. A university belonged to the people,and did not belong merely to the men who composed Convocation for the time being. The last relic of the old bad system of university tests must be swept away. (Applause.) Referring to the defeat of the proposals at Oxford by the non-residential vote, Mr. Drummond said it was not the Oxford of to- day which had refused to pass statutes such as Cam- bridge had, but the Oxford ot yesterday and the day before, whereas in Cambridge the decision was Came by the university of to-day. The resolution was adopted.</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 17, 1913.</p>
