      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0026" n="art0026">
        <p>h</p>
        <p>Anointing of the Sick.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell Pleads For Restoration of Primitive Customs.</p>
        <p>The Rev. A. M. Mitchell has an article in the current issue of the " Biirtonwood Parish Magazine," in which he contends that the Church has not been true to her mission of healing as applied to bodily sickness. He writes :—TLe Gospel of St. James, the Lord's Brother, received and practised for so many centuries, must be restored in all its fullness if disease is first of all to be checked, then overcome, and finally driven out. That Gospel reads : "Is any sick amongst us let him call for the elders (presbyters or priests) of the Church, and let them pray over him, anointing him with oil in the name of the Lord. And the prayer of faith shall save the sick, and the Lord shall raise him up and if he have committed sins they shall be forgiven him." When and why did the Gospel of twofold healing—of body and soul—cease to be heard, when precisely did it prove ineffective ? This is a question which has never been clearly ansxered, which still awaits a definite answer. That the sick and diseased were healed by prayer and anointing up to somewhere A.D. 800 would seem to be historical. These two essentials have been neglected by the Church to the grievous loss of many generations, (a) faith in her own power to heal, (b) prayerful use of anointing oil. The healing oil, which, in primitive times was prepared with so much care and used with such reverent discretion, ought, one would think, to be immediately restored. No convincing reason can be assigned for its abandonment; it is an item, an important item, an essential in the ministry of healing, without it we lack full Gospel.</p>
        <p>. P1:7.. --- -------,, As . .-.0011, %Amid ' - -4 D • ..--_ Z. 4 r5_414, 3 ; : :1 . . Zo ) et* Imme) AD</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0027" n="art0027">
        <head>PREVENTION BETTER TEIAN CURE.</head>
        <p>PREVENTION BETTER TEIAN CURE.</p>
        <p>But prevention is better than cure all the world over. Much, aye, the greater part of the disease all around is certainly of our own manufacture, and therefore pre- ventable. Disease is our own fault, our own most grievous fault. Defective sanitation, crowded vile environment, uncleanliness of person and life, wrong living generally, inferior food stuffs, haste in eating, have all helped to make us a nation of defectives and dyspeptics. Chronic indigestion is a sure foferunner of cancer, appendicitis, and such like. A picture of Mr. Gladstone on every meal table might serve a useful as well as ornamental purpose, for the G.O.M. was ever preaching the sound gospel—which he himself carefully practised—of slow eating, of careful mastication of food. Eating is no longer eating, it is bolting, it has become a race against time at motor speed. That disease is largely our own creating there can be no dis- puting. Marriage of the physically (and mentally) unfit is sanctioned and encouraged by the State and blessed by the Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0028" n="art0028">
        <head>St. John's Presbyterian Church</head>
        <p>St. John's Presbyterian Church</p>
        <p>The personality, mission and duty of the Spirit were the subjects Of the Rev. Wm. Reid's discourse at St. John's on Sunday evening last. The reiterated mascu- line pronoun was emphasised, as pointing to a personality rather than merely to an influence. Mr. Reid said in convicting of sin, which was the mission of the Spirit, His method was less to point to Sinai, or to the pit's mouth, than to the Christ of the Cross, whose witness He was. There, if anywhere, could men be convicted of sin. There also righteous- ness was seen to be not only right but possible, , and there the world was judged, and its unchanging rattitude towards holiness and purity disclosed. Further, said the preacher, His work was to build up not one fold, but one flock. It might take many folds to contain them, but in the Spirit they were one. Vary- ing tongues, customs, temperaments, thought, etc. demanded more than one fold,and unity; not uniformity, was something they rejoiced in and thanked God for. The bond to preserve that liberty was peace. As in the natural so in the spiritual world, there could only be harmony where there was variety and diversity. Unison would reduce them all to monotony. Not one gift of thought, enterprise, wit, speech or silence could be spared. In the several folds all these were needed to produce the harmony in which unity existed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0029" n="art0029">
        <head>Wycliffe_ P.S.A</head>
        <p>Wycliffe_ P.S.A</p>
        <p>The usual meeting ;vas held on Sunday last. Rev. T. T. James, M.A., presided. Mr. E. Wynne Jones, gave the address upon. What the letters P.S.A. stand for." On a previous occasion, he said, he had spoke upon the letters P.S.A. in a different way. That day he would take them to stand for "Purity, sympathy, and ambition." We could have no purity apart from the great ideals of Christ. In the religious, commercial and social worlds the true goal could only be attained by following the teachings and ideals of the man Christ Jesus. Nothing could settle the labour disputes like the teachings of Christ, which meant that each should seek the best for all. Selfishness was the great destroyer of peace. Sympathy was the grand combiner and nniter of all. The world around us needpd sympathy ; in fact, many were dying for want of sympathy. It was a great helper in all good work. Ambition was a spiritual, moral and human necessity. The man without ambition was never likely to succeed. Let our ambitions be for the good of ourselves and others, so gloryfying the great Giver of every good gift in our lives and natures. Mr. Bea wick led the prayer. Mr. Wt.j. Smith, of Scarborough, read the lesson. Two new members were welcomed. Mr. Williamson sang with good effect " A song of sleep" and" Trust and believe."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0030" n="art0030">
        <head>Unitarians and Divinity Degrees. Rev. W. H. Drummond's Protest Against</head>
        <p>Unitarians and Divinity Degrees. Rev. W. H. Drummond's Protest Against</p>
        <p>Tests.</p>
        <p>The annual business meeting of the British and Foreign Unitarian Association was held on Thursday in Essex Hall, Strand, London, the president, Mr. Charles Hawksley, presiding. The Chairman, in moving the adoption of the report, said that there seemed to be more enthusiasm in the North of England than in the South. There were many beautiful Unitarian churches in the North, and he thought they might with advantage have more in the South.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. H. Drummond (f nmerly of Warrington) moved a resolution placing on record the satisfaction</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0031" n="art0031">
        <head>Help from the Poor Box.</head>
        <p>Help from the Poor Box.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Coroner's Court on Wednesday Mr. S. Brighouse conducted an inquest on the body of Maggie Bancroft, aged 14 months, daughter of William Henry Bancroft, 11, General-street, who died in the Infirmary, as the result of scalds, on Friday.-- Mrs. Bancroft stated that at 3.30 on Saturday, May 3rd, she let her daughter Elizabeth take the child into the house of Thomas Gorst, 21, General-street, who had no children, but he and hi 3 VI ife were particularly fond of the deceased child. It appeared that the deceased was playing near the sofa and began to cross the kitchen floor. She caught the handle of the saucepan and spilled the contents over herself, receiving severe scalds. She was then taken to the Infirmary—A verdict of "Accidental death" was returned.—ln answer to the Coroner, Detective Sergeant Holland said the family were in poor circumstances. There was nothing coming into the house, except money earned by his wife for charing. The husband bad been ill for two or three weeks. There were three other children.—The Coroner said he was sorry to bear of the accident. He understood the husband bad been out of work for 11 months. He then gave Mr. Bancroft £3 out of the poor box. "It is not often," commented Mr. Brighouse, " I give halfcrowns, but I give something substantial in really deserving cases."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0032" n="art0032">
        <head>£lO,OOO BAIL IN CONFIDENCE TRICK CASE.</head>
        <p>£lO,OOO BAIL IN CONFIDENCE TRICK CASE.</p>
        <p>At Bow-street Alfred Morris,3B, and George Fletcher, 35, were committed for trial on charges of being con- cerned in stealing £3,500 by means of the " confidence trick" from Eric Walter Knowles. Bail was allowed each defendant, himself in £5,000 and two sureties of £2,500 each.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0033" n="art0033">
        <head>MILLIONAIRES AND PAUPERS.</head>
        <p>MILLIONAIRES AND PAUPERS.</p>
        <p>Rev. H. Ogden's Indictment of Socia Inequalities.</p>
        <p>"THE CHURCH BROUGHT INTO</p>
        <p>DISREPUTE."</p>
        <p>The Rev. H. Ogden, pastor of Golborne-street Bytist Church, preached on "Pierpont Morgan and £20,000,000. His hope for salvation." The preacher took as his text Matthew xvi. 26," What is al man profited if he shall gain the whole world, and lose his soul ? " The preacher went on to declare that we bring nothing into the world and can take nothing out of it, save our character. We face God with nothing save what is spiritual. Pierpont Morgan's body was conveyed across Europe in a sepulchre upon wheels. His body needed its rich attention no more than if it had been thrown in Bulgarian trenches. Dust returned to dust, even such well-fed dust as the body of a millionaire. Face to face with God, the question was: What shall a man give in exchange for his soul? The philosophy of the street corner, the escathology of the man in the street was summed up in the waggish phase : "Cheer up. you will be a long time dead." It was another form of "damning the consequences," as Lord Milner put it. But nothing was more certain than that we should reap what we had sown. What we were in character would be our final judgment before God. £20,000,000 was a fortune beyond our wildest imagination. Most of us were flurried at the sight of a£s note. Somebody told us that certain stars were 200 millions of miles away. It conveyed nothing to us. It was something huge that we could not grasp. £20,000,000 was beyond the reach of our mind. The attempt to comprehend it mattered little. What was the moral end physical effect of £20,000,000 in the hands of one man? THE TWO ENDS OF THE SOCIAL SCALE. It had been proved that a millionaire at one end of the social scale meant a swarm of paupers at the other. Then £20,000,000 in the hands of one man was social murder. What did the £20,000,000 represent? It represented in our present economic conditions £20,000,000 of that with which Providence had dowered man. It represented the necessities and luxuries of human life overwhelmingly in the bands of one man. His gold bought him anything, including muscle and brain, and yet probably Pierpont Morgan as a Robinson Crusoe would have starved. Would God hold a steward blameless that he should die worth £20,000,000, whilst nine-tenths of his fellow Americans were in 'poverty? Lloyd George told us that in this country property worth £276,000,000 was bequeathed in one year, and yet nine-tenths of those who died were too poor to leave any effects worth valuing for estate duties. Why should nine- tenths of a toiling nation live in poverty? One could understand the Egyptians. They said the children of Israel were slaves and treated them as such. The attitude of the South Americans in their championship of the slave owners was intelligible. --</p>
        <p>- - "ARRANT HUMBUG."</p>
        <p>But to talk about brotherhood, to snivel about the Fatherhood of God, when one brother possessed £20,000,000 and nine-tenths of his brothers were as poor as church mice was arrant humbug. What did brotherhood mean? Find a home where blood brothers were blood brothers indeed. The higher brother lends a hand up to the lower brother. When would the Church cease to talk of the Fatherhood of God until she insisted that brotherhood should mean something definite and practical ? Pierpont Morgan told us in one of the articles of his will that be committed his soul into the bands of his Saviour, in full confidence that, having redeemed it and washed it in His most precious blood,He would present it faultless before the throne of the Heavenly Father, and he entrusted his children to defend at all hazaras that doctrine. Would Christ %ash out the tears of a million weeping children who bad cried for bread because £20,000,000 had been in the hands of one man ? We could leave Pierpont Morgan with God. "The Judge of all the earth will do that which is right," but the blessed truth of the atonement of Christ for sin had not been helped by Pierpont Morgan's will or Pierpont Morgan's fortune. The man of observation had observed for long that the Church by preaching passive obedience had brought herself into disrepute. She had talked like a senile old woman about " Being content in the station of life in which it hag pleased God to place us." Man in the main was sick of being told " That) some are born to honour and some to dishonour," with a clerical interpretation. God had given his bountiful gifts to be shared by every child born upon this great earth, and the Church would not rest content until fortunes of £20,1100,000 were as extinct as King Rameses.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0034" n="art0034">
        <head>FARM LABOURERS.</head>
        <p>FARM LABOURERS.</p>
        <p>A STRIKE IN WEST CHESHIRE.</p>
        <p>A farm labourers' strike has occurred in a part of West Cheshire, between Eddisbury on the north and Cholmondeley on the south. The cost of living has been rising appreciably, even in the Cheshire villages. Some of the labourers have been long dissatisfied with their conditions, and dairy farmers (who are the employers in this case) are said to be doing very well at present. Last week the srike began in the district of Eaton-by-Tarporley, where a number of labourers, perhaps a dozen or so, left work without notice, after making individual application, and having their appli- cations refused, for a standard wage of £1 a week and some other concessions. The standard conditions existing before the trouble began were variable as between different districts, as between different farms in the same district, and as between different individuals on the sane farm. The direct money payment is estimated to be 16i., 175., or 18s. a week. There are known cases, where the money which the labourer receives at the end of the week is as little as 135., and there are others between 13s. and 16s. There exists another cause of confusion, in a system of what may be called bounties which the labourers receive in differing amount from their employers. From the farmers' point of view, the labourer receives, besides his money payment, but as part of his wages, free milk, 50s. harvest money, and a calf once a year. The labourer's contention would be that this is the total of a number of benefits of which most men get only a part. The result is that it is possible for some farmers to argue that their men already possess what they arts out on strike for. - - _ -</p>
        <p>The men are agreed on a demand for a standard wage of a pound a week. Beyond that, the particulars remain persistently uncertain. Each man's version differs, but a twelve hours' day, from six in the morn- ing to six at night, and overtime pay for work after that, is the other main condition. The Eddisbury- labourers, who are not out on strike, have been asking for a cottage rent free and a meal before milking as well as the pound a week. The men had neither an organiser nor an organisa tion when they came out, nor have they now. They merely associated themselves with a sort of picketing party, which went round from farm to farm on Monday and the following days, talking to the men while they were at work. If their persuasions were of avail the man would go at once to his employer, make his demand as definitely as he could on the spur of the moment, and, if he were refused, give up work and join the strikers. Some of the men who came out in that way were men who had done a life's service on the farms which they left, and nearly all the men on strike are married men.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0035" n="art0035">
        <head>LANCASHIRE LABOURERS' DEMANDS.</head>
        <p>LANCASHIRE LABOURERS' DEMANDS.</p>
        <p>Some hundreds of agricultural labourers in West Lancashire handed in applications for improved con- ditions of service on Sunday. The demands include a weekly holiday on t4aturday afternoons, 4s. per week increase, and an all-round ten hours per day, includ- ing meal times, and 6d. per hour overtime. The farmers regard the proposed conditions as impossible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <head>IRRIGATION IN AUSTRALIA.</head>
        <p>IRRIGATION IN AUSTRALIA.</p>
        <p>As a young country of practically limitless natural resources, Australia is dependent mainly on her great primary industries, and it is to the development and improvement of the country's agricultural, pastoral and mineral resources, the tremendous extent of which is only just beginning to be realized, that the best energies of her statesmen and leaders have been, and must be, directed. In no way has this been better exemplified than in the big irrigation scheme which was undertaken and so successfully carried out by the New South Wales Government on the Murrumbidgee river. In order to conserve sufficient water to ensure au adequate supply for the 30Q,000 acres of irrigable land included in the scheme, it was necessary to construct a dam at Burrinjuck, which is second only to the great A ssouan Dam on the Nile. The Dam, which is no less than 240 ft. high and SOOft. long with a thickness at the base of 1601 t, throws back a lake with a surface area of 12,740 acres and a volume sufficient to cover 766,324 acres to a depth of a foot. Of the irrigated area it may be said that in no part of the world to-day is a better proposi- tion open to settlers. That this is so is attested by all who have had the opportunity of inspecting the land and judging of its capabilities. A number of blocks have been reserved by the Government for British farmers with a small capital, and to any who may be interested, the Assistant Director of Immigration for New South Wales, 125, Cannon-street, London, E.C., will be glad to give further particulars.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0037" n="art0037">
        <head>IN THE GARDEN.</head>
        <p>IN THE GARDEN.</p>
        <p>Fruit Prospects for 1913.—Judging by the monthly agricultural report just published by the Board of Agriculture, the prospects for fruit this year are good. Blossom nearly everywhere has been abundant, and in most localities severe frost has not been experienced. In those districts where frost did occur about the middle of April, it does not appear to have done any considerable damage. From observa-</p>
        <p>(Continued in vezt column.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0038" n="art0038">
        <head>ir/List- p' Altk</head>
        <p>ir/List- p' Altk</p>
        <p>IN THE COURTS</p>
        <p>BENEFIT AND WAGES.</p>
        <p>Insurance Act Prosecution at Warrington.</p>
        <p>The first Insurance Act prosecution within the borough of Warrington was heard at the Police Court yesterday, before the Mayor (in the chair), Alderman James Evans, Bolton and Pierpoint, Messrs. S. Wright, G. Piling, T. Locker, and A. J. Jolley. Alfred Hudson, 47, Cnurch-street, was summoned for making a false statement to an officer of the Board of Trade, on the 15th February, in order to obtain unemployment benefit under Part 11. of the National Insurance Act._</p>
        <p>-Defendant pleaded not guilty. Mr. W. B. 'Forshaw, for tbe proserution, said that on Friday, the 14th February, the defent ant attended at the Labour Exchange arm signed the register for unem- ployment benefit. At 4.30 in the afternoon he obtained employment in the Electricity Department of the Cor- poration and was working until eleven o'clock at night, receiving 3s. 4d. On the Saturday he was employed from 7 a In. until Ii p.m. and received 4s. 7d. About 8.45 in the morning he told a man that he was going to the Labour Exchange to draw unemployment pay, but returned saying that the office was closed. About 11.30 he went again and returned just after 12 o'clock. On the 25th February he went to the Labour Exchange and drew unemployment benefit of Is. 2d. in respect of each day, although he had actually been working on those days. He asked the Bench to deal severely with the man. That was the first prosecution of the kind in Warrington. Mr. Jackson chief assistant borough electrical engineer, Fairfield-road, Stockton Heath, said defendant was paid at the rate of sd. per hour and worked on the days stated by Mr. Forshaw. John Henry Porter, stoker at the electricity works, gave evidence to the effect that he allowed the defend- ant to go out at the times stated on the Saturday morning to visit the Labour Exchange. Defendant did not say whether be had got the money or not. Daniel Baxter, coal trimmer at the electricity works, said defendant came back about paytime and said he had notj.received the money, but would get it on Monday. Alfred Duncan Hayes; clerk in the employ of the Labour Exchange, said that on the 28th February defendant drew ss. 10d. unemployment benefit. He made no statement on the 14th. Mr. Forahaw : In respect of which days was the money cannot say off-hand. Continuing, witness said there were two days in one week and three days in another. Two days were the 11th and 14th February and there were three days in the following week. Mr. Harrison, manager of the Labour Exchange, said be had an interview with the defendant, who said that he had had the payments, but understood that he had received nothing except what he was entitled to. He had signed the unemployment card on the 14th and 15th February by a mark, as he was not able to write. Defendant said that he was sorry, but he did not understand the rules. He had been off work a fortnight and he thought he was entitled to five days' pay. He was claiming for past days when he had not been working. In answer to the Magistrates' Clerk. Mr„Harrls3n said he did not discover the fraud until April. It was only through the defendant boasting that he had received out-of-work pay and wages at the same time that they found out. The Magistrates' Clerk said the defendant ought to have told them that he was working two days and he did not.</p>
        <p>-- --_ __ Mr. Harrison : The receipt card was not returned and it should have been when he obtained employment.</p>
        <p>The magistrates retired, and on their return the Mayor said defendant would be fined 10s. (including costs) with an alternative of seven days' imprisonment. In any future cases a severe penalty would be inflicted.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0039" n="art0039">
        <head>FOOTBALLER AND HIS WIFE. ---- Charge of Habitual Drunkenness.</head>
        <p>FOOTBALLER AND HIS WIFE. ---- Charge of Habitual Drunkenness.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington Police Court on Saturday, before Alderman Tinnion (in the chair), Alderman Bennett and Mr. A. J. Jolley, Thomas Knox Gibbs, 128, Hume-street, was charged at the instance of his wife with being an habitual drunkard. She asked for a separation order. Mr. Browne appeared for the complainant and was instructed by the N.S.P.C.C. to support the application. Mr. Steel represented the defendant. Mr. Browne said the wife applied fora free summons the previous week. Mr. Steel was then in Court, and practically supported the application. Mr. Steel : It is an absolute untruth. I didn't say a word to the Bench at all, though I happened to be in Court.</p>
        <p>The Chairman : It is rather strong language to use to a brother solicitor.</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: When a statement of that sort is made I am bound to refute it, and to refute it strongly. There is no foundation for it.</p>
        <p>Resuming his opening, Mr. Browne said the parties were married on February 26th, 1910, and there were four children, two sets of twins. fhey charged the man with being an habitual drunkard for the reason that the wife had already taken out two summonses against her husband for drunkenness, but on the intervention of friends she had withdrawn them. In addition, she had left him three times in consequence of his drunken habits. Owing to defendant's filthy disgusting habits, not of a man, but of a beast, it was impossible for the woman to live with him. Gibbs was employei at the works of Messrs. Rylands Bros., and could earn 375. per week. He was also a player in the Warrington" A" tam, and had received payment for certain matches. The defendant was never sober a single week-end, and returned home at all hours in the morning. He went to Lymm almost every Sunday and sometimes did not get home until 3 o'clock in the morning. Gibbs had been suspended at his work four times, and had been told that he had his last chance. The summons was made returnable for Friday, but he failed to appear, and a warrant was issued. The defend- ant had not given his wife as much as £1 for over two months. Inspector Wainwright, of the N.S.P.C.C. found that he did not provide sufficient food for his children. He smashed the crockery and furniture and threw food on the floor. The case was one in the which a separa- tion should be granted in the interests of both parties, and the defendant should pay at least £1 per week for the maintenance of his wife and family. Mrs. Gibbs said at first they lived in a good house, but now they had come down to one which was only 4s. 6d. per week rental. The furniture had been taken away because defendant would not pay for it. Defendant's father had provided her with money. _ _</p>
        <p>Mr. Steel: I think defendant went to the cup final at Leeds ?—Yes.</p>
        <p>And that was the cause of his breaking down.—No ; he came home sober that night.</p>
        <p>On Monday he missed his work, and do you know that for the rest of the week he was " grassed ? "—That has often been the case.</p>
        <p>Apart from the summons issued and withdrawn, have you had a separation agreement ?—Yes. One was prepared at my office, and he allowed you 12s. per week ?—Yes, but there were only two babies then. On each occasion you have gone hack after a short time ?—He has said that he would be different and we have gone back together. Don't you think you will go back to him this time? —No : it is impossible for me to live with him.</p>
        <p>By Mr. Browne : She had bad to bring coal from her parents' house in a bassinette. Mr. Steel, for the defendant explained that Gibbs r'id answer the summons, but he arrived at Court later. The unfortunate part of the case was the charge of habitual drunkenness made against the young rain, who was only 26 years of age, and his wife was 21. Gibbs was pre- pared to enter into an arrangement to support his wife and family, and offered 15s. per week. If he was adjudged to bean habitual drunkard it would damage his prospects. It was not in the interests of the parties that so order should be made on such a summons. He urged that an adjournment should be agreed upon to see if an arrangement could not be arrived at. Mr. Browne.—There is no dispute of the facts. The case was adjourned for 14 days.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0040" n="art0040">
        <head>Borough Police Court.</head>
        <p>Borough Police Court.</p>
        <p>TUESDAY. Before Mr. C. Jackson Holmes (in the chair) and</p>
        <p>Mr. NV. Richardson'.</p>
        <p>A SHOCKING CASE.—John James Birch, puddler, 258, Manchester-road, was summoned by his wife, Alice Birch, 42, Forster-street, for desertion, and she asked for a maintenance order.—Mr. Browne, who appeared for the complainant, said the parties were married in 1904. There were no children. The case was a 'very shocking one. The wife had had to enter the Work- house hospital, and had not received a penny from the defendant for 10 weeks. If she lived with the man again it meant instant death for her.—After hearing the wife, the Bench made an order for the payment of 10s. per week. No TICKET.—John Roberts,a stranger, pleaded guilty to having travelled on the London and North-Western</p>
        <p>•••••••••••••• Railway from Hooton to Warrington without bor,rilfe paid his fare and with intent to avoid payment' prisoner attempted to pass the ticket collector at d Quay by saying that he had been to see a frienad He told various tales, but finally admitted that be &amp;illy, journeyed from Hooton.—Roberts had nothing t° god was fined 10E., with an alternative of seven laY:Ded HUSBAND AS "CHANCELLOR."—The aeett summons issued by Ethel Lambert, 25, Frederick-sir tr against her husband, Herbert Lambert, railway lighter, 9, Park-place, came before the Bench* the Browne, who appeared for the wife, characterised eta, case as an extraordinary one, for it would be re,rni, hered that it was stated that the husband had utl feed brown bread for himself. The parties woe niafrfe- in November. Lambert had never given his Win r penny since they were married. He laid out the n'S-'to himself and so starved her that she was cornnelle' Of leave him. The case was adjourned for the purPsetbe the parties taking a house. The previous evening at defendant wrote to his wife, making an apPoiuta3-0 with her to inspect a house, but be failed to tnrn 0p,86, was not in Court that morning. The wife waited Or be hour with her baby, but the husband was nowhere too seen. She was willing to live with him if he coo make proper provision for her.—A further fidjournni for a week was granted.</p>
        <p>WEDNESD A Y. roori Before M:. J. W. Hayward (in the chair) APlewf Smethurst, and Messrs. C. Jackson Holmes Will her, A GIRL'S THEFTS.—Florence Mary Gertrude Arc op (17), shop assistant, 20, Ernest-street, was chargedobie remand with stealing articles of clothing,,,,s,rke, £4 2s. 98., the property of Messrs. Lee and i-i'";rhe Market Gate, by whom she had been employed .--sod Chief Constable recalled the facts of the case,. id, pointed out that the girl had sold some of the 00°,0 at ridiculously low prices. She had also niutil!)s valuable property in order to deceive her laadiBot, whom she told that the articles were damaged sc,t°olt The Rector (Canon Willis) and Mr. Firth (Police Otbe Missionary) had spent a great deal of time iPbef girl's interests, but she was beyond the control ol 0 father, who held a position in Manchester. or of 505:10 else. A home had now been found for her.—Ao°`:to was hound over for two years, en promising to gibe; the Church Pastoral Aid School, Chester, for - period. Her father became surety for her. BOUGHT IT IN THE COUNTRY—Mary Warre'foe Hargreaves, of no olied address, was remanded for Oro days on a charge of stealing a lady's coat, the pro Peso of some person unknown.—The Chief Constable) „eet the prisoner was seen at the corner of Merse3frs'iye offering stockings for sale for 28. to two womeo• ,;ttle of the women observed that the stockings were or "for or no value and prisoner then offered the jacket 25., saying value, she had bought it out in the couott't:be Detective Summers received information and found‘_oo prisoner coming out of the Lion Hotel Vaults two- -4.15. She denied having sold the coat, but when %of. fronted with the purchaser told several tales 99 she had come by it. That morning she said she the coat in the Park.</p>
        <p>THURSDAY. J. JoII</p>
