      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0046" n="art0046">
        <head>4, Bridge Foot, Warrington, May 9th, 1913.</head>
        <p>4, Bridge Foot, Warrington, May 9th, 1913.</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATIVE STORES AND POVERTY.</p>
        <p>Sir,—ln answer to the letter of " Labor Omnia Vincit " appearing in your issue of May 10th, I am quite willing to discuss the question on the basis of the three assertions made by him, but I do so in the spirit conveyed in his own sentence :—" I would welcome at any conference any suggestion being brought to light that would assist in the enlighten- ment of us all." I emphasise this because his opening sentence contrasts very strangely with it, and I ques- tion whether another person understanding English would endorse his statement that "it is perfectly clear to at,yone reading his letter that all we have to do to abolish poverty is to do all that Mr. Morton says we ought to do." Where this is the sort of " welcome " a suggestion receives I am afraid there can be little enlightenment. I take the three points in reverse order and begin with No. 3, "That the Socialist solution of common ownership of the source of wealth production is the only natural, therefore, scientific solution to the poverty problem." I think, on the contrary, that for any body of men to lay down definite forms to which societies or industrial systems shall conform is unnatural and unscientific because social evolution proceeds through the individual, and the form of society naturally conforms to, and varies with, the progressive changes in the individual. If we take a retrospective view of the course of evolution of the human race and of social relationships from a state of barbarism to the present stage of progress, nothing could be plainer than that any and all attempts to eegraft a Utopia, whether it be that of a Plato, Sir Thomas More, or a Faurier, on a society not adapted to it must end in failure from the fact that the natural course of development is opposed to the artificial limitations imposed by the Utopia. Societies and social systems must, therefore, shape themselves on the basis of the individual and any attempt to establish an artificial system which requires the indi- vidual to conform to it is unnatural and therefore unscientific. No. 2, "That Mr. Morton's unemployed scheme is unscientific and cannot have a lasting benefit." I may say in passing that this sounds like a depreciatory reference to my proposal, for, if " L.0.V." has sufficient knowledge of it to pronounce it unscientific, he must know that its object is not merely to deal with unemployment, but to effect a radical alteration of the indus- trial system itself by which an equitable distribution of wealth is attainable and the evils (of which unemployment is only one) of the present system may be prevented. Now, it seems not a little strange that one who professes to regard " common ownership of the source of wealth production "as natural and scientific can see nothing scientific in the common ownership of part of the source of wealth production. Yet this is substantially what my pro- posal would establish. In the case of the common ownership of all the source of wealth, the insurmount- able difficulty in its application (even if otherwise sound) is the readjustment of the economic relation- ships of some 40 millions of people, a task too stupendous for any body of men to carry out at once, and, as we have seen, the belief that it is coming about by the gradual nationalisation of industry is falsified by the actual tendency to a state of greater servility. As I see the question, what really matters is this readjustment to enable each individual to obtain a fair share of the wealth created and that the parti- cular kind of ownership (collective, individual or partly both), is quite a secondary consideration and will shape itself according to circumstances. At any rate, 1 claim that my proposal will set in motion this fundamental change, which is, in reality, the central idea of Socialist theory, but of which the methcd of practical application, is, as far as I am aware, still unknown to any section of Socialiste. I ask "LOX." to point out in what respects this part of my proposal is unscientific. In the second place, the proposal seeks to obtain the ultimate control, for the whole of industry, of the economic forces of " Demand " and "Supply. This is necessary to secure the readjustment of economic relationships already mentioned. These forces would also have to be controlled under Socialism if the whole industrial system were under State management, and until this control is obtained State ownership is impossible. Will "LOX." say if the method of securing Government control of " Demand " and " Supply" is unscientific, and if so, in what way? The proposal also claims that by the automatic increase of the Minimum Wage (to say 4:,3 or ;CI per week), set up under the scheme, the fluctuations in trade, resulting in unemployment, would be made impossible, because there would not a large reservoir of cheap labour at hand and industry would therefore be run with great regularity. The state industry would thus act as a permanent counterpoise to</p>
        <p>limmamail</p>
        <p>industry as a whole. Wdl " L.O.V. "say that this ler an unscientific principle? It is impossible to treat the whole scheme in the course of a letter, but if there is any other part of it which appears to " unscientific I should like to have his criticism.</p>
        <p>No. I.—" That the Liberal Party cannot solve this poverty problem. I quite agree in the sense that it has no proposal generally acceptable to the party and recognised by the leaders as one ranking for legisla- tive action. But this might have been said with regard to Old Age Pensions not many years ago. On the authority of Sir Charles Booth the idea of Old Age Pensions originated in a pamphlet published in 1879 by Mr. R. P. Honitham, of Islip, Oxfordshire, who had it in mind some twenty or thirty years before publishing it. Such is the aversion to place before the public innovating ideas, however reasonable, that are likely to be regarded and treated as strange departures from what is accepted as ortho- dox. It took about thirty years more to so permeate political thought as to secure legislative action by the Liberal party. From this " L 0.V." should, I think, see that waiting for a spontaneous demand from the masses is not the effective method of reform, nor yet the sending of men to Parliament "who will stedfastly work for the emancipation of the worker," for they may all do this quite conscientiously but in conflicting directions. It is the measure that is of importance, not the man. I suggest to " L.0.V." that if be finds what be believes to be a solution of the problem of poverty, it is his duty to push it, if possible, into the political arena, regardless of the party that may eventually zarry it through Parliament. 14th May, 1913.</p>
        <p>JOHN A. J. MORTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0047" n="art0047">
