        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 17, 1913.</p>
        <p>SCHOOLBOY RUGBY CHAMPIONS.</p>
        <p>ST. JAMES' UNBEATEN RECORD FOR TWO SEASONS.</p>
        <p>THE INVINCIBLE TEAM. SIX HOSTILE POINTS IN TWO YEARS,</p>
        <p>First Row : —Mr. E. Yates, Mr. A. H. Cppe, Mr. C. George, Mr. W. H. Stansfield, Mr. E. J. Mee, Mr. A. Greenhough. Second Row :—A. Harrison, A. Armitage, S. Buckley, R. Bibby, A. Rigby. Third Bow :—H. Smith, W. Foulkes, A. Lawton, G. Duubobbin, F. Plumb. Seated in Front :—L. Lockhart, W. Skelhorn, J. Munn, H. Newall.</p>
        <p>The School Rugby Championship for this season was finally decided on April 26th, when St. James' met Silver-street, additional interest attaching to the game becau,s of the fact that whichever won would head the League. A keen struggle up to half-time left the se, re board blank. The second half was as evenly contested and only a dropped goal separated the two teams at the finish. It was about three- quarter time when Skelhorn, who had been giving a good show, received the ball from the pack, went round bis opponents' blind side and calmly dropped at goal. The ball just lobbed over the bar. This reverse seemed to put new life into th,, Silver-street boys, who, until the final whistle sounded, gave the Latchford team a hot time, but tke defence prevailed. St. James', the winners, were best represented in this vitally important match by A. Lawton, Skelhorn, Buckley, Righy and Smith, and the losers by Mellor, Parr and the Lawtons. _</p>
        <p>- This is the second year in succession that St. James' have been successful in carrying off the Schoolboy Rugby Championship of Warrington. At the end of the season f9lO-1911 the St. James' boys reached the final of a knock-out competition, but were defeated by Christ Church by 6 points to 3. Since this date they have been undefeated and have only had two tries scored against them in league matches—one in each season. During these two</p>
        <p>Photograph by Burrell.</p>
        <p>years the competition bag taken the form of a league: each club playing one match with the other clubs in the league. The matches aie played at Wilderspool, and uniforms are provided for each club by the Warrington R.F.C., who also provide medals for the winners and runners-up. The follov.ing is this season's record of the St. James' team:</p>
        <p>St. James' 14 St. Mary's 33 Evelyn-street 26 Christ Church 0 2 Silver-street 0 6 Rest of League ... 6</p>
        <p>Only two (Lawton and Rigby) were —members of last season's team, but the latter was in the previous year's team, and is the possessor of three Rugby medals. The Latchford boys take to Rugby as natur- ally as a chick takes to water. Most of the boys live near to Wilderspool, and are often interested spectators of senior matches from which they learn various points of the game. The success of the team is due to the keenness and interest of the boys in the game. It is interesting to note that the following St. James' boys have played for the Warrington Schoolboy team : Lawton, Rigby, Skelhorn, Buckley, Armitage and Ne</p>
