      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0048" n="art0048">
        <p>naturally no understanding could possibly exist. Further, an infusion of the new idea would be of great advantage,las, by a policy of that kind they might train on, possibly evolving into a decent team. It certainly goes without saying that the premier team requires something more promising than at present exists. Matches played, 33; won, 16; lost, 17. G. DICKENSON'S BENEFIT.</p>
        <p>Your committee have decided to play a match during the month of October for the above-named player's benefit, when it is to be hoped that all members will assist to make it a bumper. George deserves well of their support, for he has been a good, sound payer, and one of the most courteous and loyal playing members.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0049" n="art0049">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION REFORMS.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION REFORMS.</p>
        <p>A new rule was passed at the Annual Meeting of the parent body in July last, making it compulsory for club members to , pay stand and ground charges at all Chal- lenge Cup-ties. Your representative was instructed to vote against the resolution. Your committee also complained of the unfairness of allocating the Cup-tie Final to Leeds again; seeing that Oldham and Dews- bury played there last season ; they felt that on this occasion it should be played in Lancashire. They intend to send a resolution suggesting that the venue should alternate when a Lancashire and a Yorkshire club are competing. A very debatable question will also engage the attention of the legislators of the Northerr Union at the forthcoming Annual Meeting with regard to the residential qualification of Colonials for two years before they can take part in our games. Your committee are much in sympathy with the resolution, but, personally I am strongly opposed to it, feeling that we must have no restrictions on our possibilities for securing the quality of player to improve our standard of play. NORTHERN UNION RETROSPECT.</p>
        <p>Generally speaking, Northern Unionism has had, in spite of unfavourable weather, a fairly successful year. The disbandment of Ebbw Vale and the Coventry Club is primarily due to their isolated surroundings, and owing to such conditions, compelled to travel North every alternate week, a matter of costly expense. Had a sufficient number of clubs existed in their particular district, similar to West Lancashire, there can be no two questions but that they would be still in our ranks. The opening of our code in Devonshire is not calculated to make any headway unless they can secure sufficient number of clubs to form a League. They would then by such means create local inter- est, and at the same time have less travelling expense. Several clubs have no doubt had a bad time financially, but their enthusiasm is unshaken, with prospects of an improvement in the near future. The hon. membership of the League is on the increase, and the number of clubs now constitutes a record. In Cup- tie club games a larger amount than ever was taken—Xl,2oo was the gate Wigan v. Huddersfield and there was also a record gate for the final of the League between the same clubs. The class of football has improved with most teams, and with the Juniors, also, a very pleasing feature to all concerned.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0050" n="art0050">
        <head>CUP FINAL.</head>
        <p>CUP FINAL.</p>
        <p>The greatest of all our achievements of this season, or of recent years, was our meritorious display in the final at Leeds. Common language from the tutored mind, as well as the man in the street, proclaimed the convic- tion that the result was merely a question of bow great a score our opponents could win by; many remarking what a pity it was such poor class was opposing Huddersfield. The gate, they said, would severely suffer in consequence. In spite of all the pessimistic feeling the attendance was a most gratifying one, considering the atmospheric conditions, a nasty drizzle falling all the morning ; thus our hopes of beating the record gate were blighted Under such unfavourable auspices, £1,445 was taken, the fifth largest gate ever taken. Over 4,000 supporters made the journey from Warrington, engaging 13 specials and 76 saloons, so that was a con- vincing proof of the loyalty of our patrons and friends. Whatever reflections existed as to our chances of success immediatelylpreceding the contest, one pleasing feature manifested</p>
        <p>itself with the players in the form of a quiet feeling of confidence, a spirit endowed with grim determination to uphold the credit ot the good old town they had the honour to represent and to maintain the traditions of a glorious past. , "All hope abandon, ye who enter here" seemed to be the cry in many minds, but this expression was merely the breath of the cynics, as the result proved bow unfounded their ideas were. Playing robust and refresh- ing football, our lads played the game of all times, causing feelings of consternation and alarm to arise in the ranks of their opponents, smashing up their combination by the vigour of their tackling and resolute forward play that visions of defeat for Huddersfield loomed ahead. However, speed as well as weight told its tale eventually, but we had the proud satisfaction of making them extend them- selves to the utmost limit of their capabilities. Adulations were showered on our team for their gallant display, the Press and public generally being very effusive in their con- gratulations, contending that such a clean manly fight had done more for Northern Unionism than could possibly be realized.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0051" n="art0051">
        <head>FINANCIAL RESULTS.</head>
        <p>THE FINANCIAL RESULTS.</p>
        <p>Satisfactory Though .Sadly Affected by Bad Weather.</p>
        <p>The Hon. Treasurer, Mr. T. Pemberton reports :—I am pleased to report that the season just ended has proved very satisfactory financially. A profit of £206 12s. 9d. has been made after allowing the sum of £96 3s. for depreciation.</p>
        <p>The membership consists of 22 life, 124 honorary and 1,055 ordinary members. The subscriptions are £45 18s. less than 1911-12. The net gate money amounts to £2,269 18s. Bd., against £2,121 18s. 9d. last season. Owing to bad weather, the receipts in the St. Helens, Wakefield, Coventry and Barrow matches were exceedingly small, the total of the four realising only £B2 10s. 9d. In addition to this, extra costs were entailed by postpone- ments. The Sports held last August were not a success. The public appear to find other sources of amusement on this popular holiday. The concessions received from the Railway Companies have made a very welcome addition to the finances. Consider- able sums have been expended in the efforts to find new blood for the team, which, in one or two instances, have been successful. There is a falling off in the attendance at the Works Matches during the last two seasons. This is not encouraging, as it should be remembered that the Committee have a double object in undertaking this work—the unearthing of local talent and the rendering of assistance to charitable institutions in the town.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0052" n="art0052">
        <head>NORTHERN UNION AFFAIRS.</head>
        <p>NORTHERN UNION AFFAIRS.</p>
        <p>Extra Medals for Cup Finalists.</p>
        <p>At the Grosvenor Hotel, Manchester, on Wednesday evening, Mr. W. D. Lyon (Hull) presided over a meet- ing of the Northern Rugby Union General Committee. The Huddersfield and Warrington clubs made application to present extra medals to players who bad taken part in cup-ties, and the committee granted the applications. Mr. Nickaon, the secretary of the Cumberland Commission, reported that he would be unable to continue his connection with the game owing to business. His resignation was accepted with regret, but it was unanimously decided to ask him to retain his seat on the commission. Mr. E Brown was appo.nted as secretary of the commission. The question was asked whether a league club would be barred under the Colonial standing order from accepting the services of a Colonial player at the beginninglof next season with whom they had arranged prior to the passing of the standing order. It was decided that if proof could be produced that such arrangements bad been made the registration would be allowed. The Warrington Club wrote the committee regard- ing the final tie of the challenge cup, and suggested that the game be played alternately in Lancashire and Yorkshire. The applications for further extension of the season was refused. T. Moorhouse (Huddersfield), D. Clark (Huddersfield), A. Jenkinson (Hunslet), T. Woods (R ochdale Hornets), F. Harrison (Leeds), and Evan Davies (Oldham) were granted international caps.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0053" n="art0053">
        <head>FREE SECONDARY SCHOOLS.</head>
        <p>FREE SECONDARY SCHOOLS.</p>
        <p>At the annual Conference of the National Association of Head Teachers at Bournemouth, the President (Mr. J. W. Jacob, of heeds), in his opening address, advo- cated the co-ordination of primary and secondary education, and urged that to accomplish this secondary schools must be free.</p>
        <p>Resolutions were adopted in favour of reducing the size of classes in the primary schools, calling upon the Board of Education to adopt means for approximating the supply of teachers to the requirements of the com- munity, and raising the status of the teacher.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0054" n="art0054">
        <head>Irttrrs to fly (fhitin</head>
        <p>Irttrrs to fly (fhitin</p>
        <p>NE EDITOR of the " Examiner " invites correspond- ence on all subjects of NM?, interest. LETTERS should be brief and pointed. Their object must be to impart or seek information, not abuse. COMMUNICATIONS received early in the week—except urgent matters—can always be given the best attention.</p>
        <p>VIVISECTIONS AND ANTI-VIVISECTIONS. Sir,—Lawson Tait, whom your "Old Journalist " quotes in your last issue in favour of anti-vivisection, was certainly a surgeon of distinction, but he also said that vivisection had benefitted bacteriology. Both sides may claim him, if they like, as his opinions were wayward and inconsistent. Dr. George Wilson is a lesser light and may be placed alongside any of the individuals who form the small medical group opposed to vivisection. I hope your "Old Journalist" will note that our worthy Marr is not to be included in this group, for be is not in favour of the abolition of vivisection, but is only opposed to the repetition of experiments especially those for teaching purposes. As this only affects the traininQ of medical students and not the march of science it is relatively a small matter.</p>
        <p>J. MANSON.</p>
        <p>8, Winmarleigh Street.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0055" n="art0055">
        <head>CHRISTIAN UNITY.</head>
        <p>CHRISTIAN UNITY.</p>
        <p>Sir,--I am sure many thoughtful perple will sympathise with the desire for more unity among Christians expressed by Rev. H. Ogden in his sermon at Golborne-street Chapel on the 27th ult. For my part, I cannot see why there should not be a closing of the ranks among the Free and Anglican Churches to uphold Evangelical truth against superstition and error. An alliance between Evangelicalism and Romanism is an impossibility. Those sacerdotalists in the Anglican Church, who aspire at leading their church Romewards are only labouring to their own undoing and providing the Roman heresy with converts. Thinking men and women must feel a degree of shame, at the present moment, when China is awakening to find that they have nothing to set before them but a divided Christendom ; but they will also, in a measure, be gratified to find the Chinese desire their new religion to be of a Protestant com- plexion. The days are past when we should take our religion and its dogmas from the infallible old man on the banks of the Tiber, or seek redemption in the polytheism of Rome. We go straight to the Author of all good, and rely upon Him for succour, and have an infinite faith in the redemption wrought by Jesus Christ, trusting in the Divine Creator and faithfully believing in the Atonement of Christ, accept- ing the Bible as the Word of God and infallible guide and rule of faith. We ought to find this sufficient common ground to coalesce for the good of each other. PHIL SQUOD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0056" n="art0056">
        <head>DR. MANSON AND CHRISTIAN SCIENCE.</head>
        <p>DR. MANSON AND CHRISTIAN SCIENCE.</p>
        <p>Sir,— Dr. Manson, like all critics of Christian Science, has discovered that Christian Science existed before Mrs. Eddy's time. Unfortunately for these critics, they all decide upon different people as the original discoverer. We have had scores of these so- called discoverers brought forward. However, Mrs. Eddy is the only one, so far, who has proved by practical results that she was the discoverer. Dr. Manson does not evidently like the term Christian Science. I could understand a boy of ten quarrelling with the name of something, but how a thoughtful man can allow himself to drop down to the position where he abuses a science because he does not like its name, puzzles me. If Dr. Manson would give a little time to finding out what some of the results of Christian Science are, he would probably cease judging a subject of which he manifestly knows nothing. Christian Scientists, how- ever, are always glad to have Mrs. Eddy's name coupled with Christian Science, and, in fact, it will always be so, for Christian Science was but the natural result of her consecrated and loving life. Dr. Manson's reply is quite weak enough to topple over without any help front me, as it only consists of several arbitrary statememts, which have no weight. Meanwhile, Christian Science continues its loving, Christ-like mission of lifting the burden of humanity, and is being welcomed, understood, and practised by an ever-increasing multitude. Facts are bard things to disprove, and baseless, didactic statements can never disprove them. All credit to Dr. Manson for the good he is doing. in his way, but he would he a better and wiser man if he left his neighbour's religion alone.</p>
        <p>JOHN W. DOORLY. 54, Corporation-street, Manchester..</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0057" n="art0057">
        <head>ARE CO-OPERATIVE COMMITTEES DEMOCRATIC?</head>
        <p>ARE CO-OPERATIVE COMMITTEES DEMOCRATIC?</p>
        <p>Sir,—l have every sympathy with the aims and ideals of your correspondents, as revealed in the correspondence re the Co-operative Committee's attitude towards questions of social reform and the poverty problem. With the capabilities of the various parties and isms to solve such problems, for the moment I am not concerned. My chief concern lies with the indifference 6f the majority of co-operators, when, by their votes they fail to elect as an executive, a democratic body of men to control such an organisa- tion as a co-operative society. I hardly think that anyone would suggest that the committee, as at present composed, forms a majority that is typical of the thoughts, aims and aspirations of the average work ingclass trading co-operative members. Nor can we look to them for a true interpre- tation of Cu-operation, using the word in the broadest sense.</p>
        <p>Until my fellow correspondents have instilled into the minds of their fellows the folly of their apathy, and substituted into the movement a spirit of broadmindedness, instead of the existing political bias, a desire for the upliftwent of mankind, in lieu of the craving for a rising dividend, and a true democratic committee and less party cranks—until such a spirit of re:orm, as I have ventured to suggest prevails the attainment of a prosperous contented commonwealth is very remote. I would like to add, that, as far as the per- sonal honour of the Cowmittee is concerned, I have no quarrel; but in face of recent events in the indus- trial upheaval and the attitude of several Boards of Management in the country, one may perhaps be forgiven for asking if the average Co-operative Com- mittee leflects the opinion of present-day democracy. JONADAB.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0058" n="art0058">
        <head>WARRINGTON AMATEUR SHAKESPEAREAN PLAYERS. A REPLY TO MR. A. PERRIS.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON AMATEUR SHAKESPEAREAN PLAYERS. A REPLY TO MR. A. PERRIS.</p>
        <p>Sir,—Although it may be a glaring indiscretion on my part through being a member of the company, I cannot "hide my diminished head " without replying to Mr. Alfred Perris's latest effusion of culling phrases from my notes, distorting same, also introduc- ing diversions, and not answering two of the questions I submitted to him, in order to bolster up his unten- able position. That I differ from him in his hypercritical attitude against the Society is very true, the suggestion that I impute dishonesty of purpose, siuieter motives, and a purposed evil, is an eroneous and gross perversion. Ido still maintain, with the utmost honesty of conviction, that he, as a devotee, with a professed desire to do honour to Shakespeare's writings and incidently to benefit local charities, does, through his unfair attitude, certainly attempt to impede the progress of a move- ment whose efforts (measured by any standard) are directed to attain that end. That Dr. Law's interpre- tation of Shylock, in the Tubal scene, did not follow upon accepted traditional lines, I agree, but that is no adequate reason why be should ventilate his pet theories, and at the same time depreciate the efforts of others.</p>
        <p>Although I have not begged the question, I know of no one who has suggested, or even hinted, that Mr. A. Perris " could act the part better." It is also interesting news to learn that, under the existing conditions of elaborate production, principals are merely measuring off so many lengths of figured verbiage. Furthermore, I ask Mr. Perris to name any stage prcduction of "The Merchant of Venice " where Act 1, scene 111, is omitted. I have witnessed at least eight different productions of the " The Merchant of Venice " and have seen that scene enacted on each occasion. He must confuse it with Act 2, scene IV. After this exchange of pleasantries, I admit I am quite in agreement with him on the idealistic way of production (if it were possible as a financial pro- position), that is simplicity of detail, costumes and scenery, and to depend solely upon the ability of the player to invest the character with that atmosphere</p>
        <p>necessary to bring out the light and shade, and tn give as near as possible the correct interpretation of the words implied in the great writings. Hamlet's advice to the players is a glorious example to follow, and if it were possible to produce without financial failure, by all means do so, on the Granville Barker or the German principle. lam of opinion, however, that he i asking too much from a small weak society to adopt methods which the greatest Shakespearean exponents of the present day do not, and will not, undertake, that is, simplicity of detail, which, even in the Granville Barker production, did not necessarily mean reduction of cost, judging from the criticisms of E. A. Baugham, which probably he has read. Mr. Perris must know that fortunes have been lost by attempting to produce, other than on spectacular lines, for until recent years Shakespearean plays enjoyed a very indifferent exist- ence. Loss upon loss ensued ; the word Shakespeare to theatre managers spelt ruin as a financial proposi- tion ; companies producing could be counted on the fingers of one hand. The advent of mountings, similar to Sir H. B. Tree's "Macbeth" and "The Merchant of Venice," Flanagan's " Cymbeline," and others of a like hind, revived flagging interest, and it is solely due to producing on these lines that the works are gradually increasing in Livour and gaining ground. Take another aspect of the view. Does simplicity of scenery mean velvet curtains? If so, would it not (to the ordinary man in the street) be drawing too much upon his imagination to expect him to appreciate and realize that a velvet curtain is a representation, say, of the battleground of the Plains of Philippi, or the shipwreck scene in "The Tempest?" Finally, I hope " my good friends " will interpret these few notes as a means to an end, as a method of causing a little more interest in a movement worthy of support, not dictated by any personal motive. W. ASHTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0059" n="art0059">
        <p>Cleanse the Liver.</p>
        <p>11111NOINI,</p>
        <p>End Constipation.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0060" n="art0060">
        <head>TOO MANY CLERKS. ---- Manual Training to Relieve the</head>
        <p>TOO MANY CLERKS. ---- Manual Training to Relieve the</p>
        <p>Pressure.</p>
        <p>PROPOSED INSPECTION OF OFFICES.</p>
        <p>A considerable amount of interest centred in the twenty-second annual conference of the National Union Of Clerks, held in Leicester this week. Nearly 150 delegates were present, and the busiuess meetings took place in the Secular Hall. The proceedings began on Saturday, with a visit to the Museum and Art Gallery, after which there was a reception and concert at the Secular Hall.</p>
        <p>Sunday's proceedings commenced with an open-air demonstration in the Market Place, when a huge crowd was present. Mr. F. Rowland Hill, president of the Leicester Branch of the Union, who was in the chair, put forward a powerful plea for all clerks to join the Union.</p>
        <p>Mr. Fred Hughes (Birmingham), vice-president of the Union, also spoke of tlie advantages of all clerks joining together. Mr. Linusley (Newcastle) delivered a telling speech, and Mr. Lawson (Hackney) also spoke. In the after— noon the Conference was formerly opened, the delegates being welcomed by Alderman G. Banton, J.P. and Mr. J. G. Austin, president of the Leicester Trades Council The president of the Union, Mr. F. Wroe, was in the chair, and in addition to those already mentioned he was supported by Mr. Scott (London), lion. assistant general secretary, Mr. George Robinson secretary of the Leicester branch, Mr. F. Rowland Hill, and others.</p>
        <p>CLERKS AS OBJECTS FOR COMMISERATION The PRESIDENT in his address, said that as far as count- ing of heads made for progress, it could be truly laid down, for the yesr just ended had been one of unparalleled success for their Uniov. Greater energy had been expended by the members than at any time previous, and the result was a great and gratifying increase in membership. It could not be admitted or claimed that they could, however, afford to rest on their laurels. Their numbers were woefully small, only about one clerk in 30 or 40 being as yet sufficiently far- sighted to perceive that the social salvation for clerkdom ultimately rested on its power to demand those reforms which it considered essential to a com- fortable existence. At present clerks were looked upon as objects for commiseration. Happily, there were not. wanting signs of improvement. The deducting of fourpence per week for insurance from wag, already for the most part insufficient to procure a decent standard of comfort, might be good legislation from the point of view of the employing class, but those responsible for the measure needed to have very adapt- able consciences. COMPULSORY INSPECTION OF OFFICES.</p>
        <p>There remained a question of compulsory inspection of offices. Large numbers of clerks were working under the most abominable conditions. It was for them to see that this necessary piece of legislation was passed as speedily as possible. There was no reason why offices should not be inspected periodically just as were factories and workshops. There was also the question of education. They needed to exert themselves in order to secure that children leaving school were guided into channels where their natural proclivities secured to them the best results. TO DAM THE STREAM OF POTENTIAL CLERKS..</p>
        <p>They must dim the stream of potential clerks con- stantly turned out on the labour market, thus intensify- ing an already difficult problem. Manual training for school children would almost immediately relieve the pressure on the cleric l market, and tend to raise wages. rhe increase in the number of women in their ranks complicated their difficulties enormously. The meagre wage accepted by the average female worker placed a. serious obstacle in the way of rapid improvement in the wages of males. They could only appeal to the women's sense of dignity and comradeship to demand a higher and nobler status than had hitherto been their lot.</p>
        <p>Mr. F. HUGHES (Birmingham) proposed a vote of thanks to the President for his address, and Mr. Nixes (Manchester) seconded. The Conference was resumed on Monday. The General Secretary reported that during the past 1 2 months the Union bad enrolled 4,828 new members, thus making a total membership of 10,100.</p>
