      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0066" n="art0066">
        <head>WILLIAM MONKS &amp;SON, Ltd.</head>
        <p>WILLIAM MONKS &amp;SON, Ltd.</p>
        <p>THE CREAMERY, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Wish to invite their friends and the people of the district to try the DELICIOUS CREAK</p>
        <p>EGGS, MILK &amp; BUTTER Which they are receiving daily from the Farmers in the neighbourhool of Warrington.</p>
        <p>Everything absolutely Fresh and perfectly clean.</p>
        <p>Opposite their Grocery, Provision, Wine and Spirit Establishmen</p>
        <p>THE EXAMINER.</p>
        <p>SATURDAY, MAY 17, 1913.</p>
        <p>A NOTABLE ANNIVERSARY.</p>
        <p>Thursday this week was a notable anniversary. Tariff "Reform," or so much as remains of it, celebrated its tenth birth- day. The period of ten years, we know, is but a short span in the history of a nation, but it is a convenient span to take to judge of the progress of any political movement. And what can we say of Tariff" Reform ? " What can its best friends say of it? What the electors said of it on the very first occasion upon which they had an opportunity of giving vent to their outraged feelings is matter of history—the friends of the strange and threatening policy ,went back to the House of Commons reduced to vanishing point. It was on May 15, 1903, that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN, having returned from the scene of the war in South Africa, unfolded his scheme for uniting the Empire on trading lines, the foundation of which was preferential treatment for the Colonies, which involved, of course, the setting up of taxes to cover the preference as against out- side nations. He shattered his party —so far as the leaders were con- cerned—and the electors fell upon the rank and file three years later. But Mr: CHAMBERLAIN captured the party machine, and even Mr. BALFOUR went down eventually before his well-laid plans and effective manceuvring. More money and more unscrupulous methods than were ever available for any other cause were placed at the disposal of these " reformers," and it was hoped that Time would Ideal gently with the innovators. The advent of the Liberal party found this country on the crest of industrial prosperity which has persisted almost continuously ever since in beating records as year has succeeded year—an argument which has finally decided that there shall be no interference with the fiscal system that has brought us so much prosperity and still keeps us in the van of the nations, even in times of unprecedented striving on the part of others to excel.</p>
        <p>How completely Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S designs have failed may be seen on examin- ing the foundations upon which he and his friends sought to build ,and on reflecting upon the entire absence of any superstructure up to the present time. Most political innova- tions have a temporary set back, but success if there is to be success,comes along, if slowly, perceptibly, Now, ten years after, what do we find? It will be remembered that Mr. CHAMBERLAIN said that "sugar has gone, silk has gone, iron is threatened, wool is threatened, cotton will go." Pathetically he asked how long we were going to stand it. Well, we have stood it for ten years and will probable continue to stand it, especially as so recently as last Christmas all our trades were in such a state of boom that even the Conservative newspapers had to come out into the open and confess that their country was really doing remarkably well. It will be remembered how, locally as well as generally, Tariff "Reformers "quoted our over-sea trade returns to show how we had—under a long period of Conservative rule—been going industrially to the dogs. Where are those quoters now? Where is Mr. BONAR LAW now, the gentleman who was called to wear MR. BALFOUR'S mantle because he was a Tariff "Reformer" after Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S own heart and Tariff" Reform " was the first plank in the Conservative programme? No one knows exactly where, Mr. BONAR LAW stands; but we have a vague idea that food taxes are dead and buried, the food taxes which gave the one possible justification for Mr. CHAMBERLAIN'S scheme. Without prefer- ence for the Colonies Tariff "Reform ," becomes sheer Protection; without food taxes that Protection would rob the farmer of the ,chance of buying cheaply while denying him the privilege of protection for his own products. It should, moreover, be remembered that originally the principal claims o! the Protectionist party were in the interests of the farmer. The Conservative party are in a maze,and the position of their candidate in a partly industrial, partly rural division, like the Altrincham division, is most unenviable. Here we have a Unionist candidate reflecting the hapless state of the party to which he belongs by saying that he is a Tariff Reformer but not a Protectionist, and later that he does not come "into this</p>
        <p>ARROL-JORNSTON, BAYARD. PALLADIUM and TALBOT CARS.—SoIe Aiont, J. S. GARLAND, 51, 53, and 55 Bridge at., Warrington. See the 8 H.P. Bayard, £197.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0067" n="art0067">
        <head>Passing Week.</head>
        <p>The Passing Week.</p>
        <p>THE PARISH CHURCH CLOCK.</p>
        <p>The Parish Church Clock has been on strike this week and for quite three.days refused to strike. This is somewhat of a paradox but the different meanings attach- ing to the same word explain that matter. We have not been able to ascertain whether our friend struck for shorter hours or more oil; perhaps it was for both. However, in Church-street it was twenty to eight all Monday and a quarter to nine all Tuesday and Wednesday, somewhat slow progress when so much was involved, but by Thursday the lifficulty had been adjusted with the exception that our friend was just a trifle fast. Not to be able to know how Time is going, as it is measured out by the Parish Church clock, is an infliction that can only be understood by those who have temporarily suffered in this way.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0068" n="art0068">
        <head>ALEXANDRA DAY.</head>
        <p>ALEXANDRA DAY.</p>
        <p>Alexardra Day will be celebrated in June in 250 cities and towns of the United Kingdom, largely by means of a great floral festival which will be made to serve a double useful purpose. Millions of roses are to be made for the occasion and will give beneficial employment for months to a large number of cripples. Then the flowers will be sold by an army of voluntary workers and the pro- ceeds will go to the funds of hospitals in the work of which Queen Alexandra has always been keenly interested. In Warrington the celebration will take place on Saturday, June 28th, and the arrangements are in charge of a committee that meets next Thursday. The proceeds of the sale of roses will go to the Infirmary so that there are special reasons why the day should be made a success. It is interesting to note that to-morrow will be observed in some parts of this country as Mothers' Day, an insititution that has long flourished in America and Australia. A white flower is worn in memory of one's mother and the day is made sacred in the thoughts of elders to the person whose name, in the telling language of Thackeray, is the name for God in the hearts and on the tongues of little children.</p>
        <p>WHEN WILL THE ELECTION TAKE PLACE?</p>
        <p>The Speaker of the House of Commons, Mr. Lowther, has been giving a good hint as to when we may expect the next General Election. 'Writing to the Conservative Association of Mid-Cumberland, he says he hopes to "go on" representing them to the end of this Parliament which, he guesses, will run "to the autumn of 1914 or to the Spring of 1915."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0069" n="art0069">
        <head>BOWLS FOR LADIES.</head>
        <p>BOWLS FOR LADIES.</p>
        <p>The suggestion which found great favour at the last meeting of the Town Council that one of the municipal bowling greens should be set apart for lady devotees of the game one afternoon each week, has already been acted upon. The Town Hall, Park and Baths Committee have, however, gone a step further and decided to reserve a green for the ladies two afternoons weekly. The green selected is in Victoria Park and the ladies will be allowed exclusive possession of it between the hours of 2 and 5 p.m. every Tuesday and Thursday afternoon. There seems to be every reason to believe that the concession will be greatly appreciated and the only difficulty we can foresee is that the lady bowlers who live a considerable dis- tance from Victoria Park will be inclined to claim the privilege of playing on a green nearer their own homes. The extent to which the experiment succeeds will, how- ever, be a good indication of the wisdom or otherwise of embarking upon further innovations in this direction.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0070" n="art0070">
        <head>WHAT'S IN A NAME?</head>
        <p>WHAT'S IN A NAME?</p>
        <p>It was certainly in accordance with the fitness of things that two men who were summoned at the Borough Police Court, on Thursday, for furiously riding a pony, should be named Speed and Gallop respectively.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0071" n="art0071">
        <head>NOT TWO-FACED.</head>
        <p>NOT TWO-FACED.</p>
        <p>The morning " business " train was approaching Victoria Station on one of the earlier days of the Marconi inquiry, when accusations and recriminations were flying about freely. The ugly, sour-faced man of the compartment had expressed his views rather offensively, and had betaken himself to his newspaper. Looking up, he said with evident satisfaction, "Another o' them Liberals gone wrong. Ah'm noan surprised. I fnelr nowt on 'em—a two-faced lot i' my opinion." Then, turning to a mild man at his side, "Y ow're a Liberal, a'nt yo' ? " "Yes." "Well, I mek nowt on 'em—a two- faced lot." Stung into retort, the other said, " Yow're not a Liberal, then ? " "No 1" with intense scorn. "Then yow're not two-faced ? " " No ! " " Ah thowt not—else tha'd a left that one at home ! "—" Manchester Guardian" Miscellany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0072" n="art0072">
        <head>PRAYER AT EVEN.</head>
        <p>PRAYER AT EVEN.</p>
        <p>Night closes on the city's troubled street, Strange lights gleam forth and eerie voices call; A sound ensues of many restless feet, And pallid faces glimmer through the pall. 0 Thou who watcheth from the skies, Soothe our pain and fear, remit our sin; Give ear unto the groans that rise, From out the dust of battle, and the din. F. C.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0073" n="art0073">
        <head>MIMI</head>
        <gap reason="ocrQuality" quantity="2" unit="words">
