      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0074" n="art0074">
        <head>Examiner Saturday, May 17, 1913. DEMAND FOR LIVING WAGE.' TALK IN THE TOWN</head>
        <p>The Examiner Saturday, May 17, 1913. DEMAND FOR LIVING WAGE.' TALK IN THE TOWN</p>
        <p>SOME FORGOTTEN POINTS. [To THE EDITOR OF THE " WARRINGTON EXAMINER. 7 Sir,—Your able article on the "Living Wage" in the "Examiner" will be use- ful for its own sake. But its value will be vastly increased if it opens the way to discus- sion, to study its reflection. Certain it is that the problem while it can well be ventilated in the columns of a newspaper cannot there be settled. Ido not propose to do more than raise one or two dependent issues, if only to show that the problem is complex, and that' no way out can be discovered except by much hard thinking. The living wage lies at the root of many other social problems. The housing difficulty cannot be solved until the worst paid labourer can afford to pay for decent housing. To cut down the standard of housing and to relax well-considered by-laws is no solution. Yet lwe find municipal politicians ready to deal gently with slum lords in the supposed interests of the very poor, or to let houses at less than an economic rent, which, in effect, is a form of subsidisinc, cheap labour. Another point often forgotten is that there is always a class below that of the wage- earners. Low wages increase this class. Gutter-trading and hawking and pitiful rag and rubbish dealing always flourish where the wage earners are badly paid. And when the ranks of the hawkers are swelled, com- petition becomes cut-throat in its keenness. Then pauperism increases and the rate- payer is called upon to pay heavily for the wreckage ultimately resulting from low wages. It is impossible to escape from the vicious circle. Society finally pays for all injustices. The principle of the living wage ought not to be treated as a party political question, though doubtless any legislative scheme for its application will sooner or later divide political parties. But in the meantime the next step would appear to be to study the whole problem without preconceptions or prejudices. No method could be better than that of the study circle. There is no reason why a dozen small circles should not be started next autumn if two or three sensible people would take the trouble to organise such a plan ?tow. If 70 men would trouble to think now, 70,000 might act not long hence. Whatever happens, let me thank you for the new note of deep concern for social welfare that has been struck by the "Examiner."—Yours, &amp;c.,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0075" n="art0075">
        <head>SEEKER.</head>
        <p>SEEKER.</p>
        <p>HOW TO REGENERATE SOCIETY. TO THE EDITOR OF TIIE " WARRINGTON EXAMINER."</p>
        <p>Sir,—Kindly permit me to say a word in line with the demand for a " Living Wage." lam heartily in sympathy with your healthy endeavour to bring about an exchange of views on such an interesting problem, and your quotation of Mr. Gladstone in 1863 when, referring to the Church, he states "to extricate her in some degree from entangled relations, without shock or violence."</p>
        <p>I take it that yon mean we should endeavour to extricate our poverty from existing conditions without shock or violence. I have before me a pamphlet written by John Morton (Warrington), "A National Minimum Wage and the Organisation of Industry." It is an endeavour to show wherein daft State can organise and control industry so that those living below the State National Minimum may be assured of employ- ment; and a." Living Wage." I should, however, like to ask Mr. Morton wherein his schems differs in principle from the communistic ideas that have been tried in the past and failed ? Or where it differs in the long run from final State ownership of all the means of production and life? Speaking in Cardiff, December, 1911, we have the following from. the Right Honourable D. Lloyd George' M.P., Chancellor of the Exchequer—Exchequer—("South Wales News" December 30th, 1911) :—" What are the facts? Let us take the main facts. The first fact is this - that poverty is not the fault of Providence. Providence has provided an abundance. This is the richest empire under the sun That is the first fact we have to get into our minds. Then if there is poverty, misery, and wretchedness in this land it is not because the land is sterile and poor, and does not provide enough for all. What is the next fact? There are millions of men and woman in this country who through no fault of their own are suffering. There are millions of men' women and children in tbis rich land who go through life sodden in poverty, wretchedness and despair, maybe through low wages and high rents, maybe a breakdown in health, maybe a chronic condition of under-health, maybe the fluctuations of trade. What is my other proposition? That there are multitudes who live a life of wretchedness, misery and despair, partly through their own fault, and largely through their surroundings." Mr. Lloyd George then proceeds :—" Well now, I come to my other position. You cannot deal with a problem like this by mere spasmodic appeals to the charity of the benevolent. It is hopelessly inadequate as a remedy. You might as well try to run the Arnay and Navy by voluntary subscriptions. I have a very much more effective method than that—not as popular, I admit - still I get enough. If you depended on appeals to charity you could not maintsin a navy of Chinese junks." With this proposition I am sure we can all be in accord, for it is self-evident that the giving of a shilling here and there, and the distriLu- tion of funds raised by Church suppers, and the activity of municipal soup kitchens, are inadequate if not worse.</p>
        <p>He further states :—" The national income is 3:1,800,000,000. That is the revealed income. Very well, what does that mean? That means .-C2OO a year for every family in the United Kingdom. One third of that income is received and spent by 250,000 people—one fortieth of the people receive and spend one-third of the income." This is pointed out in order to show that if there is poverty, penury and oppression in the land it is not because there is to an abundance ; secondly, that it is incumbent upon thcse who have been more blessed by Providence to be prepared to make greater sacrifices. This to be effective must be done in an organised way, and not by any spasmodic individual acts of so-called charity. .This cannot be done among any section or class of people, who are not prepared to accept the method of carrying it out. Only a people working under the Golden Rule, in fact, and not in theory can do it, among whom these differences in material wealth will be the result of natural and just causes (such as mis- fortune or disability on the one hand, and vigour of body and mind, and executive or manual skill on the other) ; and not the result of graft, monopoly, or oppression. It will never be to the credit or good of any nation for certain ones to obtain immense wealth by fraud or otherwise, and then, voluntarily or under coercion, bestow a surplus on the poor. So in this particular regard I will differ from Mr. Lloyd George, who holds that it is the duty of the Churches to point out the social evils and then to uphold the Statesmen of the realm while they put into loperation corrective schemes, such as Old Age Pen- sions and Employers' Liability Acts, &amp;c., which are supposed to remedy, if not 'cure, the indicated evils. I am heart and soul in sympathy with all such correc- tive methods as suggested by social reformers, as by experience shall be discovered to alleviate suffering, and make more tolerable the lives of the aged, the sick, and the poverty stricken. But these things do not go far enough,and the remedy lies in human hearts, and unless man is prepared to accept the only terms that can be offered for the solution of the problem, it must ever remain a truth, "The poor ye have always with you." There have been many schemes advocated, and there are many schemes advocated now, for the abolition of poverty, war, and crime, and the bring- ing in of what might be termed the millennium, but these are in effect the efforts to save men, without men accepting the conditions wherein a society might be renewed and regenerated, and these terms have supposedly been tried for nearly two thousand years. My reply is if they have, theh the result is a drastic failure, and no greater failure exists in evidence to- day than the failure of the Churches to propound a successful remedy to the existing evils of society. Legislation in the past has proved over and over again its inability to cope with the evil. The demand for a living wage, I admit, is right, but the greatest demand- is for a living society composed of living individuals, and not dead ones, and the terms are equa: for all. "He that believeth the Gospel shall be saved," morally, socially, physically, and economically. WM. H. CHANDLER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0076" n="art0076">
        <head>How Public Money is Raised and Spent —and Other Subjects.</head>
        <p>How Public Money is Raised and Spent —and Other Subjects.</p>
        <p>Much earnest and animated talk has gone on during the week bearing on the questions of Local Taxation, and especially condemning the inequalities in its incidence, as between wealth and comparative poverty. The talk has generally led to strong censure being pronounced on the Imperial Exchequer for non-pay- ment of services, which, though national, are adminis- tered locally—as in the twitter of education—thus causing an ever-increasing burden to be laid on the householder of the poor class, who is compelled to pay in an equal ratio with the merchant, Anauufac- turer or professional man. The talk was, that this should be remedied ; that workers of all trades and grades should give their votes only to those who, desirous of representing them, will engage to press forward this reform, both on our local committees and in the House of Commons._</p>
        <p>The talk has also been largely -concerned with the remissness of our Boards of Guardians, who, notwith- standing the relief that old age pensions have given them, yet have made no corresponding reduction in the ates. The more outspoken have not hesitated to characterise the conduct of our local authorities as showing indifference to the needs of the working- classes, and have freely animadverted on the gre4t waste of time spent in discussing trifles relating to procedure, whilst important and even vital matters which affect seriously the well-being of the community are inordinately delayed or set aside entirely. Intelligent artizans, discussing this question, say it is absolutely necessary that a radical change should be made in the b tsis of local taxation, and an adjustment effected which shall be proportionate to the earnings of those who are called upon to pay rates. Meantime, they urge, our local badies should be pressed to petition Parliament to take up the duty which devolves on the Imperial Exchequer of defraying the entire cost of education—which is a national service—out of Imperial taxes. This, it is said, should be insisted upon as a measure of justice.</p>
        <p>Nurses as Ministering Angels. A topic that has given rise to much gratifying talk has been the work of the District Nurses' Association in the homes of the sick poor of the town. In all quarters of the town it is abundantly evident that its teal helpfulness is being better understood and more clearly appreciated by people, who, at one time, were inclined to look upon the "lady nurses" with a some- what supercilious condescension. But "doubt" has been "solved by action." These truly gentle women flitting about from house to house (within which " pain and anguish wring the brow" of many a suffering patient) have been "ministering angels" indeed. The talk is all in prase of them and their work, and should in due course be translated into practical aid for the Association, and assist materially the further spreading of its beneficent operations.</p>
        <p>Municipal Bowling Greens. In the Manchester-road district there has been a god deal of talk about the opening of the new muni- cipal bowling green at Fairfield. It has been said that the eagerness with which the residents have availed themselves of it affords ample justification for the policy which has been entered upon of providing additional facilities for this delightful pastime, and it has again and again been remarked upon by thoughtful observers, who have watched the bowling, that the benefit young men will derive from playing the game under such healthy conditions is enormously enhanced by the entire separation from all inducements to indulge in the use of alcoholic stimulants. The exhilar- ating influence of fresh pure air and the very natural and laudable desire to excel in play, it is contended, afford sufficient stimulus, and will give more enduring satisfaction.</p>
        <p>Mr. Warburton's Benefit. Amongst playgoers, the benefit of Mr. H. L. Warburton at the Royal Court Theatre, on the 22nd inst., is occasioning much interest, and the talk gener- ally has indicated that nothing less than a packed house is to be looked for—Bo universal is the good feeling expresspd for the obliging Acting Manager. The Pity of It. The Woman Suffrage cause has excited much eager diecussion, it being said by many, who have all along, favoured this extension of the franchise, that the sense- less conduct of the militants is not only degrading that section of the party, but bringing discredit on the cause generally, and postponing the time when a calm consideration of the case by Parliament, or the people, is possible. The habit of closing discussion by threats and warlike demonstrations has always had a strong hold on unregenerate mankind. And now it is made plain that womankind, also, is addicted to the same Habit. Surely, it can only be a passing phase of distempered action and distorted vision ; but—oh, the pity of it!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0077" n="art0077">
        <head>RESCUE AT SEA.</head>
        <p>RESCUE AT SEA.</p>
        <p>Warringtonians' Exciting Experience in the English Channel.</p>
        <p>ALDERMAN AND COUNCILLOR IN A GALE.</p>
        <p>Alderman D. Tinnion and Councillor Firth Lindley had an unusual and somewhat thrilling experience at sea last week, when they were passengers on a steamship which, at the height of a gale, was able to save a sailing ,vessel from almost certain destruc- tion. These Warringtonians had been visiting the Channel Islands and were return- ing to Weymouth on the Great Western mail boat " Reindeer, " which started from Jersey and made a call at Guernsey. Under normal conditions the passage is one of the most trying round the English coast, but on this occasion the sea was particularly rough, a North-west gale having suddenly sprung up. As the " Reindeer " was nearing the group of rocky islets called the Caskets, she overtook the sailing ship "Thesis," which had left Guernsey a few hours before, and was laden with stone for Poole in Dorset. The " Thesis " was flying signals of distress, her foremasts were damaged and her yards were down, and she was in grave danger of drifting on to the Caskets. The " Reindeer " promptly responded to the call for help, and, as it was seen that the sailing ship was abso- lutely helpless, there was no other course but to take her in charge and. tow her back towards port. To use the words of Alderman Tinnion, the sailing ship was "simply tossing about like a cork." Owing to the choppy sea, it was a difficult manceuvre to get alongside the drift- ing vessel, but the task was safely accomplished, A line had to be attached to the towing rope, but it was only after numerous attempts that it was at last secured. Three lengths of rope had to be fastened together and one seaman after another tried to throwitthe necessary distance When it did reach the " Thesis " the sailors were evidently so benumbed with cold that for some time they could not get a firm grip of the rope. Naturally considerable excitement prevailed on board the mail boat, which was carrying a full complement of passengers. The operations called for an exceptional degree of skilled seamanship, but they were admirably carried out, and the "Thesis" was towed for several miles towards Guernsey. Finally, she was given in charge of a steam tug, which had come out from Guernsey, and after three hours' interruption, the " Rein- deer" was able to resume her voyage. "It was a rather thrilling experience, " said Alderman Tinnion to an " Examiner " reporter. "The fact that the mail boat interrupted her voyage to go to the help of the sailing vessel showed that she was in imminent peril of being dashed on to the rocks—in fact we were told that, if succour had not been at hand, it would have meant certain destruction. The sea was one mass of white horses and, in consequence of this rescue incident,we had to repeat the roughest part of the passage."</p>
        <p>PRRAIrER MOTOR CYCLES.—SoIe Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge et., Warriegton.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0078" n="art0078">
        <head>forat sntriligture</head>
        <p>forat sntriligture</p>
        <p>Horse Parade.</p>
        <p>The thirteenth annual horse pirade will take place on Saturday next. May 24th, commencing at 2 o'clock from the Town Hall. Entries close to-day Saturday. For further particulars see advertisement. 1,0 O.F.M.A.</p>
        <p>The delegates representing the Warrington District of the Independent Order of Oddfellows, Manchest3r Unity, this Whit week at Scarborough animal movable conference are : —PG. A. E. Burdett (Constitution Lodge), and P.G. W. E. Ohre (Haven of Rest Lodge).</p>
        <p>Death of Mr. Joseph Unsworth, Many of our readers will regret to hear of the death of Mr. Joseph James Cusworth, of the Sankey Chapel House Inn, who passed acry on Wednesday, in his 58th year. The funeral took place at the Warrington Cemetery yesterday, the Vicar of Sankey (Rev. J. R. Jones), officiating.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0079" n="art0079">
        <head>Wycliffe Sale of Work.</head>
        <p>Wycliffe Sale of Work.</p>
        <p>A sale of work-in connection with Wycliffe Congre- gational Church will be held in the Memorial Buildings, Edgeworth-street, on Wednesday and Thursday, next, in aid, of the building fund of the new school chapel, Padgite-lane, and Wycliffe Church. Mrs. B. S. Townroe's Gift to a Church.</p>
        <p>Mrs. Townroe, wife of Mr. B. S. Townroe, prospec- tive Unionist candidate for the Middleton Division. presented a Whitsuntide gift to the Middleton Parish Church in the shape of a delicately embroidered mat for a fald stool. Mrs. Townroe has worked the mat herself.</p>
        <p>Bogus County Court Forms. At Northwich, on Wednesday, James Hollins, New- road, Latchford, was sentenced to one month's hard labour for obtaining Is. by false pretences by selling to a Budworth newsagent bogus county court forms, which prisoner asserted her husband had ordered. The forms cited sections which were repealed years ago. Prisoner had been convicted twelve times, including five times for false pretences. Death of Dr Willett.</p>
        <p>Dr. James Willett, of Stockton House, Stockton Heath, one of the best known medical men in North Cheshire, died suddenly yesterday morning in Ms eightieth year. He was a native of Great Budworth, near Northwich, where his father practised as a surgeon. He commenced his practice in Stretton in 1860, where he remained until he removed to Stockton Heath in 1879. In 1864 he married the daughter of the late Mr. Peter Edgerley, of Stockton Heath, who pre-deceased him 20 years ago.</p>
        <p>Local Artists at the Academy. Warringtonisns will be pleased to learn that the following pictures have been. hung at the Royal Academy :—Two portraits by Sir Luke Fildes, RA., " Mrs. Sorer Whitburn " (No. 264) and "Viscountess Downe " (No. 341) ; three Venetian paintings by Mr. Henry Woods, R.A., "The Chairtnenders " (No. 86), " A Venetian Fruit Shop" (No. 140), and "An Onion Seller" (No. 146) ; a water colour by Mr. Oswald Garside "Autumn "(No. 983). Mr. Oswald Garside also exhibits four water colours of English landscape in the Paris Salon.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0080" n="art0080">
        <head>Rev. W. Reid's Forthcoming Ordination.</head>
        <p>Rev. W. Reid's Forthcoming Ordination.</p>
        <p>The Rev. W. Reid of St. Joint's Presbyterian Church, Wilderspool Causeway, will be ordained and inducted early in June by the Manchester Presbytery in St. John's Church. A memorial was sent from the session and congregation at St John's to the Manchester Presbytery in October last, asking the Presbytery to take the necessary steps to obtain the Synod's sanction for the ordination and induction of Mr. Reid as minister at St. John's The session met at Liverpool last week and on Wednesday evening the Synod unanimously granted the request of the congregation of St. Jonn s. Music In the Park.</p>
        <p>By kind permission of Lieutenant-Colonel B. Fairclough and officers of the 4th P.W.V. (South Lancashire Regiment), the band will play the follow- ing programme in Bank Park to-morn w (Sunday), commencing at 3.15 p.m. (conductor, Mr. C. Wam- vvright) : —Hymn, " Nearer, my God, to Thee." Short;, march (sacred), " Haverhill," Ord Flume; selection, "On sacred melodies," Gounod : euphonium solo, "Asleep in the deep," Petrie ; "Gavotte, "The way to the heart," Lincke ; selection, "Mikado," Sullivan ; grand march, "Brigade Camp," Ord Hume. The band will again occupy the stand on Thursday evening at 7.15 Local Passengers In Motor Accident. A. serious motor accident occurred at West Timperley, on Thursday afternoon. A taxi-cab in which was Mr. James Patterson, of Latchford, Warrington, and a party, was going to the Manchester races. The car had passed West Timperle9 Station, i and a tramcar immediately n front of them going in the same direction pulled up. Mrs. Higson, of Wash- lane Cottages, West Timplrley, got off the tram, and evidently failed to notice the approaching motor. She was knocked down and severely injured. At the Altrincham Hospital she was found to be suffering from a compound fracture of the right leg and numerous scalp wounds.</p>
        <p>House Struck by Lightning. During the severe thunderstorm which raged at Warrington about one o'clock on Satulday afternoon, the house of Mr. C. Jackson Holmes, Latchford Without, was struck by lightning. A..chimney stack was dislodged, some of the tiles on the roof were removed, and a portion of the debris fell over the next house into an adjoining garden. The kitchen was also filled with soot smoke, and a servant had a very narrow escape. When Mrs. Holmes went into the kitchen she could just discern the servant, who complained of a pain in her leg, the current having apparently come down the kitchen chimney. The damage to the house is fully covered by insurance. Warrington Yacht Second in Long Distance Race.</p>
        <p>The Tranmere Sailing Club held a long distance race from Rock Ferry to Llandudno on. Saturday. At the start there was a fair breeze, but it was a "soldier's wind," horn the SSE.. However, during the passage it veered about in various directions and eventually fell to a flat calm, making the finish a protracted one. The yachts were passed abreast of Wallasey Church in the Rock Channel by the Sc. Elvies, outward bound, and again at 8.8. p.m. near the Constable Buoy. They all carried topsails, and the relative positions of each were several times changed during the run. The second position was secured by a Warrington yacht, the lone, belonging to Messrs. Thompson and Knowles. The times were : —l, Pearl (25min8.) 8 54 54 ; 2, lone (15mins.), 9 6 52; 3, Eurynome (7mins), 9 10 3; Jompar (scratch), 9 14 0; Darling (scratch), 9 17 L.</p>
        <p>THE POPULAR BPE"Nt°EgFraiCrliAbl9Rlr 3-rel</p>
        <p>Mr. H. L. Warhiirron. the genial acting niftl3-01,' the Royal Coon Theatre will be accorded gbie deserved benefit on Thursday evening next. purl° rtfl• three and a half years' connection with the thestrelf all Warburton has gained the goodwill and osteeto sections of the nntrons hy his uniform courteS9 tact. He is in fact an ideal theatre manager.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0081" n="art0081">
        <head>Band of Hope May Festival.</head>
        <p>Band of Hope May Festival.</p>
        <p>In connection with the recent May Festival promoted by the Band of Hope Union, the choir and other principal characters were provided with tea in Cairo- street schoolroom on Thursday evening. The arrangements were carried out by the Festival Com- mittee and lady workers of the festival, and their efforts were thoroughly appreciated by the 300 children who attended this annual event. After tea a short entertainment was presided over by Mr. Thomas Dale, chairman of Executive Committee, who congratulated the children on their efforts in the Festival. Mr. A. Spruce, musical conductor, also expressed his great satisfaction with the manner in which the various musical items were rendered, and thanked the boys and girls for their strict attention to rehearsals. A number of additional prizes for ticket- selling were given to rnembPrs of the choir. Songs and recitations were contributed by several of the boys and girls. New Manuscript of the Gospels: Mr. Mounfield's Promised Article.</p>
        <p>The " Times " published on Tuesday the first account of a new manuscript of the Gospels discovered in Egypt and purchased by Mr. C, L. Freer, by whose direction a facsimile has now been produced and a copy presented to the British Museum by the University of Michigan. The manuscript, now in Washington, dates either from the fourth or fifth century, and contains some important new material, particularly in the texts of St. Mark and St. Luke. This manuscript has aroused the keenest interest and is likely to be discussed throughout the civilized world. We are glad to be able to say that our con- tributor, Mr. Arthur Mounfield, has received permis- sion to inspect the pages of these newly-discovered Gospels, and will set forth his views in the " Examiner "of next Saturday. The decipherment and appraisement of a fresh manuscript fourteen or fifteen hundred veBrB old is, of course, a task for experts, but the subject has religious as well as antiquarian interests, and we think that many of our readers will look forward to an article by A.M. upon a subject which is likely to engage the attention of scholars in every Christian land.</p>
      </div>
