      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0091" n="art0091">
        <p>ing Classes Bill, 1912, which passed its second reading in the House of Commons without a division, was carried with some opposition. The PRESIDENT remarked it was a pity they could not have a united vote on a question of so much importance. mENTAL DEFICIENCY.</p>
        <p>The Mental Deficiency Bill, which the Government was urgently asked to pass without furtter delay, received strong support. Miss ALISON GA ILLAND said that many menial defec- tives were in ;nstitutions, but the yoult was that they were in the wrong institutionE. Not to restrict the feeble-minded mother was to commit a crime in the name of liberty</p>
        <p>PARLIAMENT AND MORAL QUESTIONS: SOON TIRED."</p>
        <p>Suggestions for strengthening the Criminal Law Atkenument Act, 1912, were as follows : That the age of consent be raised to at least 18 }ears, and 21 3 ears in ca,e of seduction by employer or guardian ; that it shall not be a valid defence to plead resisonahle cause to suppose a • girl to he older than she actually is ; that the time during which action can be taken aga nst the seducer be further extended ; that the feeble- minded shall, as such, he affor3ed punitive preventive protection against the assaults a d s-ductions of vicious person,. A Bill embodying those changes hid been dratted and would probably be introduced into the [-louse of Commons presently. We most go on v.ith the questions, otherwise whAt we did lust year would fall to the ground. Parliament very soon got tired of moral cp eat ions. Miss BER.THA MASON said although the Bill is now law their task was by no means finished. These engaged tu this nefarious traffic to-day are evolving new schemes for evading the law, and it wss probable in the I noire more than ever that the traflic would be in the hands of women. We shoul-1 not get rid of the evils tt sextml vice until we taught in the homes that purity is as much a viri ue for men as it is for women.</p>
        <p>—With the additional -recommenration that public solicitation by either sex should be repressed, the resolution was carried unanimously.</p>
        <p>SPEAKING AT A HIGH LEVEL The speaking throughout the Conference Was main- tained on a high level. The debates were conducted in a very efficient manner, and all the speeches were very mucts to the point and teemed with practic,al know- ledge. From 10 o'clock in the morning until sin the evening the delegates devoted themselves to the meet- ings. In the evenings they attended receptions at the invitation of the President and Executive Committee, Lady Cowdray and the HOD. Mrs. F. Guest. The Warrington delegates were also entertained by Miss Crosfield.</p>
        <p>ELECTIONS. At the conclusion of the business the SECRETARY announced the result of the ballot as follows :--Presi- dent, Rosalind, Countess of Carlisle ' • hon. secretaries, Lady Bamford-Slack, Mrs. Broadley Reid ; hon. treasurer, Mrs. Eva McLaren. Executive Committee: The Lady Aberconway, Mrs. F. D..Acland, Miss Mabel Billson, Mrs. Joseph Bliss, Mrs. Bonwick, Miss Margaret Bonney. Mrs. C. A. V. Conybeare, The Lady Cowdray, Miss Alison Garland, The Hon. Fred. Guest, The Ludy Dorothy Howard, Lady Howard, Mrs. Lloyd-George, Lady Lawson, Mrs. Thomas Lough, Miss Bertha Mason, Mrs. P. Heron Maxwell, Miss Lucy F. Morland, The Hon. Lady Norman, Mrs. Walter Rea, The Lady Cecilia Roberts. Miss Agnes E. Slack, Mrs. Handley Spicer, Mrs. E. Stewart-Brown, Mrs. Louise B. Swann, Lady Yoxall.</p>
        <p>EMMA. C. OSBORNE, • Hon. Secretary Warrington Women's Liberal Association.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0092" n="art0092">
        <head>MISS GROSFIELD AND THE SUFFRAGE BATTLE. The Choice Between Principle and Expediency. WOMEN'S INFLUENCE AT ELECTIONS.</head>
        <p>MISS GROSFIELD AND THE SUFFRAGE BATTLE. The Choice Between Principle and Expediency. WOMEN'S INFLUENCE AT ELECTIONS.</p>
        <p>I have been asked to give my impressions of the Women's Liberal Federation Meeting. Now that the rush is over, one can look back on the 1913 Council and realise that the outstanding feature was the five hours' battle over Suffrage on Tuesday, the tith May, the very day on which that same battle was being waged in the House of Commons. Briefly, Mrs. Eva McLaren, who presided in the two previous years in the absence of Lady Carlisle, bad put down a resolution in the name of Tunbridge Wells, binding ail Women's Littera' Associations belonging to the Women's Liberal Federation to pledge themselves to abstain from working for anti- suffrage Liberal candidates. Her contention was, not only that since we were all in favour of woman suffrage it was illogical to help to return to Parlia- ment a man who would oppose women's interests; but that "measures affecting women and children were the one special object singled out and bracketed with Liberalism in the constitution of the Women's Liberal Federation. Lady Carlisle and many followers on the other hand, though equally for the suffrage (they said) declared that such a resolution %scald be an infringe- ment of the personal liberty of Women's Liberal Associations, and that since many of them no doubt would resent the carrying of such a resoluti( n, they would leave the Women's Liberal Federation whose power, such as it is, would thus be broken. It was a nice impasse, to many of us it looked lire a choice between principle and expediency. This is the day of expediency. Though a perfect storm of cheers greeted Mrs. McLaren's : "I wish ladies you cared for suffrage as much as you care for me," she declared the vote on the resolution was adverse; there were, roughly, 390 for it and COO odd against. What motive animated these latter delegates, or rather, the committees which sent them forth? Personally, I expeet that timidity an subordination to the views of husband, fathers, brothers and so forth, largely held their baud. There have been striking examples cf the power which does lie in the hands of Women's Liberal Associations when they decide to use it. Cricklade and Cardiff must be names which will long linger in the minds of Lib2ral Whips. It was my privilege one morning to sit beside a Cardiff lady, Mrs. Lester Jones, , who must have been a prime mover in the Liberal ' ladies' strike there. The men adopted an anti-suffrage Liberal candidate. The ladies' committee refused to work for him ; but the men refused to give him up. In consequence Cardiff seat fell to a Conservative, after having belonged for many years to Liberalism. "1 was president of 1,000 women in the Women's Liberal Association, now I am president of 125 (or thereabouts) in the Women's Progressive Liberal Association," said Mrs. Lester Jones, " but fcr nothing in the world would I have gone back on the women's cause by helping an anti-suffragist." The same thing resulted at Cricklade. It will probably be repeated in Birkenhead, where the Liberal ladies have also g3ne on strike against an anti-suffrage Liberal candidate</p>
        <p>Yet every delegate present voted heartily in favour of Mr. Dickinson's Bill. They were not anti-suffragist, but they put Liberalism first : failing to see that true Liberalism, from my point of view, stands for repre- sentation, not a denial of it; stands also for protection of the weak. THE WOMEN AND GIRLS OF HAPPY ENGLAND</p>
        <p>The trouble with many delegates was that they were still ignorant cf the intolerable wrongs under which myriads of women and girls suffer in "happy England." The appalling fact that nine-tenths of the sweated labour in cur country falls upon the shoulders of its women had either failed to reach them or they lacked imagination to realise what that stood for. They forgot that Mr. Lloyd George, Lord Haldane and others of our leaders had publicly declared that this frightful sweating of women (and their consequent underselling of men) was directly due to the fact that they had no political standing, The Council threw away by a majority of two-thirds the one lever to which the Government would have responded—the threat to with- hold aid at the next election unless justice were at last done to the women citizens of the country. Of what use after that was their hearty and unanimous vote in favour of Mr. Dickinson's Bill ? It carried as much weight as a ball of thistledown. Out had the same impression about the subsequent work done by the Council. Of what use to deliberate, las we did, on Education, Housing, Land, Temperance, the Care and Detention of Feeble-minded and the Criminal Law Amendment Act? We passed the same resolutions that we have passed year by year, the same speeches, excellent in the main, were heard. But one felt, regretfully and sadly, that this great body of delegates, representing over 130,000 Liberal women, had thrown away a great chance of obtaining the pushing power of the vote, whereby all these resolutions might start into active, vigorous life, and bear fruit. Unrealized by the majority, they had helped to perpetuate the unequally weighted scales against women, they had helped to grind the faces of their poorer sisters. Will they learn to see deeper into things before the Council meets in 1914? T. H. CROSPIELD.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0093" n="art0093">
        <head>e.; amin er, Saturday, May 17, 1913.</head>
        <p>The e.; amin er, Saturday, May 17, 1913.</p>
        <p>LYMM MAY QUEEN FESTIVAL.</p>
        <p>Glorious Weather Crowns 'IA ell Attended and Picturesque</p>
        <p>Revels with Success.</p>
        <p>The May Quee:</p>
        <p>Beautiful Lyrnin looked her best ion Thursday when the picturesque ceremony of crowning the Miy Queen attracted thousands of visitors to the old-world village. The natural charms of the place provide a perfect setting for such a festival, and on this occasion they were seen under ideal conditions. The trees, in all their glory of fresh gieenery, the blossom-ct owned orchards, and the golden laburnum bushes make a picture of rural Cheshire which an artist might love to paint. But, best of all, they bad tile finishing touch of glorious sunshine. Fortune, for once, favoured the promoters, whose experience from the weather standpoint has, of ;ate years, been so dis- heartening that it required a large store of optimism to persist ia the undertaking. The good people of Lymm are, however, too proud of their festival to give it up without an effort, and this year their perseverance had its full reward in weather which rejoiced the hearts cf residents and visitors alike. The promise of the early morning *as fully borne out, the sun shining radiantly from a blue sky throughout the afternoon. True, the wind was some- what penetrating, but, contrasting the conditions with those of last year, there was not only nothing to com- plain about, but abundant cause for congratulation. By rail and road p3ople flocked into the village from Manchester, Wallington aLd district, and the attend- ance on the football field, where the coronation was enacted, was exceptionally large, the receipts from patrons of the stands probably reaching a record amount. Afternoon and evening programmes were carried out in their entirety, and afforded delight to the town dwellers who regard a May festival as a refreshing break in the year's routine.</p>
        <p>A PICTURESQUE PROCESSION. Within the shadow of the village cross the processionistm mustered fur their march through the quaint, winding streets. The weather-beaten relic of bye-gone ages was itsrlf the vantage ground for a large number of spectators, and, when the various characters were passing, the E cane presented a picturesque blending of the ancient and modern. The pageant, which appealed more by its tasteful simplicity than by any striking degree of originality, was quite in keeping with previous displays. There was the usual array of nursery rhyme notabilities and historical personages. Those old antagonists Napoleon and the Duke of Wellington fraternised as though the little incident of Waterlo ) were unheard of. Mary Queen of Scots and Joan of Arc lode on horse-</p>
        <p>;ri, Miss ' Gladys Hawthorne, Proceed'</p>
        <p>were a highly effective feature, the subjects depicted being " Blitannia. rind John "['he harvest queen " and "the old woman who lived in a shoe." Last of all came the Royal cort6ge on which, of course, chief interest was focussed. The May Queen, Gladys Hawthorne, a bright maid of thirteen, had been chosen for her exalted position by the scholars of Oughtrington school, who, alternately with the Lymrn children, have the honour of nominating a queen. Beautifully dressed in white, her Majesty " rode in an open carriage, attended by the crown and train bearers and two maids of honour. She carried a lovely bouquet of lilies and carnations, presented by Mr. 'l'. W. Dixon, and comported herself with queenly grace and dignity, acknowledging the plaudits of the audi- ence by repeatedly bowing. More maids of honour and the sceptre bearer followed in another carriage.</p>
        <p>to the Throne.</p>
        <p>before an even larger crowd, and a memorable day wound up with dancing en the green.</p>
        <p>TRE WORKERS.</p>
        <p>The arrangements for the festival involved an enor- mous amount of detail work, but the festival is fortu- nate in having officials who carry out their duties to the letter.</p>
        <p>Acknowledgment must be made of the invaluable help of the following : —Chairman, Mr. James D. B. Smith; vice-chairman, Mr. A. H. Hudson ; hon. treasurer, Mr. A. H. Hudson ; hon. secretary, Mr. James Webb, jun. Ladies' Committee : President, Miss M. Field ; hon. secretary, Mrs. Jos. D. B. Smith; Mrs. C. Lewis, Misses C. Boond, W. Mairs, J. Pearson, N. Pearson, E. Rushton, G. Shaw, B. Timperley, E. Winstauley, M. Watson and E. Wright. Gentlemen's Committee: Messrs. H. S. Brown, W. H. Blount, W. Coupland, jun., F.W.Druce, A.B. Edwards, A. Forrest, R.H. Gotobed, fl.Hesketh, S.Tnglefield,jun., W. G. Jones, H. Jones, Pye, C. Richards, H. S. Saville, jun., T. Saville, J. Thomason and \V. Timperley, jun.</p>
