      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0094" n="art0094">
        <head>CROWNING AND THE REVELS.</head>
        <p>THE CROWNING AND THE REVELS.</p>
        <p>Bordered on three sides by rich, spreading trees and commanding in one direction a pleasant view of pastoral country, the Football Field is ideally adapted to be the scene of rustic revels. Befoie the procession arrived the enclosure was thickly lined with spectators. At the top of the field the throne had been set up,amid an effective arrangement of trellis work and greenery, while at the other end the varie-colouied ribbons of the maypole fluttered in the breeze. ThrA crowning ceremony took place immediately the children had Made a circuit of the field and taken up their allotted positions to do homage to their "Sovereign." With stately, reverent mien, the crown-bearer slowly advanced along the strip of red baize which led to the steps of ;he throne. At intervals he paused to do obeisance. Having reached the throne, he placed the diadem on the head of the Queen, who added a happy personal touch by assisting to secure it in position, and the Boy Scuts' bugler gave a royal salute. With ,similar ceremonial, " her Majesty " was invested with the sceptre, and the Coronation was com- , plete. Then the familiar revels were entered upon, beginning, of course, with the Maypole dance. The children tripped the quaint measure in charm- ing style, and the ribbons 'ere speedily plaited, with- out. the semblance of a hitch IA seemed slightly incongruotN,however, f r the dance to be gone through to the strain of various modern ditties, instead of to some of the old-time tunes which we usually hear at May festivals. St. Joseph's band played a selection "Gaiety echoes" with praiseworthy precision and</p>
