      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0095" n="art0095">
        <head>e Liberal Candidate, Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth, admires the Children at the Festival.</head>
        <p>e Liberal Candidate, Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth, admires the Children at the Festival.</p>
        <p>back as to the manner born. King Chatles and Oliver Cromwell had o'er-leapt the centuries at d become contemporaries of Lord Nelson, while the little l'rinces in the Tower, judging by their equanimity, were quite oblivious of the doom which awaited them. Little 80-Peep had found her sheep and was, tierefore, relieved of all anxiety There were representatives of nationalities galore. Uncle Sam walked unconcernedly beside a Turk, who, to do him justice, was far from fearsome. Hindoos rubbed shoulders with Red Indians, and Australians found congenial company with fellow-colonists from south Africa. Leading characters in drama and opera were there in the p,Tsnils of Hamlet, Romeo and Carmen. A judge in full-bottomed wig did his best to assume judicial dignity and severity. Demure Quaker girls, bonny fisher lasses, troupes of fancy dancers and flower girls—all helped to add colour and variety to an ever-changing picture. A novelty which came in for nmeh appreciation was provided by the Gaelic Pipers' band from St. Joseph's Industrial School. Preceded by a standard hearer and halberdiers, the boys, dressed in the traditional garb of old Gaul, made a brave 'show, and the skirl of the pipes had a fine martial stir. Xtueic was also pro- vided by the military band from the school, aid detachments of boy scouts marched well and bore themselves in soldierly fashion. The lurry tableaux</p>
        <p>taste. Next a squad of bays from the school enter- tained the audience with an exceptionally smart dis- plgy of Swedish drill, which did credit alike to them- selves and to the r instructor, Professor M. Collins. Later the boys appeared in a series of delightful folk dances, and also in a Morris dance. the free rhythm of the old-fashioned measures contributing not a little to the charm of these exceptionally pleasing items. The village children, who had been excellently trained by Miss Field, of Altrincham, entered witn spirit into a country dance, the Irish jig, and an Italian dance which combined graceful movement and picturesque colour effects. Miss Lucy Jackson, of Altrincham, scored a very striking success in a solo dance, which was perfectly done, and at the close Mr. J. D. B. Smith, chairman of the com- mittee, expressed the appreciation of the promoters by presenting her with a beautifully bound copy of Tennyson's poems. The Gaelic pipers won round after round of applause as they paraded round the field play- ing a weird war march. The interesting programme completed, all the " subjects " of the Queen formed into procession and marched past the thrane. Among the interested spectators of the revels was Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth, the Liberal candidate for the Division, who was warmly cheered on entering and leaving the field. In the evening, p-irt of the programme was repeated</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0096" n="art0096">
