      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0097" n="art0097">
        <head>.ing</head>
        <p>.ing</p>
        <p>ALTRINCHAM BY-ELECTION.</p>
        <p>Liberal Candidate's Rapid Progress.</p>
        <p>RISING ENTHUSIASM.</p>
        <p>Candidates : Mr. L. Kay-Shuttleworth (L) Mr. G. C. Hamilton (C). . POLLING WEDNESDAY. MAY 28TH. December. 1910 : J. R. Kebty-Fletcher (C), O02;R Sir W. J. Crossley (L), 7,883 ; Cons. majority, IE3.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0098" n="art0098">
        <head>ACTIVITY AT LYMItt</head>
        <p>ACTIVITY AT LYMItt</p>
        <p>Young Liberals Enthusiastic.</p>
        <p>The keenest interest is being taken in the Lymm district in the Altriucham Division by-election. Last Saturday afternoon the Altrincham League of Young Liberals visited Lymm and distributed several thousand, pamphlets, whilst in the evening they held a lirgely attended and enthusiastic open-air meeting at the Cross, when the spi a'ters were accorded a roost re3pect- fill hearing and a resolution in support of the candi- dature of r. L. U. Kay-Shuttleworth was carried by a large majority. The Junior Conservatives took up their stand after the other part" had finished and their speakers were given a fair hearing, until tbe Chairman uttering an uncomplimentary remark about the Liberal candidate, which some of the young men present resented, with the result that the rest of the meeting was of a rather disorderly character. I\fonday a truce was called, that is as regards public meetings.</p>
        <p>On Tuesday night Newfield was the centre of attraction.where a meeting of the Junior Conservatives had been arranged; however, before they arrived a Mr. Weyland, an Irish gentleman, got up an impromptu meeting. and with Mr. Heywood Brooks as chairman delivered a most able, and judging from the attitude of his hearers, a most appreciated address on Home Rule. In the meantime the Junior Conservatives with Mr. T. F. W. Dixon as their chairman, had set up their pitch close by, but the crowd declined to leave Mr. Weyland until that gentleman had finished his speech. The opposition meeting was eventually adandoned as the audience were totally out of sympathy with lthe speakers and lost no opportunity of apprising them of the fact.</p>
        <p>A Free Trade Union meeting was also held at the Cross on Tuesday evening. The., same organization also held a meeting at Nev% field on Wednesday, when many of the Liberal stalwarts participated in a meeting at Broom Edge. he Liberal party also held a meeting in the Heatley Ward on Friday night.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0099" n="art0099">
        <head>ENCOURAGING SIGNS.</head>
        <p>ENCOURAGING SIGNS.</p>
        <p>Eroadheath Workingmen against Conscription.</p>
        <p>It is one of the most encouraging of the many signs which have been given to Liberalism in this con- test that when Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth went to Broad- heath on Thursday, says the " Manchester Guardian" correspondent, to address a dinner-hour crowd he was received with open arms. In putting the case against Tariff Reform be was talking obviously to people who were already convinced; in speaking of the record of the Government, to people who were, at least, satisfied. it was when be came to deal with conscription, or which, under the milder name of compulsory military training, Mr. Hamilton avows himself an enthusiastic supporter. that the Liberal candidate got his greatest cheers. Every point was followed with ready appre- ciation. First, it was unnecessary if our navy were really efficient, as it must be; without any compelling necessity to justify it, it would mean a violent break from the good old British tradition of voluntary service which puts one volunteer at something better than two pressed men. Incidentally it would affect prejudicially recruiting for the navy, our first and almost all-important line of defence ; and all through it would weaken our industrial resources and place an additional burden on the worker. The Broad- heath mechanic cheered every point, and his interest in the newest danger which a change of Government threatens to bring with it suggests that the same sub- ject might receive equally full treatment on other plat- forms in the constituency. Although he does not. exactly parade it, Mr. Hamilton at least does not con- ceal his affection for the new conscription, and, con- sidering the stage to which a diligent propaganda has pushed it, the electors of Altrincham are entitled to take account of the fact in considering their verdict. They did at Broadheath, and one gathered that Mr. Hamilton need not look for much support there. Not a single question was asked of Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth, and in an audience which has both the independence and the intelligence to heckle a candidate if needs he, this also may be taken as an encouraging sign. " We are all satisfied," shouted one of the workmen when the usual opportunity for the heckler to show himself had been offered, and the answering cheers proclaimed him a true spokesman. TARIFF REFORM AND FOOLISHNESS.</p>
        <p>The contest has already lasted quite a considerable time, and time always brings change. One of the changes it has brought in Altrin3haai is a matter tor nothing but regret. The happy, boyish disingenuous- ness with which Mr. Hamilton started out is wearing off under the stress of many trials. He has already begun to speak with the air of the old campaigner, whose best points need the lift of a shoulder or a move- ment of the eyelid for punctuation. "1 honestly confess to you," he said to a rural audience, " that I profess, at any rate, not to he a fool, and therefore I don't come in this constituency to advocate Tariff Reform. lam a Tariff Reformer, but lam not a fool." The Tories of Northern Etchells thought it was an awfully knowing thing for a fory—and Tariff Reform— candidate for Altrincham to say, and platform and hall joined in a long and steady chuckle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>MR. GLADSTONE AND DISESTABLISHMENT.</head>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE AND DISESTABLISHMENT.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. G. C. Gladstone, M.P., speaking at one of Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth's meetings, said Mr. Hamilton had appealed to Liberal Churchmen to put their religion, " for once at any rate," before their politics, to stand up and defend their religion, and vote on their politics at a general election. (Laughter.) If Mr. Hamilton desired the support of Liberal Churchmen, he ought not to appeal to them in a manner which was almost offensive. (Hear, hear.) What right had he to say to Liberal Churchmen, "put your religion, for once at any rate, tefore your politics ? " (Cheers.) Liberal Churchmen did not consider that they were guilty of subordinating their religion to their politics. They believed with all their hearts, that religion actuated their politics. (Renewed cheers.) As a member of the Established Church and the patron of two livings in Wales, while he had not hesitated to act and speak, where he thought the Bill was ungenerous and incon- siderate to the Church, against the party to which he had the honour to belong, if he had a vote in that division he would not hesitate to give it in favour of the Liberal candidate, if the question of Welsh Dis- establishment was the sole issue before the electors. (Loud cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>MR. GLADSTONE ON THE LIBERAL</head>
        <p>MR. GLADSTONE ON THE LIBERAL</p>
        <p>RECORD.</p>
        <p>At a large meeting at St. John's Schools, Cheadle Heath, Mr. W. G. C. Gladstone, M.P., in a reference to the social legislation of the Government, said the Trades Boards Act supplied the answer of the Liberal party to the evil of sweating. (Applause.) The experiment was applied in the first place to four industries which included some 200,000 workers, most of whom were women; and he was glad to see that, according to Mr. Sydney Buxton, five more industries were to be included under the Act. (Applause.) In his constituency a matter of concern to the work- ing classes was that the cost of living had gone up while there had not been a corresponding increase in wages. Of course, it was not true to say that wages had not advanced at all.—(Some interruption.) They had not. increased as much as the cost of living had increased. World cheapness could not be restored in a moment, but. thanks to the legislation of the Government since 190 G, the workers' condition had been substantially improved, not only by remission of taxation, not only by measures of social reform, but also by the fact that a gigantic sum had been collected and set aside for the benefit of the workingmen and women of this country. The Workmen's Compensation Act put nearly £4,000,000 annually into the pockets of the working classes, old- age pensions accounted for £12,000,000, and the con- tributions under the Insurance Act from the State and the employer in both parts of the Act amounted to £15,000,000 ; in all, an annual sum of £31.000,000. It was because of this great and benefic.nt legislation that he asked the workingman of Altrincham to stand by Mr. Kay-Shuttleworth at this electiou. (Loud cheers.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>Sale of Pigs at_ Walton Hall.</head>
        <p>Sale of Pigs at_ Walton Hall.</p>
        <p>Mr. W. Bainbridge —offered for sale by auction at Walton Hall on Thursday aft.,rnoon the celebrated Walton herd of middle white Yorkshire pigs, owned. by Sir Gilbert Greenall, Bart., C.V.O. The herd sNas dispersed in consequence of the recent purchase by Sir Gilbert of the entire herd of large white Yorkshire pigs owned by the Earl of Ellesmere, it having been found that much more accommodation would be required at the Walton piggeries, and in future, only pigs of the large white breed will be kept. The highist price realised was 30 guiueas, which was said by Mr. L. C. Paget, of York, for Halsnead Rose YL, a middle white sow nearly three years old. Altogether 43 lots realised 410 guineas, or an average of over 91 guineas.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
