      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>RAGTIME CRITICISED,</head>
        <p>RAGTIME CRITICISED,</p>
        <p>The Public Craze for Novelty and</p>
        <p>Excitement.</p>
        <p>TOWN COUNCIL AND MUSIC.</p>
        <p>The ragtime craze, which has held sway to such a remarkable extent that the country seems at last to be Nrearying of it, formed the subject of interesting criticism in the address of the president, Mr. Arthur Down, at the annual meeting of the Warrington Association of Bands, held at the Town Hall on Tues- day evening. The association has just attained its majority and the president spoke very hopefully of the future prospects. In his address, Mr. DOWN said that as bandmasters and bandsmen they had one aim in common—to uphold and, if possible, to advance music in Warrington and district. What could they do to achieve this? How could they convince their young players that they would never excel if they did not put some" backbone" into their work, and diligently practise scales and .exercises and the works of the great masters, the bed- rock of true music? By a very happy coincidence they were met that night on the 21st anniversary of the birthday of the Warrington Association of Bands. The association had that day attained its majority, and he was sure they would all join with him in wishing it a long, useful, and happy life. (Applause.) They might well review with satisfaction the work done in the years that, were past : the large number of performances given, and the great amount of pleasure those performances must have afforded to many thousands in the town. It was also a matter for no small degree of complacency that they hadheld together so long, and that their relations with one another had continued to be of such a friendly character. One principal reason for tbat happy state of affairs was that they were imbued with enthusiasm, without which no really good work could be accomplished. THE DIFFICULTIES OF CULTIVATING GOOD</p>
        <p>MUSIC. Perhaps there was no one present who did not realise the difficulties by which the cultivation of good music was surrounded at the present time, and all who %ere bandmasters doubtless felt the aecessity—now more than ever—for making every effort in their power to maintain the efficiency of their bands and to keep up the enthusiasm of the members. He was in entire agreement with a remark made by his Worship the Mayor :—" The influence of good music is incalculable and everything possible ought to be done to encourage the art." For toe past 21 years it had been their one aim and object to provide that good music, and he thought they might justly lay claim to a considerable measure of success.</p>
        <p>MUNICIPAL MUSIC. Although the words he had quoted did not particularly Tefer to music in the Park, the remark itself and the discussion which followed, gave reason to hope that the provision of music for the public would, in the future, receive a more prominent place teen -formerly in the municipal schemes for the betterment of the town, and that facilities would be afforded whereby that association would be enabled not only to provide more performances, but also to arrange for them to take place in different parts of the town, simul- taneously if need be. (Hear, hear.) At the present time there did not appear to be any immediate prospect of achieving this very desirable result, but from con- versation he had bad with gentlemen within the inner circle of municipal government he was led to the con- clusion that the town was awakening to a realisation of the fact that music was a necessity for the community, and that it should receive from the authorities adequate encouragement and recognition. (Applause.) With an adverse balance of over .£lO the association could not increase the number of performances this year ; on the contrary, there seemed no alternative but to curtail them "My earnest advice is," continued Mr. Down " that we go on hopefully and energetically with the perform- ances, even though on a less extensive scale, and by the high standard of the music rendered, endeavour to stimulate the public—like Dickens' Oliver Twist—to come forward and ask for more. If that happenr,l venture to think that the demand of the public will receive more favourable response than did that of' Oliver. He feared that, in these days, music was not regarded so much as a recreation as it formerly was, and that many young players lacked the determination and persever- ance necessary thoroughly to master their instruments, and methodically to plod through a systematic course of scales and exercises. CRAVING FOR NOVELTY. We often wondered where the present day craving for novelty and excitement would lead us. Skating rinks had given place to picture palaces, and tkese in their turn wculd doubtless, in course of time, be superseded by the boxing ring and other entertain- ments of a like nature, each more exciting than the last. All this was to the detriment ot music. It was a sad experience for our local musicians after putting -forth their best energies to produce high-class concerts, to find—as was usually the case—that good music by itself was not sufficient to fill Parr Hall. lie did not think this fact was due altogether to a lack of appre- ciation, but rather that most people now required some- thing more exciting for their money. He was told by frequenters of popular places of amusement that when any music above the average of merit was well played it was always appreciated and applauded by tee -audience. "RAGTIME AND OTHER RUBBISH."</p>
        <p>_ There was much ragtime and other rubbish performed in public in these days. Ragtime was considered new. It was nothing of the kind, but was simply a morbid and eccentric application of what might be found in any musical instruction-book. viz., "syncopation." It was syncopation run riot. It was extraordinary that this type of composition had obtained such a hold upon the public, but it was satisfactory to musicians to observe unmistakable signs that it had almost had its day. Under the beading "Too Much Ragtime" he read in the "Daily Mail" recently these words :—" The music-halls still resound with the haunting melodies of American troupes exploiting this craze, uut elsewhere ragtime appears to be dying. Its life—fast, furious, and even glorious at its zenith—shows signs of decline. . . . To ruany people the approaching end of ragtime brings no regrets, and conductors of orchestras in particular are looking forward to the time when their programmes shall contain no reference to ragtime" On the same page of that paper he read Chamber music, the most intimate of all forms of the tonal art, is growing in popular favour, and the eagerness with -which young musicians combine for the performance of concerted pieces is a healthy sign." Here in Warrington the question of a return to a better state of things rested largely with themselves as practical musicians. If, by their choice of programmes bandmasters showed that they themselves appreciated really good music, they would find that the true musicians amongst their bandsmen would appreciate it also. His own experiences during the past year had been decidedly encouraging, and he had observed a growing apprecia- tion of high-class music amongst bandsmen. Taking one consideration with another,he thought they had no cause to be despondent about the future work of this association, but that they should rather go forward with confidence and the determination to make the coming season a greater musical success than those that had goue before. That meant work—hard work—not only on the part of bandmasters, but also by every member of the bands. Was not the art of music worthy of their effort ? He thought so. (Applause.)</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>SEASON'S WORK.</head>
        <p>THE SEASON'S WORK.</p>
        <p>In his report the SECRETARY stated that the season in the Park opened on May 19th and concluded on October 6th. Thirty-one performances were given, 20 on Sundays and 11 on week-days, or one less than during the previous season. Some very good perform- ances were given, and there was quite an advsnce in point of merit on the performances of former seasons. Applications bad been received from the Royal Antedeluvian Order of Buffaloes Military Band and the Brick-street Mission Band to become members of the association.</p>
        <p>The report was adopted. The balance sheet was approved, and showed that there was a balance due to the bank of £lO 7s. id. The contribution from the Warrington Corporation was £7O, and the sum of £BO 12s. 10d. had been paid to bands. Mr. Down was heartily thanked for his interesting address.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</head>
        <p>ELECTION OF OFFICERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. R. SMITH, in proposing the re-election of Mr. Down as president, said be thought there were few associations in the country that had a better president. If they lost him they were losing one of the best grips for upholding music in this part of the country. (Applause.) Mr. R. THOMPSON seconded, and the proposition was carried.</p>
        <p>Mr. DOWN, in reply, said it would be his endeavour to do all he could to promote the interests of the association.</p>
        <p>The Mayor, and Messrs. Harold Smith, M.P., F. W. Monks. A. H. Crosfield, and the members of Town Hall, Parks and Baths Committee were elected vice- presidents. Mr. J. A. Dawson was re-elected hon. secretary, and Mr. J. Fairhurst bon treasurer by a unanimous vote. The PRESIDENT said they could not have two better men to occupy the positions. (Hear, hear.) Mr. DAWSON promised he would do his best to further the interests of the association.</p>
        <p>Hearty votes of thanks were accorded all those who had helped to make the association successful.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>WHITSUNTIDE CAMPS.</head>
        <p>WHITSUNTIDE CAMPS.</p>
        <p>TERRITORIALS AT ALTCAR.</p>
        <p>Whitsuntide is always a popular holiday for camp- r. ing purposes, and, notwithstanding the unsettled weather conditions prevailing last week-end, local Territorials, Chweb Lads' Brigade and Boy Scouts were not deterred from indulging it the usual sojourn under canvas. Over 200 members of the 4th Battalion Prince of Wales Volunteers (south Lancashire Regiment) underwent a course of musketry at Altcar, from Saturday to Monday. Lieut.-Colonel B. Faitclough was in command, and the other officers present were: Majors Appleton and G. It. Crosfield, Captains H. G. Roberts, H. Owen, F. R. Monks and Egerton Fairclough, I ieutenants M. Woods, 0. L. Whittle, Eric Fairclough, L. S. Elenshall, A. Skinner A. Seymour-Jones, E. L. Frost, Second-Lieutenant B. T. Dickson, Captain and Adjutant G. Elgood, and Lieutenant and Quartermaster G. H. Ingram. The instruction in musket,ry was efficiently carried out and the results are regarded as highly satisfactory. A religious service was held on the field oa Sunday for members of the Church of England. It was conducted by the Colonel, assisted by Major Appleton. Roman Catholic members of the battalion attended service at Formby Church.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0111" n="art0111">
        <head>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE AT</head>
        <p>CHURCH LADS' BRIGADE AT</p>
        <p>APPLETON.</p>
        <p>From Saturday to Monday the 2nd Battalion Liver- pool Regiment (Cadet) were in camp in a field at Appleton, kindly placed at their disposal by Mr. C. Britch. A B and E Companies mustered strongly, under the command of Sergt.-Major H. H. Bottoinlev. The advance party left Warrington on Friday at 7.30 p.m., and, reaching the camp at 8.45 son set to work to make the necessary arrangements. Tents were pitched, and, after lighting a fire, tea was prepared, and the party retired to a %%ell-earned rest at midnight. At four o'clock on Saturday morning the party were again astir, and the final setting and pegging of the tents was carried out.</p>
        <p>The main contingent paraded at Warrington at 2.15 on Saturday afternoon, and soon arrived in camp. After being detailed to their respective tents the cooks proceeded to is-ue tea, which was very acceptable. At six p.m. guard was mounted and kept throughout the night, great pride being taken in the arrangements which had been made. A countersign was used on both nights, and no person was allowed in or near the camp after 10.15 p.m., nor was anyone allowed to pass unless he could give the countersign. Attempts were made by several N.C.O.'s to pass into camp, but all proved unavailing. On Sunday the camp was astir at reveille and paraded under the c,mmand of Sergeant Instructor Leach, B Company, and went for a sharp double and march for a couple of miles. On returning to camp the cooks had prepared breakfast, which was thoroughly enjoyed. The whole camp (excepting the hard working cooks) afterwards attended Divine service at Appleton Church. During the morning Major Brookes, Captain and Adjutant Sankey, Lieut. and Quartermaster Bunch, and Lieut. Younger, E Company, rode over from Warrington and inspected the camp and also attended the church parade. The Vicar took for his text" If ye love Me keep My commandments." He spoke earnestly upon the need of discipline in our spiritual and everyday life, and emphasized the fact that that was the aim of the C.L B.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>BOY SCOUTS AT FRODSHAM.</head>
        <p>BOY SCOUTS AT FRODSHAM.</p>
        <p>A week-end camp at Bradley Orchu.rd, Frodsham, was attended by about fifty members of the Ist War- rington (Whitecross) and All Saints' Troops on the invitation of the 2nd Widnes (St. Mary's) Troop. The Scouts left Warrington shortly before 3 o'clock on Saturday afternoon under the charge of Warrant Scoutmaster Bennett. Several showers were encountered during the march, but ample precautions had been taken, and the Scuts arrived at camp quite dry, though tired. On Sunday the Scouts paraded at Overton Church, together with the 2nd Widnes (St. Mary's) and the Simms' Cross Troops. The Ist Warrington Bugle Band headed the troops, and Scoutmaster Bennett was in command. Several games were indulged in on the Monday, and a start was made for home shortly before seven o'clock. Warrington was reached at 10 o'clock, and on arrival at headquarters the troop were given a hearty reception by the great crowd of people waiting for them. The camp was visited by a large number of people, both from Warrington and Appleton. A word of praise is due to Sergeant S. Thornton (the sergeant- cook) and his assistants who so admirably suppled the wants of the camp. On the whole the weather was very good. Showers occasionally fell, but they did not interfere with the work of the camp. Football and other games, coupled with music supplied by Sergeant Fenton on the violin, served to make the camp lively, and although work and play were combined, all ranks thoroughly enjoyed the outing. There was no case of sickness and there- fore the ambulance had nothing to do.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0113" n="art0113">
        <head>RED RASH ITCHED AND BURNED.</head>
        <p>RED RASH ITCHED AND BURNED.</p>
        <p>22, Mildmay St., Plymouth, Eng.—" The first sign of my skin trouble was a lot of red rash which came out on my face and at night would itch and burn and I was bound to scratch the places, and after a little while my face was covered with white-headed pimples which caused me much pain at night. This lasted two or three months. All this time I had been trying different remedies which did not take much effect and so I thought I would try some Cuticura Soap and Ointment which I did and after a few weeks nearly all the pimples were gone, the burning and itching was stopped, and within a few days my face was clear. I still use Cuticura Soap and have not had any signs nf any more pimples." (Signed) F.Winnicott,Aprillo,l9l2. Cuticura Soap and Ointment are sold by chemists everywhere. A sample of each with 32-p. book post- free from nearest depot ; Newbery, 27, Charterhouse Sq., London; R. Towns &amp; Co., Sydney, N. S. W. ; Lennon, Ltd., Cape Town; Muller, Maclean &amp; Co., Calcutta and Bombay; "Cuticura," Boston, U.S.A. Tender-faced men should shave with Cuticura Soap Shaving Stick. Sample free.'</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0114" n="art0114">
        <head>ANGLERS IN CONFERENCE.</head>
        <p>ANGLERS IN CONFERENCE.</p>
        <p>National Railway Privilege Ticket Advocated.</p>
        <p>Mr.L. G. Woodruff, the president of the National Federation of Anglers, presided at the annual confer- ence of the Federation at Birmingbam,on Saturday. The gathering was attended by 40 delegates representing upwards of 78,000 anglers. A long discussion took place on the question of a universal railway ticket. The fit.ll and District Amalga- mated Anglers' Association had placed on the agenda a resolution that steps be taken towards securing a National Federation of Anglers' railway privilege ticket . .</p>
        <p>Mr. Tingle (Saeffield) moved an ainendmei-it that a deputation be appointed to wait upon the railway com- panies in regard to securing a National Federation of Anglers privilege ticket. Mr. Broadwith (Hull) agreed to withdraw the pro- posal of his association. Mr. F. Grey (London) opposed the resolution. While Sheffield took five per cent. of all railway tickets issued to its members, his association were not allowed by the railway companies to issue a single ticket. Finally, the Sheffield proposition was accepted by 16 votes to four.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0115" n="art0115">
        <p>_ V ,II IA (AUSTRALIA): c_ LAI4O of SUNSHINE. FRUIT de FLOWERS. GV,P For Farmers, Farm Workers, British Lads for Farm cl N xko • Work, Domestics Vi• and 4i) 00.. \'' For Artisans. ...65) _ \ particulars, . apply 6 N$ 0.85 vO' James MacLeod, E Land Settlement and ,smigration Commissioner, Victorian Government Office, Strand, London, W.C.</p>
