      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0116" n="art0116">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 17, 1913,</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 17, 1913,</p>
        <p>CO-OPERATION MD</p>
        <p>POVERTY.</p>
        <p>Are Present Dividends or Permanent Benefits Sought ?</p>
        <p>EQUITABLE WEALTH DISTRIBUTION.</p>
        <p>It is just about one hundred years ago that the movement, of which Robert Owen was the mainspring, took its rise with the object of revolutionising the whole system of industry and social institutions. In reading the history of those days, no one can fail to be fascinated by the intrepid Owen, who could throw to the wind all conventionalities, orthodox theories and popular prejudices, attacking the evils of his day by the experimental application of his theories. The educational provisions made for his workpeople at New Cranark would alone mark him out as a man very much in advance of his time; but his faith in the possibility of re-constructing the industrial system, coupled with the zeal and vigour with which he endeavoured to put his faith into practice, is the feature that raises him to a worthily conspicuous position in the army of reformers. The possession of a moderate fortune would, no doubt, be an advantage to him as compared with others of a similar bent; but that he must also have had a remarkable power of infusing other people with his own spirit is sufficiently evident from the innumerable and very varied projects initiated, directly or indirectly, by him with a view to a comn-on object. Ventures for the co-operation of workers in joint-stock efforts dotted the land; Labour Exchanges, under various names, for a time flourished for the purpose of enabling anyone to exchange the product of his labour for the product of others, and, as Mr. Holyoake says, "communities, the form which the religion of industry was to take, were from 1825 to 1830 as common and almost as frequently announced as joint-stock companies now are." " Communities " failed, Labour Exchanges failed, Co-operative Stores all but failed. A LIFE-GIVING INNOVATION.</p>
        <p>It was the now famed Rochdale 'pioneers who, in 1811, put new life into the languishing Co-operative Store by their innovatory device of distributing profits amongst the purchasers in proportion to the amount of their purchases, instead of amongst shareholders in proportion to their holding. To what gigantic dimension the movement has now attained is common knowledge; but it is perhaps not so commonly appre- ciated as it ought to be that this innovation was not the outcome of the investigations of professional political economists or others of the cultured classes, but was the result of the uncultured business instinct of those early pioneers who were evidently still so far under the spell of Robert Owen as to think and act inde- pendently, regardless of precedent and unfettered by conver.tion. This new departure was the means at any rate of giving a tremendous impetus to the Co-opera- tive movement and in that respect may be regarded as eminently successful. But we cannot be quite so certain that its effect has been to bring us appreciably nearer to that Co-operative Commonwealth, which is or should be the objective of all co-operators. We are in the first place faced with the dismal fact that in spite of the expansion of Co-operation the conditions of industry, so far as the workers are concerned, remain broadly as they were in Owen's time, and the industrial unrest is as acute to-day as ever. And, what is still more discouraging, the growth of the Co-operative movement to its present national proportions has been accompanied by innumerable other agencies making for the uplifting of the masses—the large number of legislative enact- ments affecting all phases of the people's lives—the trades union movement—the many services rendered to the public by municipal and State action. If, then, we can discern no appreciable improvement in the industrial system from Co-operation, even in conjunc- tion with the many other reforming agencies, we rosy, at least, doubt whether the movement is yet on right lines; and it is, perhaps, not unnatural that many people should view the Co-operative Stores pretty touch in the same light as Mr. Owen is said (on the authority of Mr. Holyoake) to have regarded them, i.e., "as ignominious substitutes for the reconstruction of the world."</p>
        <p>DIVIDENDS: THEIR ECONOMIC EFFECTS. There is, however, another side from which we may approach the subject and consider the economics of the question. We have said that the ideal of the co-operator is, or should be, a Co-operative Common- wealth. The probability is, however, that only a very very small percentage of members of co-operative societies have any such ideal, and that nearly all neither have nor ever had such an ideal. The inducement to " co-operate " is in fact dividend, and, under existing conditions of industry, which impels men to look to self first even against their inclination : any other motive for some two and a half million of the working class seems out of the question. Complaint is often made that Stores' members exhibit comparatively little interest to the social and educa- tional side of their work. As a matter of fact, there is not the same necessity for this branch of the work as was once the case before the many avenues that modern improvements have made possible were accessible, and members probably pay more directly out of their earnings to-day according to their individual necessity or inclination than ever they would have paid collectively through their society. The test of the value of the co-operative movement (at least as far as the distributive societies are con- cerned) seems to lie in the consideration of the ultimate effect of the dividend. Now, assuming that the whole of the dividend declared is so much absolute profit or gain, can thus be considered a per- manent addition to the purchaser's income ? Or does it simply result under the constant stress of industrial competition, in a reduction of the cost of living and therefore of wages? We are only too familiar with the fact that in other departments of trade this is actually what results. We cheapen production by ' the introduction of machinery, but, as wages tend to the subsistence level by the operation of competition, the workman does not get the benefit in his earnings. The introduction of the Free Trade fiscal system has resulted in the reduction of the cost of living, but not with a corresponding advantage to the lower classes of labour. We must therefore assume that the Stores dividend, if it does amount to an actual reduction in the cost of living must, by the action of economic forces, result in a corresponding reduction of wages. To prove this by actual figures would, of course, be impossible on so narrow a margin as two or three shillings. Whilst we may have forces tending to reduce wages in one direction we may have forces tending to inciease them in another, the one set of forces obscuring wholly or partly the effect of the other. Taking a broad view of the relation of the Co-operative movement to the industrial system, the truth seems to be that by an effective and economical form of distribution it becomes one agency amongst many for cheapening cost of living, thus tending to the greater expansion of the nation's industry, but does nothing to equalise the distributou of wealth.</p>
        <p>It seems to be the view of many co-operators that by the extension of producing societies the two departments will come together and form the Commonwealth. They are, however, as really separate concerns as are private firms of producers. They buy their lab ,ur in the open market at competitive rates and sell the pro- duce, to all intents and purposes, in the open market. Before the producers and consumers can come together as a Co-operative Commonwealth the very basis of their economic relationships will have to be altered and adjusted to their mutual interests when combination of Co-operative workers would be unheard of. THE HOPE OF CO-OPERATION.</p>
        <p>If the Co-operative movement could be again infused with the spirit of Robert Owen and resort to those empirical methods of procedure from which the movement sprang the true end might yet be attained. We know what one man with a comparatively small fortune at his back was able to do. What then ought a national organisation to accomplish which has established itself through the land and has millions of wealth dt its disposal? Not surely a mere two or three shillings per week for its members by way of dividend but rather two, three or more pounds per week by way of a more equitable distribution of wealth.</p>
        <p>JO dN A. J. MORTON.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0117" n="art0117">
        <head>ODD FELLOWS AND THE INSURANCE ACT.</head>
        <p>ODD FELLOWS AND THE INSURANCE ACT.</p>
        <p>The conference of the Manchester Unity of Odd- fellows this week adopted a resolution declaring that the Insurance Act should be so amended as to restore to approved societies the right to administer medical benefits under the Act. The director who moved the resolution said that in regard to National Insurance the doctors ha ii played a despicable part, and he com- plained of the cost and quality of the medical service given.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>LYMM.</head>
        <p>LYMM.</p>
        <p>CfIOIR. OUTING.—The Lymm Parish Church Choir with a number of friends went to Frodsham for their annual outing on Wednesday. Being favoured with a fine day they had a very enjoyable trip.</p>
        <p>ORGAN RECITAL.—At the close of the evening service at the Parish Church on Sunday, Mr. W. Cooke, F.R.C.0., gave one of his welcome organ recitals. The items were judiciously nelected, and the performance, as would he expected by all who are acquainted with Mr. Cooke's abilities, was highly meritorious. The follow- ing was the programme, Findlandia (Sibelrus), Andante (Franz Schubert), and Ails Marcia (V. Righini.) Mr. G. Bowen also contributed a bass solo " Hosanna in the Highest," which was greatly enjoyed. HOLIDAY VISITORS.—Owing to the increased rates for bringing children as far as Lymm, the unsettled weather, &amp;c., there have not been so many sohools from the Manchester districts as in past years ; in fact Wednesday was the first day this week when any num- ber of visiting scholars were about, and these were principally from one school. There were a fair num- ber of visitors in on Monday, including a Boys' Brigade, which serenaded Booth's Hill for several licurs. Tuesday was exceptionally quiet in spite of its being such a bright day, whilst Thursday, with its attraction in the form of the May Queen Festival, was the popular day of the week, in fact at certain periods the village was crowded to a very uncomfortable extent.</p>
        <p>LYMNI TERRITORIALS IN CAMP.—This year the Cheshire Territorials have gone to camp much earlier in the season than usual, presumably in the hope of securing better weather, and a considerable number of people assembled on the Lymm station platform on Smithy morning, despite the early hour of departure, 7.30., to give the local men a hearty send-off as they boarded toe special train. On this occasion the camp, which consists of the 4th, an, GO, and 7th Battalions of the Cheshire Regiment, under the commind of Lieut.-Colonel J. E. G. Groves, V.D., of Lyrnm, com- prising men from Chester, Altrincharn,Sale and Cheadle, Frodsham and Lymtn (under the command of Captain Muller, and Lieu!. G. W. C. Hartley), Northwich, Runcorn, Stockport, Macclesfield, Stalybridge, etc., is being held at Abergavenny in Monmouthshire, a favourite place with the older hands." Most of the Lymm men have gone for a fortnight's training, but a few of them who are only able to go for a week make their departure to-day (Saturday). The early reports from the camp state that the climatic conditi'ms are most unfavourable, but the men, profiting from lessons gained last year, are in much better circumstances.</p>
        <p>STATHAM FESTIVAL.—After the FUCCO33 which attended last year's Statham festival the committee of management, consisting principally of Messrs. T. F. W. Dixon, F. Latham, F. Lockyer and G. H. Parker, decided to make the event an annuli one, with the result that full plans for a similar fete were arranged for last Monday, only, however, to b 3 spoiled by the heavy rain which poured down all afternoon and most of the evening. Making the best of the unfortunate conditions a procession, headed by the Lymm Subscrip- tion Band, marched through Booth's-hill to the Croa and then on to Mr. Norcott's field down Statharn,where a Amber of children, who had been trained by Miss Appleton, creditably performed the Maypole dance. Tea was immediately served to the children in the Statham Infants' school, after which the old folks were similarly treated. Though it was not possible to hold the sports each child received a choice present,A bilst the old ladies were the recipients of a Ilb. of tea, and the men a ,}lb. of tobacco each, the presentations being gracefully made by Mrs. Dixon, Mrs. Parker and Miss Lockyer. During the evening public dancing was participated in on the school playgrouid to the strains of the Lymm Band.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>STOCKTON HEATH.</head>
        <p>STOCKTON HEATH.</p>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE TRIP.—The choice of New Brighton by the committee of St. Thomas's Band of Hope for their trip was very popular, both with members and friends. Hence on Thursday a large party of members and friends left the village in cars for the Central Station en route for Liverpool. The sail to New Brighton was a treat to the children, many of whom had never been on a steamer before. Tea and cake were provided for the children on arrival and at four o'clock an excellent tea was provided in St. James' Hall, which had been taken for the day. The party was accompanied by the Vicar aud a large band of willing workers, who deserved every praise for their efforts in looking after the children. After tea the time was spent in racing on the sands for prizes, which the committee had been able to provide through the kindness of Mr. Davies, Birchdale ; Mrs. Milling, Mrs. Bedel, liss Edgerley and Miss Willett. The party left by the 7.15 boat for home, which was safely reached a little after 9 o'clock.</p>
        <p>Daresbury Petty Sessions. These Sessions were held at the Stockton Heath Court House on Tussday, the ruagistrstes on the Bench being :—Mr. Koger C. Parr (in the chsir), Messrs. H. C. Burder, L. Greening, W. Fletcher, Captain H. G. Roberts and Mr. C. Jackson Holmes. MINOR OFFENCES.—Peter Curbishlev, of Little Leigh, was charged with having, on the 18th April, on the Tarporley-road, ridden a bicycle at 9.15 p.rr.i without a lighted lamp, and was fined 53.—Joseph Mulsdale, Appleton, was charged with a similar offence on the Appleton-road, and was fined 2. 6d.—For allowing a dog to stray on the highway without a collar bearing the name and address of the owner, Thomas Hankinson, of Ash Grove, Latchford, was fined 2s. 61—The follow- ing were fined for keeping dogs without a licence : Fred Andrews, Herbert C. N. Peapell and John Thomp- son (two dogs), all of Walton Inferior, 2g. 6d., ss. and 7s. Gd. respectively.—Ann Beatrice Joynson, of the Hall Cale, Stockton Heath, was summoned in respect of two dogs, and as she did not appear was fined 15s. and costs. —William Ward, Dosberton Bank, near Spalding, and James Cook, of Orford-lane, Warrington, were each fined 104. and costs for a bresch of the Locomotives Act, 1898.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>FEARNHEAD.</head>
        <p>FEARNHEAD.</p>
        <p>WHITSUNTIDE TREAT.—Whit Monday WBS Et dis- appointment for the children at Fearnhead, as they have come to regard the annual treat given by Mr. George Mather as the great event of the year. In spite of the inclement weather, however, a large nsmber of children assembled at the farm and enjoyed the tea which had been prepared for them. After tea a package of nuts and an orange were given to each child. The games usually indulged in had to be dispensed with on occount of the rain.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PADGATE.</head>
        <p>PADGATE.</p>
        <p>ANNIVERSARY SERVICKS.—The anniversary services in connection with the Wesleyan Sunday School were held on Sunday last. Eloquent sermons were preached at morning and evening services by the Rev. F. W. Kernick, M.A., of Blackburn, formerly of the Warring- ton Circuit. The rev. gentleman also gave an interest- ing address at the flower service held in the afternoon, over which Mr. H. W. Guest presided. The amount of the collections showed a gratifying increase over those received last year.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>ST. HELENS.</head>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS RATES. Another increase is expected in the St. Helens rates on account of the large expenditure on new schools and tho cost of providing treatment for tubercular patients under the Insurance Act, but it is hoped that the increase will not exceed Id. or 2d. in the pound. The Gas Committee are said to he in a position to give £5,000 from the gas profits to the relief of the rates, although the profits made during the last year on the gas works is £2,000 less, owing to the increased price of coal and the reduction in the charges for gas to the townspeople. WHIT MONDAY PROCESSIONS. - - -</p>
        <p>The anr;;Z-pin-c;;sion of the guilds and school children in connection with the nine Roman Catholic Churches in St. Helens took place on Whit Monday and, fortunately for all concerned, was favoured with fine weather. Over 8,000 people took part in the procession, which was headed by Dr. Whiteside,Catholic Archbisho D of Liverpool, who was accompanied by the Very Rev. Dean O'Sullivan. Fifteen brass bands were engaged in the procession. The chief point of interest was the Town Hall Square, where the Mayor and Mayoress, Mr. and Mrs. Crooks, Alderman Foote. and Councillor Brooke received the Archbishop,and afterwards watched the long procession march past. The school children and Bible Classes connected with St. Helens Parish Church, Ravenhead, and St. Thomas's Churches, held their field days and sports in the afternoon, turning out in large numbers. The rain greatly interfered with the success of these functions, but all the events were carried out as well as possible.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PRESCOT GALA.</head>
        <p>PRESCOT GALA.</p>
        <p>A carnival and gala, promoted by the Prescot Athletic and Wireworks Recreation Club took place in Knowsley Park on Monday. The procession of tradesmen's turn</p>
        <p>TO' BEST SPRING TONin --..zarcri.</p>
        <p>outs, tableaux, decorated cycles, &amp;c., paraded through the town and were afterwards judgeo in the park, where all kinds of old English sports were indulged in, and the Rose Queen (Miss !kitty Ames) was crowned by Miss Sinclair, of Huyton. The procession was exceedingly pretty,and the attendance on the ground numbered about 7,000. Unfortunately, rain set in soon after the gala commenced and greatly interfered with the comfort of all concerned. The large covered stand was a grea- boon to the onlookers. The dances by the school children, which were carried out on a raised platform and to the music of the British Insulated Silver Band were really well done, and earned the warmest admira- tion of the crowd. Messrs. J. Butterfield, A. Quarrie Robert Clayton, F. E. Millward, C. W. H. Richardson G. Hemingway and Mrs. Evans were the judges, and a very capable band of workers were under the direction of Messrs. A. Lauder and W. CIAO, hon. secretaries.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN INSURANCE COMMITTEE'S CLERKSHIP.</p>
        <p>The first meeting of the Runcorn Insurance Com- mittee was held in the Town Hall on Saturday evening, when Mr. J. Howard, of Victoria-road, Runcorn, was appointed, out of nineteen applications for the position of clerk, at a salary of £4l per annum. Mr. J. W. Leathwood was selected chairman</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <head>RUNCORN SUNDAY TRADERS.</head>
        <p>RUNCORN SUNDAY TRADERS.</p>
        <p>Last week Superintendent Ennion asked the magis- trates at Runcorn to impose costs on the Sunday traders, and remarked that one of the persons cr,ncerned had figured before the magistrates over 120 times. Nine defendants appeared at the court on Tuesday on the Sunday trading charge, but the question of costs was not raised, and a nominal fine of ss. was imposed. In the case of Mary J. Lamb, she told the magistrates she was not in a position to psy the fine. " Very well," remarked the clerk, "a distress warrant will be issued for your goods."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <head>WHITSUNTIDE PROCESSION AT RUNCORN.</head>
        <p>WHITSUNTIDE PROCESSION AT RUNCORN.</p>
        <p>The principal event of the Bank Holiday at Runcorn was the annual procession of Sunday school scholars. Since the Coronation the Nonconformist, the Anglican, and the Roman Catholic schools have united and make one huge demonstration. It was estimated that some 4,000 scholars assembled at the various schools and proceeded via Regent-street, High-street, and Greenway- road, to the Park, where games were indulged in. Throngs of people lined the route. Special interest was attached to two veterans who were walking, one Mr. J. Higgins, who made his 70th appearance, and Mr. J. Pownall, who had previously walked in the procession 45 times.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <head>FIRE AT A RUNCORN WORKS.</head>
        <p>FIRE AT A RUNCORN WORKS.</p>
        <p>Early on Saturday morning some workmen dis- covered a fire to be raging in a large wooden shed near the end of a jetty at Wiggs' works of the United Alkali Company, at Runcorn. The jetty, which runs alongside the Manchester Ship Canal, is used for the purpose of unloading boats, and the elevators and conveyors which are used for the purpose are driven by electricity. It was seen that the flames had obtained a good hold of the structure, and the fertilising department of the works was in grave danger of becoming involved, but by the promptitude displayed by the works fire br gade this was averted. The efforts of the Runcorn Fire lirigade were directed to the surrounding sheds, and a plentifu supply of water being available they accomplished their task. The machinery was destroyed, and a number of workmen will be temporarily thrown out of employ- ment. Tbe fire could be discerned for ma,ny miles around. The damage is stated to be considerable.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>DREADED CONSUMPTION. INQUEST ON RUNCORN MAN.</head>
        <p>DREADED CONSUMPTION. INQUEST ON RUNCORN MAN.</p>
        <p>An inquest was held at Runcorn on Wednesday on John Richard Pope, aged forty-seven years, of Rutland street. Dr. Al'Donald slid he had made a post-mortem on the deceased, who was &amp; very muscular and well-nourished man. He would have had very little difficulty in say- ing to what he attributed to the cause of death if it was not for a bottle containing a liquid labelled " Poison " which the deceased's wife handed to him when he was called on the scene' saying she had found it on the table. Pope had expired fully half an hour before he arrived. The membranes and the external parts of the brain and the liver were very much congested. The stomach was inflamed, but any kind of alcoholic liquor would cause that. The heart was not diseased. The result of the post-mortem, to his mind, naturally pointed to syncope as being the cause of death. The widow said her husband was very eccentric in his habits. Seven years ago he threatened to take away his life. He had a dread that he would die of con- sumption, which, his family suffered from. When he used to talk to her he would often ask her how she could get on in the world if he was gone. Ho had com- plained of pains in his head. The Coroner (gr. T. Ridgway) adjourned the inquiry until next Thursday, so as to have the contents of the bottle analysed.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</head>
        <p>WIDNES TOWN COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>LIVELY SCENE.</p>
        <p>The second evening meeting in connection with the Widnes Town Council was held in the Town Hall on Tuesday. There was only a moderate attendance of members and the general public. The Highway Com- mittee's minutes provoked a scene, which was ended by the intervention of the Mayor.</p>
        <p>Mr. Welding reported progress in the road making, and finally touched on the vexed question of the motor- buses. He congratulated the 'Bus Committee on the excellent facilities offered to the public, and ventured to say that in the near future the service would be a financial success.</p>
        <p>Mr. Garghan, a member of the committee, repudiated indignantly the assertion that the Motor-hus Committee was making progress at all. They bad lost £2OO during the month, be said, and the time had come for drastic action to be taken. He suggested doing away with the service for six months. Rates were going up, and the people were harassed in consequence. There were some members who did not look on the serious side of things. Mr. Hamilton : Prove it.</p>
        <p>Mr. Garghan : Look your figures up. There are two 'buses in the garage without chassis and others without engines.</p>
        <p>--ill% Caldwell : Dln't play to the gallery. Mr. Hedley protested strongly against the statements made, and said Councillor Garghan rarely attended. Mr. Hamilton said Councillor GArghan had never expressed himself in committee as he had done that night. He was pandering to the gallery, and what he bad said was not common sense, not business, and there was no sincerity in his remarks. After a further explanation by the Chairman (Mr. Welding) the minutes were passed. HOSPITAL FACILITIES.</p>
        <p>The provision of the new Accident Hoqpital was urged on the Council. Mr. Hedley said there was a sum of money in the bank (C 1.200 subscribed by the people of Widnes. The hospital was a disgrace to the town, and it was only a rabbit warren; and if ever the place fired it was God help the pour cripples who were there.</p>
        <p>Alderman G. Neil said a scheme was under consideration. £3OO EXPENDITURE ON ROYAL VISIT. The General Purposes Committee reported that the arrangements for the Royal visit included the erection of stands and platforms at the Town Hall and pro- vision for children in Victoria Park. The committee had voted the Mayor £3OO to defray the cost. The Mayor said the larger portion of the money would be spent on the children in the town.</p>
      </div>
