      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0133" n="art0133">
        <head>"I REMEMBER "</head>
        <p>"I REMEMBER "</p>
        <p>REMINISCENCES AND STORIES OF OLD WARRINGTON. GLEANED FROM MANY QUARTERS.</p>
        <p>Mr. Mounfield continues his series of recollections supplied to him by Warringtonians who have a personal knovvlcdge of the past. Ile will be glad to receive contributions to what will undoubtedly prove to be amongst the most interesting chapters in local history ever published.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0134" n="art0134">
        <head>COCKHEDGE OF SIXTY YEARS</head>
        <p>THE COCKHEDGE OF SIXTY YEARS</p>
        <p>AGO. DAYS OF FARMS AND GARDENS.</p>
        <p>Reading in the " Examiner " of the Mayor's visit to Cockhedge during Health Week recalled to my memory what it was like over 60 years ago. At that time Cockhedge was regarded as one of the healthiest parts of Warrington, standing high and with an open space on the left. The entrance to the factory was at the top of Naylor-street at that time, and Allan's Factory stood where the Catholic Church is now. I remember Allan's factory being burned down. My father was one of the men that helped to lift Mr. Gandy over the iron railings. I think the same railings are there yet. The poor man was coming from the top storey down a rope and fell between the rails where there was not much space. The mill was down in twenty minutes, I believe. At that time there was no water supply, only from pumps in different parts of the town. Of course, the houses had raintubs for cleaning purposes. When Allan's factory was on fire I remember there were two rows of people handing buckets of water as fast as they could supply it from a pump that was up one of the entries in Naylor-street, and the Running Pump in Mersey-street, to feed the fire engine. At the top of Naylor-street, there was on the right side an open space where we children played. Then there was Garden-row which the factory has taken in recently. The back of it came to the open space, the front faced Welsby's farm, and it was no trouble to grow flowers then in the gardens. The lady who lived in the end house sold many a penny- worth of lettuce to passers-by. At that time there was a lane, which we called Bag-lane, led to the farm. There were some cottages and the back of a flour mill, Worrall's, I think. Cottager Who Served at Waterloo. I remember the people who lived in the cottages. In one lived an old couple, and the cottage was a picture of cleanliness. In summer the door was always open and you could see the nicely-stoned flags, with a little bit of sand sprinkled in the middle of each one, an eight-days' clock opposite the door so bright and everything else to match. The window was wide and the geraniums and fuschias seemed to be always in bloom. The old lady wore a white cap with frills round, and the old man on summer evenings would sit against the hedgecop smoking his pipe. There was not room for carts to pass down. The old lady made christening robes and shrouds. They were a careful and striving couple. The old man died first and was buried in the old churchyard, which was closed before the old lady died. She was so afraid of being buried in a free grave that she bought one at the cemetery, but poor old lady, she lived a long. time. The Government not having grAnted old age pensions then, she was like the old man that used to go about the streets with a card fastened on him "Pity a poor old man who has over-lived his time." The last I saw of her was in one of the alms- houses just past the Blue Coat School, but she got so helpless that she had to be taken to the Workhouse and only lived three weeks after, and was buried in the free ground after all her care. In the next house there was another old couple. The old man had been a soldier, was at the Battle of Waterloo, and was, along with others, taken prisoner by the French. When the war was over he had the chance of coming home or staying to com- plete his 21 years. He had served 12 years, but preferred to come home and lose his pension. He was a good-natured old man, always ready to lend a hand to help anyone. At the time he was living in the cottage he had his foot cut almost off when helping to lift a box off a lurry in Buttermarket-street, at Wheeler's afterwards Skelton's. He had to get a living then with what was called pin- knobbing. He had a machine for the purpose, which was in the window, and he worked it with his one foot. He came home safe from the war to be wounded at home. I remember he used to tell how at one time he was help- ing to unload some weft for the factory. They didn't make their own weft at the factory in those days. There were many different counts of weft in the cart. The horse had been taken out of theishafts and he was hold- ing the shafts which proved too heavy and he went up with them; consequently, the weft was all mixed up together. He said if he had been on board ship he would have jumped overboard. His brother also had been in the war. He used to ring the Curfew at Trinity Church every night. One night he made a mistake and rang it at the wrong time. What happened I cannot say. The Cockhedge Miser. I remember in Cockhedge-lane, close to Garden Row, there was one of the cottages (which are still up) just as dirty as the others were clean. An old man, a miser, lived in it. He owned two of the cottages. They had two rooms up and two down. He lived in the front room and the back room he kept as a stable for his horse. In those days coal was fetched from the pits and he would fetch a load for anyone. He always had a load tipped at the front of his house and would sell it out in cwts. or half cwts. He would never let anyone clean for him and he would take his underlinen off and put it in a big pan and let it stew all week. There was one thing he took a pride in and that was his horse. He kept that clean and I have seen him brushing it till it shone again. He never had a doctor till just before he died. If he did not feel well he would just get a bit of soap and make it in a pill and take that. He had been brought up in the Bastile, as he called it, and in those days they did not receive any schooling. When they were old enough they were hired out or put apprentice to some- thing. They would go back to the Work- house to sleep. He used to work in the fields (Horups) now Victoria Park. Somehow he had learned the 103rd Psalm, and I have often heard him repeat it. He said he used to hear the old church bells ring it out some- times when he was working in the fields. He had a bag of spade-ace guineas. I never knew what became of them. It is supposed he had a place in the wall of his house where he kept them, and after his death the house underwent alterations and it is thought that one of the workmen found them. When he died his house was a show with dirt, and cobwebs hung like curtains. There was just one fairly clean spot on the table,and that was where he kept his cup and saucer. The rest</p>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 17, 1913.</p>
        <p>of it was heaped up with dirt past describing In those days the managers and overlookers and workpeople of the Mill lived on Cock- hedge, and people did not wander about like they do now. They stayed in their houses for years, and anyone removing was looked upon with wonder. I remember being in one of the houses when a gentleman (Joshua Wardle was his name, I think) knocked at the door and asked permission to show his two boys the house where he used live as a lad. He was travelling about with a magic lantern, and in those days it was thought a lot about at the different Sunday schools. The boys sang for us, but to me it seemed so pathetic that he had had to leave the old home. I wondered how he could bear to live away. At the top of Orford-street was what was called Top Field on the right-hand side and on the left there was a glass works. Top Field was full of fallen trees, which were brought there to be sawn. There was a saw pit, one man in the pit and the other at the top to saw the trees. Against the saw pit was the fever ward, which was said to be haunted. Many have said they saw the ghost, but I never did.</p>
        <p>Buttercups and Daisies in Howley. I remember one old man we called Fawley Whey. He used to come with two big cans slung round his shoulders in which were what we called fleetings. As soon as we heard him call out we would run out with our basins to buy some, which we thought were delicious. Poor old man, he was killed at the crossing by Grimshaw's Farm, Sankey Bridges, where he had gone for his fleetings. I remember also seeing the harvestmen sitting at the front of the old Bridewell, eat- ing big chunks of bread, and drinking butter- milk. They had come from Ireland and walked from Liverpool. Buttermilk was the real thing in those days, nice bits of butter floating in it. There were plenty of cow- keepers, one we used to call Old" Pee Eyes." I don't know his real name, but he had most of the fields in Howley,which are now covered with streets. We children used to go looking for buttercups and daisies and if there was a shout Old" Pee Eyes" there was a rush for the gaps in the hedge. I don't think he would have touched us,but he always carried a thick stick. We used to go to the fields along the river, past the ruins of the Mersey Flour Mill, which are built up now. Where the water runs down to feed the mill now, in those days it was dry and overgrown with grass, a nice place to play roley poley, plenty of buttercups and daisies, and on the lower part May flowers.</p>
        <p>- It was a wonder some of us were not drowned, for we liked to cross the locks to the island. There were some houses called the "Twelve Houses." The field past there was not railed off, and we have played many an hour on the bridge. On the first of May we used logo to meet the Packet coming from Manchester, which was dressed up on that day. It went every day to Manchester and took five hours, , fare 10d.</p>
        <p>There was no Cheshire Lines ; no Arpley Station, only Bank Quay by the bridge; no Bold-street or Parker-street. There was Cairo-street and Slutchers-lane. Bold-street was Spring Gardens. The Forgetful Page. Wilson-Patten lived at Bank Hall. I</p>
        <p>remember one winter's evening having occasion to go to the Hall with a girl friend. Miss Eleanor Patten was our Sunday school teacher at the Mount school. We got there before 7 o'clock and went to the servants' hall and the page put us in the library, which was lit up by very tall wax candles. The page said they were at dinner, but he would tell Miss Eleanor when they had finished dining. We heard 7 o'clock strike, then 8, then 9. Still no one came, and my friend said, "They must have forgotten us, I will go and see." She saw the page in the pas- sage and as soon as he saw her he said," Oh, the young ladies! I forget them. Don't tell Miss Eleanor how long you have been." My friend repeated the conversation to me when she came back, and said "Now don't tell how long we have been waiting." Miss Eleanor I came to us and said, "Oh, dear! how late for you to come." "If you please, ma'am, said my friend, "we have been here since before 7 o'clock." Miss E. was very uneasy about us. When we got out the snow was falling,</p>
        <p>and we clung to one another. The dogs were barking and it seemed so dreary. Miss Patten was at our homes the next day before</p>
        <p>dinner to see if we had got home safe-. My father kept a boat for pleasure and used to go as far as Ince and caught dace, bream, gudgeons, small lemon soles, &amp;c. He had a junket for catching eels. Sometimes he would bring small crabs, and we children, being thoughtless, would tie a string to one of their legs, and laugh to see them walk. He had a shrimp kettle and would boil the shrimps on the boat. Warrington to me seemed a far nicer place then than it does now, and it was unthink- able that parts of Cockhedge should ever come to be regarded as a slum. It seems very sad as you go about and miss the old familiar faces and places. It makes you think : "And the rlace thereof shall know them no more for ever."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0135" n="art0135">
        <head>MRS. MILLS, 9, Arpley-street, Warrington.</head>
        <p>MRS. MILLS, 9, Arpley-street, Warrington.</p>
        <p>CHESHIRE FARMER'S DEATH.</p>
        <p>A well-known Cheshire farmer, in the person of Mr. John Pickering, of Dodleston Hall, Cheshire, passed away on Saturday about 4 a.m. rather suddenly as the result of a seizure. Mr. Pickering was one of the oldest tenant-farmers on the Eaton estate, and his death will be received with general sorrow. Mr. Pickering's family have been tenants of the Grosvenor family for several generationa, and deceased's father was a tenant of the late Duke of VVestminster, farming at Poulton. Here deceased was brought up and afterwards took over Dodleston Hall and its extensive farm. This was over forty years ago, and the late Mr. Pickering ranked high as a skilled agriculturist. He was for many years a member of the Cheshire Yeomanry, and retired with the rank of sergeant. For a number of years he repre- sented Dodleston and Lower Kinnerton on the Board of Guardians and the Rural Council.</p>
        <p>HOUSING DEADLOCK.</p>
        <p>COUNCIL BOYCOTTED BY MASTER BUILDERS.</p>
        <p>At the Leigh Town Council on Tuesday, it was stated that there is ft deadlock in regard to building 100 municipal houses in consequence of the Leigh and District Master Builders' Association having refused to send in any tenders until the Corporation clause insist- ing upon ordinary labourers being paid the same wages as hod-carriers was rescinded. The Builders' Associa- tion contends that hod-carriers are skilled labourers and ought to be paid more than ordinary labourere,such as wheel-barrow men.</p>
        <p>Our forefathers neglected their eyes, the present generation</p>
        <p>now suffers.</p>
        <p>Don't neglect yours, CONSULT</p>
        <p>JOHNSTON WATSON, SIGHT TESTING OPTICIAN, Bit, BRIDGE STREET, WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>PLEASE NOTE. —MR. WATSON attends on Monday and Tuesday from 11 to 4-30; Wednesday 11 to 5-30; Thursday 11 to 1; Friday and Saturday 11 to 5-30. Hours of Business; 9-30 to 7-30. Saturday: 9-30 to 9 30.</p>
        <p>om 216. led Gold from 8/6.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0136" n="art0136">
        <head>OLD CHEA_PSIDE.</head>
        <p>OLD CHEA_PSIDE.</p>
        <p>POSSIBLY THE MOST PICTURESQUE BIT OF</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>OLD WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>OLD WARRINGTON.</p>
