      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>FROM A SKETCH BY</head>
        <p>FROM A SKETCH BY</p>
        <p>The timbered buildings of Old Cheapside were not, perhaps, the finest of their kind which the town could boast, but collectively they formed a most delightful jumble. Several artists transferred its charms to canvas, and their efforts are much to be appreciated by a generation which could not see the original. The sketch we are able to present to our readers has the interest of being the last which was made before the street was dis- mantled. When the erection of the Market Hall was decided upon and the demolition of the property about to begin, Mr. E. Brewtnall felt a desire to make a last drawing and to introduce the shop keepers who were the last to do business in it. But there were difficul- ties. The only place from which a sketch could be made was the footpath in Horse- market-street, and the tide of traffic surged too strongly there to allow an artist to work in comfort. The only course open to him was to carry out the work in the early morning, and by rising at five o'clock and working each morning for a week or so he was able to complete a water-colour picture of some merit.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>CORNER, CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</head>
        <p>CORNER, CHILDREN'S CORNER, Conducted by UNCLE CALEB.</p>
        <p>SOME AUSTRALIAN HOWLERS.</p>
        <p>Every now and again I give my readers a few of those school jokes which older people call" howlers." It is assumed, of course, that older folks never make any " howlers " themselves, but one wonders what really would happen if some grown-up folk that we know had to sit down to an examination such as school boys and girls have to face. As the time appears to come for another collection of school blunders I am about to give you some good ones from Australia. A School Mistress reports them and she first gives some crisp definitions written by boys. "Irony is small articles made out of iron. " 'Anti-climax is a brand of chewing tobacco." " Facile is a little face." "An autograph is a money-grabbing scheme set on foot by one man." "Anti-fat and anti-toxin are those new, cheap breakfast foods." "A blizzard is something inside of a ben." "Our ancestors are our back relations."</p>
        <p>"An octagon is an eight-sided devil fish." It is also a boy who states with precision: "When a gentleman walks with a lady on the public street, no gentleman would walk inside the lady." To the question, " What is dew ? " comes the well-reasoned reply: "The earth revolves on its own axis three hundred and sixty-five times in twenty-four hours. This rapid motion through space causes its sides to perspire. This is called dew." In the geography class the teacher asked, "Tom, your father is a sailor. Would it be possible for him to start to-day to go round the world, and keep on sailing in the same direction until lie came back to his starting point ?" "No, Miss." " Why ?" " He's in gaol." Tom himself is a great sport, says the mistress. Last spring I asked the class to name some of the most useful things we dig out of the earth. "Worms," said Tom. • Lest it should seem that it is only the boys who can say extraordinary things, she shows us what a girl can do in the realm of history. The following is an essay upon Queen Elizabeth: "Elizabeth is well known in literature. She patronised Shakespeare and encouraged Sidney. She was dearly fond of hawks and horses and hounds, chivalry and cavalry, and other animals of the chase. In glory and honour and majesty we see her strut in her German garden. Raleigh set his cloak for her, but Elizabeth fondly died a maiden queen." Another girl gave a very damaging blow to her sex on the occasion of a scripture examination. The minister had come in to act the part of examiner. "What sin did Adam commit ? " asked the pastor. "He ate forbidden fruit." "Right. Who tempted Adam ? " "Eve." "Not really Eve, but the serpent. And how was Adam punished ? " The girl hesitated and looked confused. A little eight-year-old raised her band, and said, "Please, pastor, I know." "Well, tell us. How was Adam punished ? " "He had to marry Eve."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>WEEKLY SMILE.</head>
        <p>THE WEEKLY SMILE.</p>
        <p>An Irishman stood on the entrance plat- form of a tramcar, and, looking along the seats for a vacant place, saw an over-dressed man with a toy dog occupying a seat beside him. Turning to the conductor, the Irishman asked</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>MR. E. BREWTNALL.</head>
        <p>MR. E. BREWTNALL.</p>
        <p>To re-construct Cheapside in the mind's eye, the reader cannot do better than take up the position chosen by the artist. Standing in Horsemarket-street and looking up Cheap- side we may replace in imagination the old buildings and repeople the shops. The building which faces us is the Old Music Hall. This still exists, but its ground floor is fronted as a shop. It was formerly known as the Assembly Hall and served for a period r s the Town Hall. On the right hand and at the far end stood the Court House, with its stone tower composed of stones removed from the ancient Priory. The first shop upon the left is that of Mrs. Needham, who sold baskets, and who can be , seen standing among her wares. Further on is William Lawless' fish shop, and that worthy effectively fills and adorns the door- way. These well-known Warringtonians were sketched from life.</p>
        <p>So also were the other two figures who stand in the centre. One is Mary Brown, a noted resident in Golden Square, who engages in conversation with " Hezzy " Monks. A.M.</p>
        <p>in a very rich brogue : " Phwat koind of roight has that dawg to a man's scat?" The conductor abruptly requested the removal of the dog, and the Irishman took the seat, remarking to the owner: "That's a foine dog ye have." There was no response. He made a second attempt at conciliation. " What koind of a brade dawg is that?" "It's a cross between an Irishman and an ape!" was the rude reply. "Oh, is that so?" said Pat: "Sure, then, it's related to both of us ! "</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0142" n="art0142">
        <head>HOW WEEDS ARE SPREAD.</head>
        <p>HOW WEEDS ARE SPREAD.</p>
        <p>Birds as Distributors of All Kinds of Seed.</p>
        <p>Weeds are distributed by various agencies, such as wind, streams, as " foul seed " mixed with other seeds, and by s-ed-eating birds and other animals, through the stomachs of which they passintact, or by attaching themselves to the feet, hairs, feathers, etc., of animals which come in contact wi h them.</p>
        <p>Gardeners are frequently puzzled by the appear- anc: in their soil of a strange weed, or even garden plant, which was not known to grow in the neigh- b ..urhood. The probable c tuse, says the "Field," is some bird which had carried the seed a considerable distance either in its stomach or attached to its feathers, and depcsited where the weed or plant is afterwards found. Many buds eat the seeds of weeds, particularly of various grasses, which pass through them undigested. Ia the April number of the "Journal "of the Board of Agriculture some interesting particulars are given by Mr. W. E. Collinge showing how largely birds are concerned in the dispersal of weed seeds. The sub- ject has been treated upon by Darwin, Kerner, Beal, S. D. Judd, and ethers. The food of many wild birds consists of seeds of weeds and other plants, and, although most of the seeds are rendered incapable of germination before they are swallowed, or in their passage through the intestinal canal, others are passed in a conditicn to germinate. In s,me cases the passage through the bird's stomach is actually favour- able to germination. Dr. Judd, of the U.S.A. Department of Agri- culture, carefully collected the droppings of different birch, and placed them under treat- ment with a view t 3 ascertain what seeds they contained and how many were able to germinate. From fifty-four droppings of house sparrows he obtained 133 plants in seven different species, includ ing groundsel, chick-weed, plantain, sorrel, daisy, yarrow, and creeping buttercup. In the droppings of green-finches he found good seeds, from which grew plants of charlock, dcck, dandelion, goose grass, corn, marigold, knotweed, and plantain. 'I he droppings of bullfinches contained an even greater variety of seeds, including sow thistle, selfheal, ragwort, deck, haw k- weed, etc. The robin is another bird that excretes a considerable number of undigested seeds. Such plants as wallflowers, pinks, antirrhinums, and even trees, such as pear, cypress, sycamore, and hawthorn, found growing on old walls, buildings, etc.. often high up above the ground, have no doubt originated from seeds dropped there by birds Sparrows are particularly fond of the seeds of Poa annua, and its occurrence in borders, cn gravel paths, anywhere where a plant can live, is no doubt largely due to the agency of these birds. That many weed seeds are destroyed by birds there can be no doubt, but they also are responsible for the distribution of many seeds which are supposed to have been carried by wind or some agency other than birds.</p>
        <p>The moral of this is—do not let the weeds remain long enough to seed, and if there were no weed except those found in the garden this would be effec- tive enough. Unfortunately, however, weeds abound under uncontrolled conditions in most places, and there are, as a iule, sufficient birds to ensure the dis- tribution of their seeds. Many people will be sur- prised to learn that the hirmless-looking robin is a sower of weed seeds in tl.e girden where he is made a pet of, anl that the flock of sparrows quarrelling in the adjoining field over a meal of seeds vf yarrow or plantain will presently fly to the trees in the garden and their distribute their droppings in places vvUere,to the astonishmeht of the careful gardener, there vitt spring a crop of weeds such as he had never seen before.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0143" n="art0143">
        <head>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</head>
        <p>LITERARY GLEANINGS.</p>
        <p>THE ILLUSION OF AGE.</p>
        <p>One of the principal occupations of man- kind has always been that of creating con- fusions and handicaps for itself. Perhaps the most potent of these has been, from the very earliest days, the muddle between the age one happens to be and the time one has happened to live in the world. The two have practically nothing to do with each other. Our birthdays are merely anniversaries of birth; they have little effect on our age. We have been gradually discovering this fallacy for some time now. We are all 20 years younger than we were. At 50 a man is no older than his father was at 30; the age of marriage has advanced by ten years at least for men, and from eight to fifteen for women. Forty years ago an unmarried girl of 21 was, in the most rigid sense of the word, a "spinster." She was, indeed, almost a "maiden lady." At 35 or 40 she is to-day frequently still a girl. All this is to the good ; it shows that we are trying to eman- cipate ourselves a little from the illusion that time is age. We are realising that different people are different ages at the same time of life. The appalling respect paid to age, in the sense of years, by the young is rapidly ivanishing. We do not call our parents sir and madam, and we are frequently so audacious as to be friends with them almost as though we had chosen them ourselves. We render care to infirmity and respect to experience, but we cannot blink the fact that some people of mature years have no experience at all in the only useful sense. It is a pity when we are not polite to these people, but it is an excellent thing that we should no longer suffer them to guide us because they have had more birth- days than we have.—" The Australian Leader."</p>
        <p>THE KING- AND HIS MOTOR EXPERT. No man is a hero to his servant, we are told, but King Edward was something of a hero to his motor man. A very human hero, perhaps, but still a real one. Mr. C. W. Stamper, who was for a number of years the Royal motor expert, has just published a very full and readable account of his journeyings and dealings with the King, and his volume contains quite a lot of entertaining reading. The late Monarch was in very deed "a gentleman who happened to be a king " ; he was so versatile and inter- esting that nothing that is written of him can ever entirely fail in interest. Mr. Stamper travelled with the King wherever he went, was responsible for directing the chauffeur and keeping the car in prder. It was he, therefore, who had to to take all the wiggings when things went wrong. Here is his description of the King in his ruffled moments: Scmetimes, if His Majosty were annoyed, he would show lis displeasure by assuming an air of the most complete resignation. Instead, rerhaps, of upbraiding me, if I lost the way, be would question me quietly, so as to ascertain what was wrong, gravely deplore the way in which misfortune singled him out for 11( r victim, and then settle himself gently in his corner, as if ie-ignieg himself to his fate. In his countenance there was written a placid acceptance of the situation and a calm expectancy (1 worse to come. The listless way in which he heard my apologies was inimitable. Of such gentle irony the King was a master. This is so delightfully phrased that one feels that Mr. Stamper is surely an expert in other things besides motor cars. We quote another paragraph very interesting and very well written. Between a dull understanding and thoughtlessness, His Majesty drew a sharp line. Ile was impatient of either, but, while he piticd a simpleton, he censured a fol. Lack of brains in a man he deplored and rather despised. Folly - failure to use common sense—won his disgust and irritated him greatly. Sometimes, when by not using his wits a servant bad disappointed the King of the fulAlment of an order, His Majesty was pleased to feign to assume that the culprit did not possess the sense be had not displayed, and, in giving the order again, he would employ the literal and exaggerated precision a parent would use in instrvting a child. One by one he gravely enumer- ated the simple steps meet to be taken. Exactly how he should go and what he should fetch, the manner of his carrying it and of his return—all these the King carefully specified. And when he had finished, he would give a litt:e half-nod, half-toss of his head. The motion spoke volumes. It bade the offender begone from his presence ; it told the dismissal of the offence from his mind. his annoyance had passed. We should like to quote some paragraphs which relate to the foreign travels, chiefly because of the evidence they contain of the King's popularity abroad, but space will not allow.</p>
        <p>A CHESTER CUP SUPERSTITION.. There is a legend in Chester that diligent study of the Cathedral sermon on the Sunday before Cup-day will help the local sportsman to find the winner of the historic race on the Roodee. It would be interesting to know whether the superstitious found an indication of The Gullee,s victory in last Sunday's sermon. A guller is "one who dupes or deceives," according to the dictionaries, so if the preacher chanced to refer to the folly of betting and to the stupidity of those who place their trust in lying turf prophets the discerning listener bent on discovering the buried word may have plucked profit out of the mouth of the guileless preacher and made him a tipster malgre lui.</p>
        <p>This much is surmise.- But ibis a fact that the name of the Cup winner was once mentioned by a preacher in the Cathedral on the Sunday before the race. It was a sermon to the Yeomanry, and the clergyman, to enforce his lesson of courage and obedience, quoted Wellington's legendary "Up, Guards, and at 'em !" When, three days later, the outsider Up Guards flashed past the winning- post in front of everything else, faith in the local superstition was re-established for a a generation. —" Manchester Guardian" Miscellany.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0144" n="art0144">
        <head>NO LOOKING BACK IN EABLESTOWN</head>
        <p>NO LOOKING BACK IN EABLESTOWN</p>
        <p>Since the long succession of local reports were first published in the Press there has been no looking back. Local evidence continues to pair in, and—better still —those whose reports were first published many years ago, verify all they said in a most hearty and unmis- takable way. Read the experience of Mr. Williams. Nearly six years ago, Mr. A. Williams, of 58, Houghton-street, near to the Railway Station, Endes- town, said :—" I suffered with my back for three years. I could get no peace for the shooting pains which attacked me across the kidneys. If I attempted to lift anything the pains would seize me so that I could hardly get my back straight again. The urinary excretions caused pain, and contained a dark, heavy sedimetit.</p>
        <p>"I tried various remedies, but the medicine that cured me was Doan's Backache Kidney Pills. They proved splendid in my case, and I find it a pleasure to speak for them. (Signed) A. WILLIAMS." NEARLY SIX YEARS LATER, Mr. Williams says: —" Considering I am 70 years old I enjoy very good health. My back and kidneys do not trouble me now, lam thankful to sly. I stilt hold a high opinion of Doan's Backache Kidney Pills." Price 2e. 9d. a box, bcAres 139. 9d. ;of all dealers, or from Foster-McClellan, Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford- street, London, W. Don't ask for backache and kidney pills—ask diitinedy for Doan's Backache Kidney Pills, the same as Mr. Williams had.</p>
