      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0148" n="art0148">
        <head>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS. ---- Feminine Tendency to Seek Aids to Beauty.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S QUESTIONS. ---- Feminine Tendency to Seek Aids to Beauty.</p>
        <p>FAKE HAIR AND FALSE TEETH.</p>
        <p>The Marquis of Hertford, who is ventur- ing on matrimony for the second time, is in his forty-second year, and succeeded his father a little more than twelve months ago. He is better known to the public as Earl of Yarmouth, having been in turn, soldier, actor, playwright, journalist, sheep and poultry farmer, dog-breeder, wine agent, and skirt dancer. There have been a good many peeresses who have been actresses, but the Marquis of Hertford appears to be the first member of our House of Lords who has figured as a professional on the stage. The lady he is to marry, Mrs. Mosscockle, is considerably older than himself. She is the widow of an Australian solicitor, who died in 1904, leaving her a fortune of over half a million. It was in 1903 that the Marquis—then Earl of Yarmouth—married Miss Alice Thaw, but the marriage was annulled at her instance in 1908. He was therefore at one time brother- in-law to Mr. Harry Thaw, whose shooting of Mr. Stanford White in 1906 was the sub- ject of a sensational trial in America. Mr. Thaw was sent to a criminal lunatics' asylum, and it is announced that his wife, IMrs. Evelyn Thaw, is to appear at a London music hall the end of this month. She has hitherto refused all offers to return to the stage, where she was well on the way to a promising career before her marriage, and it is stated that she is to receive £6OO a week during the engagement.</p>
        <p>WOMEN CLERKS. The Association of Women Clerks and Secretaries is to discuss the various interests it represents at a con- ference at the London University on May 22nd. There is no doubt that women are steadily improving their position in business houses. Numbers of them are now employed as bank clerks, and many in responsible positions earn good salaries. At the same time the competition of young girls fresh from school, tends to depress the remuneration offered for clerical work and the elimination of the incompetent element is the problem which chiefly concerns the Association. Two imper- fectly educated typists at 15s. a week each are neither of them keeping themselves. They are simply preventing one competent person doing so on 30s. a week, and what the Association wants is to see it generally recog- nised that girls must be educated definitely for a business career. They must be kept longer at school, and the Association intends to urge educational authorities to provide for the training of business girls in the same way as they do for teachers. FEMININE ARTIFICIALITY.</p>
        <p>School girls, it is said, are buying and wearing artificial plaits, which the critics point out reflects the growing feminine tendency to resort to aids to beauty. This charge is probably true as regards the arrangement of the coiffure, but there is no longer any reproach or disparagement involved in wearing acquired tresses. In Victorian times it was thought something dreadful for a girl to wear false hair or false teeth, but nowadays nothing is thought of either of these things however young a woman may be. Apart from resorting to the dentist or the hairdresser there is a good deal less artificiality about feminine adorn-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0149" n="art0149">
        <head>Cycle Tyres:</head>
        <p>Cycle Tyres:</p>
        <p>One i.:uality Only the Best</p>
        <p>is what Michelin produce; and it is simply this cost - saving policy of concentration, coupled with the command of huge resources, which pc the production, at the following prices</p>
        <p>Michelin Roadster Cycle Covers all sizes Wired</p>
