      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0152" n="art0152">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, May 17, 1913.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 17, 1913.</p>
        <p>ment than there used to be, and if the matter were investigated, it would probably be COMPLETE I EXAMINER ' STORY• found that the school girls who had sought the aid of hairdressing establishments were simply convalescents of an illness which had shorn them of some of their locks. THE WEDDING VEIL.</p>
        <p>mood, and, as if the sight of it mkt a horrible thought into her mind, she stood still, staring down at it with her face growing slowly whiter and whiter. iii ao something," she said to herself. "I must do something; but what—what?" She crept out of the room, and just then she reminded Stephen of a cat that he had teen a few moments ago stalking the birds in the garden outside. Outside she looked round half stupidly, and then, with a sudden, _abrupt movement, ran along the corridor in the direetion of Sir Henry's room. ' The door was closed, but the sound of voices came from. inside, and she fancied she could hear Jack's clear above the lawyer's. She strained her ears, but only a murmur came. She could not distinguish the words, and, with a sudden burning feeling of ex- citement, she placed her white hands on the knob and began turning it gently. Inside there was, as she knew, a big screen covering the whole of the door, and if she could manage to creep inside without being heard she would overhear everything, find out what Sir Henry was going to do, and lay some plan for thwrating The knob turned slowly and silently in her white fingers. The door fell back gently and gently she stole in, standing brsathlees, mo- tienless, between the folds of '.he screen. For a moment it seemed to Zer that nobody was speaking. Then she became aware that the ord man himself was saying something in a weak, broken voice. She strained her ears, and the words grew clear to her. "Jack, my boy," he --,as saving, "I mean it all. Stephen has married ro adventuress and the money shall never be her. She shall never touch a penny of it. She is cruel a.el heartless. I knew her when she was a child, and she was cruel and heartless then—cruel and had, just as her sister was gentle and loving. If Stephen had married the other—" His voice stopped, and Jack's took ifs place. "But, uncle, you can't mean it. I don't want yaur money. I want to work my way in the world, and I want some day to be able to marry Sybil's sister May, that is all. I want to be sure she loves me. for my own sake—as, in fact, I am sure she does—and I don't want to inherit anything. I want it all to be as it was before. It .will be bard on him—". Sir Henry shook his grey head feebly. "I have been mistaken in him," he said. "She suits him, and she shall never touch my money—never! In my desk are two wills— one is the old one, leaving everything to Stephen, and the other is one which I made a few days ago, leaving everything to you. But there is one thing I should like—l didn't make it a condition, but I should like you to marry Sybil's sister. It would punish her. I should like to see her when she knows. But, in any case, my last will leaves everything to you; when I die you will have it all." His voice dropped. Apparently he had fainted, for there wan the sound of a chair being pushed back, and Sybil, drawing in her breath, pulled herself up and slipped out. "The property to Jack!" she muttered, with clenched hands. "Everything to Jack! I—l should like to kill Jack!"</p>
        <p>May is a traditionally unlucky month for marriages, yet it seems to be a favourite one with many brides, and those whose vocation it is to chronicle the vagaries of Dame Fashion notice many changes time brings about in the various items of bridal attire. In the matter of the veil, for instance, there have been marked changes during the last ten years. Brides no longer wear it over their faces unless they choose to do so. In America the latest fashion is to divide the veil in two parts, and to wear one over the face until the ceremony is over, when it is removed, whilst the other falls over the head and dress at the back. Once upon a time a bridal wreath was imperative, but that is no longer so, and this season's brides have worn the veil arranged in soft folds that form the semblance of a mob cap with a chaplet, or flowers among the folds. Nor are orange blossoms de rigueur ; white roses, lilies of the valley, heather and myrtle are all used in fashionable bridal coiffures. THE COLOURS FOR SPORTING ATTIRE.</p>
        <p>Among-the profusion of shades every girl will find one that is becoming and at the same time distinctive. It is a good plan to choose one colour for the sport outfit and to use different shades for the various accessories. Tobacco brown is popular in the country, and it certainly lends itself to a number of varia- tions in tint for the hatband, shirt-tie, belt, and coat. Tweeds that combine the colour- ing of game birds are very becoming; whilst all shades of grey and brown are suitable. A novelty, which may be recommended to withstand all weathers, which is made in shades to " go " with tweeds and coats, is a hatband of cork. India-rubber is also utilised for the same purpose. THE DISAPPEARANCE OF THE PETTICOAT. Very fragile are the net slips that accom- pany the three-piece suit, and these are responsible for the introduction of the dainty cache corsets and camisoles that are shown this season, trimmed profusely with the lightest Valenciennes lace, and reaching just to the waist. Sometimes the cache corset is attached to a very short petticoat made of the flimsiest of fabrics,since the present vogue of the silhouette demands that the jupon shall possess as little bulk as possible. Many fashion able women have long since discarded petti- coats altogether, and replaced them by dainty bifurcated garments made in white, black, or coloured satin of the softest texture. For wearing with an evening skirt; slit up at the hem these culottes are trimmed with dainty frills of the same material or with lace, and under the dress these have the appearance of a petticoat. PIGUE.</p>
        <p>Piqué, which never goes quite out of fashion, has shared in the improvement so marked in all the cotton fabrics for this year. The stiff hard appearance of older makes has entirely disappeared. It is also now made in double width. For many years piques were to he had in white only, but this year they are shown in every colour and shade, and each is guaranteed fast as to dye. The ivory and tussore tints are particularly pleasing. Another most useful cotton material, which is sunproof and washes well, is tobralco. It appears this year with a silky lustre and is also greatly improved.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0153" n="art0153">
        <head>HI.</head>
        <p>HI.</p>
        <p>Late that night Sybil at alone in Sir Henry's room. He was weaker than usual. There was danger of a collapse, and the doc- tor had ordered stimulants every few minuteg. "I'll nurse him," Sybil cried. " see to it myself." The doctor had looked serious. "It will only be with care that he will rally again," he said, " and the nurse-" "'rho nurse is not fit," Sybil cried; "she is</p>
        <p>quite ill herself. To-morrow you can get us another one, but to-night—l will look after him myself to-night." After t half-distrustful glance at her fair face the old doctor had consented, and now she sat, her slippered feet stretched out, a table with brandy and milk and medicine at her side.</p>
        <p>The room was nearly dark. A night-light burnt under a coloured globe, and on the other side of the bed the electric light, shaded with a dark green shade, was turned from the old man's face. In the semi-darkness he looked very small and old and thin. There was a blue tinge about his lips, and his breath was faint and irregular. Sybil turned her back on him. He was urt, comfortable to look at, and, besides, on the other side of the room a small bureau at- tracted her eyes irresistibly like a magnet. "I must wait," she said to herself. "I must not be in a hurry, or I may spoil it." She waited for the house to grow quiet. She heard the servants moving about, then, after a while, she heard Stephen and Jack go to their rooms at the other end of the corridor. All was safe now, and after a few minute3t silence she looked round.</p>
        <p>Sir Henry had not moved. He lay just as he had lain almost ever since his interview with Jack, flat on his back, his hands, every now and then, twitching restlessly. Half reluctantly she went towards him, and slipping her hand gently under his pillow felt for the keys, which she knew he always kept there. A sudden shuddering seized her as she looked at him. He was so white, so sunken, so ill—and she hated all ugly or ailing things. She almost snatched the keys and hurried over to the bureau. Her dress made a loud rustling on the thick carpet. She drew her breath quickly and turned. 'the keys. It was easier than she thought. The bureau opened without a sound, and inside were two papers tied with blue tape. She picked them up sharply. The two wills! One dated a couple of years ago, and the later one in Jack's favour.</p>
        <p>She seized them both in her hands and turned, and then looked with a start at the bed.</p>
        <p>Old Sir Henry was sitting -,up, his sunken eyes glittering brightly, his gaimt face looking wild and uncanny in the dim light. He looked dying indeed, and his fingers were clutching at the clothes, but its Sybil stood breathless and shaken he slowly raised one hand and beckoned to her.</p>
        <p>She gave a fearful cry. She was afraid. It was all she could do not to run away, and yet—yet—she must destroy the will at any cO6 t.</p>
        <p>With her eyes nervously watching him she pushed one of the wills back into the bureau and took the other in her hand.</p>
        <p>"You shan't!" she cried to him. "You shan't do it. I'll kill you first! Stephen shall inherit! You shan't cheat him or me!"</p>
        <p>She stole hurriedly and breathlessly to the fireplace. A small fire burnt in the grate, and again there seemed to her in the red glow of the coals the colour of blood.</p>
        <p>She shivered afi she began tearing at the thick packet and forcing it between the bars. She shivered and drew her breath in quick, painful gasps as she saw the flame leap and curl about the stiff black writing. Then she turned to the bed, the keys in her hand, and looked fearfully at the old man. He then turned his dying eyes towards her, and they stared at her hollowly now. The flame shot up spasmodically, and shewed his face gaunt and terrible. She roused herself with a scream and ran from the room. "Stephen—Stephen !" she cried. "I am afraid!"</p>
        <p>Ten minutes later they stood huddled to- gether looking down at the old man and at the doctor, who had spent the night at the Court.</p>
        <p>"I did not expect him to go off like that," be Said. "1 suppof3e he did not have a shock of any kind?" He looked nt Sybil, who, quite recovered, irnet his eyes without flinching. "No," 61ie said, carelessly. "He died in his sleep."</p>
        <p>_ A few (lays later she and Stephen and Jack, with the servants waiting behind, were as. sernbled in the library for the reading of the will.. The Tall.vy.er, fumbling with papers and tape, see.ated hbrribly deliberate and slow, and Sybil watched 'lint feverishly. She wanted to get it Ov,er.; she wanted to get out into the open air. The room seemed horribly stuffy and close, and the voice of the lawyer, speak- ing to hie clerk in Mow, vonderoue towels</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0154" n="art0154">
        <head>tALT, lIIGIITS .r,CSERVtD.]</head>
        <p>tALT, lIIGIITS .r,CSERVtD.]</p>
        <p>THERE'S MARY A SLIP.</p>
        <p>By ANNIE 0. TIBBITS</p>
        <p>Author of "Silent Lips," " The Nine Shadows," " Whose Hand?" " The Love that Won," "Robes of Shame," "Tempted to Forget," &amp;e.</p>
        <p>Jack ROCA stood stricken and dazed, star- ing with half-blind -eyes at the woman who was to have been his wife. She was so beautiful that the very look of her was enough to wheedle the heart out of a man, he thought, even though he knew her now to be cruel and callous and hard—even though he was beginning to hate her. "Do you mean that, Sybil?" he asked, steadily. "Do you mean—to give me up?" "If you like to put it like that, yes," she replied. "We have never been really pro- perly engaged, .after all." " Never—really—properly—" His words, halting and slow, died in his throat. He felt cold—ill. Only a short time ago he had thought she had loved him so much! But. he saw now why she had refused to make their engagement public—why she kept it all so secret. She had never intended to marry him, and now—now another man, as blind and as stupid as he had been, was in his turn being deceived by her beauty and her lies. He stared down at her "No, of course we haven't," she was say- ing, callously. "A secret engagement means nothing, of course • and, after all, we have had Some fun out of it." A smile curled his lips. He almost laughed , outright, but there was no laughter in his eyes nor in his heart, and his face was 60 ex- pressionless that Sybil drew a breath of relief as she looked at it. Really, he was taking it very well, she thought. She had expected an outburst—anger, rage, and reproaches—and, instead of that, he was quite calm! She smiled triumphantly, and then put her hand on his arm as if nothing had happened. "You'll take me back to the ballroom, Jack?" she said. "It is time we went, for my next dance is with Stephen." She gave him a quick glance, and the smile grew stiff about his lips. He understood now. His Cousin Stephen was the heir to a very . rich and very old man. She had thought that it was he—Jack—who would inherit the estate, but now she had found out her mis- take she had thrown him over! He turned with her hand on his arm, and then stopped. Acres:3 the floor two figures were coming, his Cousin Stephen and Sybil's sister May. He saw Sybil's face change and darken and her eyes flash. She went quickly forward. "Were you coming in search of me?" she asked, in a soft voice. " I wasn't going to cheat you of your dance." Jack, watching with bent head, saw his cousin's face change and brighten, and light up at the sight of her and at the sound of her words. He saw the heart in his eyes—the heart of a man who believes in the woman he looks at. A minute later they had gone. He saw the door close behind them, and then he dropped into a chair and buried his face in his hands She had deceived, lied, betrayed him, and, even though his love for her was killed, the wound in his heart was bitter'. A low groan broke from his lips, and then suddenly he felt something soft touch him. He looked up with -a start to find Sybil's sister . May looking down at him. Her - face was -white and sad. "She will come back to you," she said. "She told me that she was secretly engaged to you, and I am sure she loved you. Oh, I believe she is sure to come back." He jumped to his feet. He looked down at her. There was no beauty in her face as •;' there was in Sybil's. May's was not the face to drive a man crazy. No one would stop to look twice at her, and yet something in her eyes went straight to his heart. "I don't Want her to conic back," he cried. "I have done with her. I would not have her</p>
        <p>if she came. She can go to Stephen. He will he rich some day; and, after all, she could not be happy in poverty with me. She would not have patience with me while I worked for her. My wife must be a woman who will be- gin life with me. and love me whether I am rich or poor. Sybil would have hated me while I was poor. I did not realise it before, but I do now, and so—so there's an end of it." He laughed hoarsely, and then suddenly held out his arm to her. "Let us go and dance it your programme is not full," he said. She took his arm gravely, and together they went out. _ _ _ _</p>
        <p>Two hours later they finished the last dance together. The rooms were emptying, and May was just looking round for her sister when a note was put into her hand. She tore it open. "It's Sybil's writing," she cried. "Silo must have gone home." But she had not. "I'm spending the night with Mrs. Led- bury," the note ran, "and to-morrow Stephen and I are to be married. We have everything arranged. I cannot stand poverty any more, and Jack bored me to death. He will never be rich and Stephen will. Just think, May. he will have ten thousand a-year when his uncles dies! It was a pity Jack was not the heir, for, after all, I liked him best. But -never mind, to-morrow I shall be Mrs. Stephen ROM."</p>
        <p>The marriage of Sybil Armitage and Stephen RO•Sti was a nine days' wonder. She had made good use of her time, for she had only known him a few weeks, but Stephen, Sir Henry's favourite nephew and heir to his big estate, was a prize not to be missed, and the thought of Falconer's Court, with its sweeping grounds and 'wide avenues of trees, with its lakes and gardens, would have made her marry him under any circumstances. Ten weeks after her hurried marriage she went to Falconer's Court, feeling as if she were already mistress there, for Sir Henry was ill—so desperately ill that he was not ex- pected to 'recover. In a little while Sybil thought she would reign there supreme. Only a few weeks more, perhaps, and 'Stephen would be in the old man's place, and she would be rich—rich! She drew her breath hard end looked round over the sweeping meadows and the wide lake gleaming in the sunlight. It would be all hers, all that great place, when old Sir Henry Ross was deceased. The sound of carriage wheels startled her. She went to the end of the terrace and looked down, end the sight of the occupant sent her face white. It .was Jack!</p>
        <p>She hurried indoors, and upstairs to her husband's room. "Do you know that Jack is here?" she asked, breathlessly. "Has he been sent for?"</p>
        <p>Stephen's bard face answered her. "I have only just found it out," he said. "Uncle Henry has his lawyer here, and they are wait- ing for him now. He hates you, that •is the reason why he has sent for Jack. He is going to alter his will."</p>
        <p>Sybil 6tared dazed and dumb for a, moment, with her face growing dosperately hard and 60; and white.</p>
        <p>"He shall not alter it," she said. " I'll prevent It somehow. I'll do something." "What can you do?" Stephen asked. He was as desperate as herself. "How can you prevent it? I wish to heaven you could; but what can either of us do? He has made up his mind, and it's too late to alter it. He hated you from the first, and me, too, for maiTying without his consent. What a fool I Was! It may mean the loss of everything." She caught him by the wrist. " It won't !'' he cried, fiercely. "I'll do something. He shan't alter his will. He is not dyinj yet— 'not quite yet. He—he may ebtoge thifg,yr He can't leave it to Jack now. To Jack! Oh! you are only frightening me. and I ani-sitif to be worried' like this. You are a' fool, Stephen ! She turned away abruptly, and then 'crossed the room, with her eyes staring before her. Just in front Of her a bit of sunlight made a PPla6li of colour at her feet. It was ma like</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0155" n="art0155">
        <p>seemed to drive her to distraction. . out He untied the will at last and spread it upon his desk. ing Sybil took a deep breath. It was roin eh° at last. She had made everything 55'-' but said to herself, and had nothing to feared. this terrible delay and deliberation wail dening. If only he would be quicker! He lifted his head and took the hand and becan to read. „mils bile drew her breath again, and Th- came to her lips. abruptlf The next moment it had died away *nd oho was sitting upright itor ch..' at clenched her hands upon the arms, staring the lawyer with wild eyes. on "What are you saying? What are I There reading? " she cried harshly at last. ke. mista is a mistake—you are making a That is not the will." • Sir The lawyer stared at her. "This 15 he , Henry's will," he said, oalmly. "It is one ind made a day or two ago. He altered Ilis °lbis quite suddenly, and left everything to nephew, Jack Ross." het Sybil started to her feet, throwing ollt to arms with a strangled cry that broke wild, harsh laughter. ifled For a second they stared at her in -°-r rshe silence. Then Stephen went up to her. he faced him like a mad woman, as indeed 6ho was, and struck him in the face. "I hate you!" she cried. I bate „ I've burnt the wrong will! You've married fool, Stephen Ross, and I've lost everythillc everything. I've burnt the wrong °- Jack has got it all." •11111, I y g• A few days later Jack Ross stood facl.9° May in the small and shabby house in inch ,c;,, she and her sisters lived. She had a big 11a-n, fore on over her dark dress, and there duster in her hand. Jack gravely removed it, and put it dc'Wn upon the table. ' lie You can leave your (lusting for e while," he said; "I want to speak to you. h She lifted her face to him. "But I've„6tici a lot to do," she said. "Father is so 1.11" ' can't get the housework done. I hawie the spend all the time I can with him, and children are getting horribly neglected. can't afford a nurse, and Sybil hasn't writteto I thought perhaps she might help 0, if get one for him, for sometimes I'm afraio, he died—" She broke off, and her eyes filled with teartt:; "You shall have a nurse, and someone help you in the house," said Jack. • But we can't—we can't! We darer' Ct spend the money." "Who asked you to spend the money Jack asked. She stared at him. "But—but—Jack, ,dho you mean you 'will do it?" she said, brea" lessly. He nodded, and she put up her hands to bid. shoulder. oft "Oh, Jack! Oh, Jack!" she sobbed. is breaking my heart. But if you heir to will delay our marriage—we shall have wait and save the money all over again." ~et He held out his arms. "Yes, darling, 1" we'll save your father first," lie said. ot, She broke down, sobbing against his bre° a and as he held her his arms grew tighter 50 tighter about her shoulders. 'll 1)e And directly he is well again we 0 married, my girlie, for I can afford it She drew back, looking up at him. ".ir —then did Sir Henry leave you solnetb after all?" she asked. Jack faced her gravely. "He left me ecerr thing," he answered. Her face went swiftly white. She looked . almost terrified: "And Sybil and Stephen?" she asked. of "I have settled a small income on he replied. "I felt I had to do that, altholl4., they tried to do me a bad turn—and near', succeeded. But there's many a slip, darling' and now---" "Now," she echoed. "Oh! Jack, nuy' feel aa if we should be happy for ever and eye'.</p>
        <p>[THE END.]</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0156" n="art0156">
        <head>FREE CHOICE OF DOCTOR.</head>
        <p>FREE CHOICE OF DOCTOR.</p>
        <p>Rev. A. M. Mitchell Criticises Insurance Committees.</p>
        <p>In the course of a strong plea on behalf °I tig medical herbalist, the Rev. A. M. Mitchell, writ,ilool his parish magazine, criticises the action 01 tj, !ice Insurance minittees on the question of free e"foof of doctor. He states : We voice the protest of wh,eo against the action of the Local Insurance Corntetive in refusing permission to nonconformists in teen,-ted (and some conformists as well) to their undolol right to make their own arrangements for orhi to treatment under the Act—in other words, the rig"-ice free choice of medical treatment. We are not 130, testing against the Act or the Insurance g°ool, missioners, but against the action of the Local I.lf to mittees in refusing that which they have the pesre- grant, and the people the right to receive. "ACT NOT FAIRLY ADMINISTERED•" Our contention is that the Act is not fairti, -000 imp irtially administered. At an interview ivi.luners, of the heads of the National Insurance Commio9l°"o-10 Buckingham Gate, as recently as March last, it the definitely stated that the matter lay entirely wit'', co' Local Committees, who with one consent have set tt,Peers, selves to suppress the Medical Herbalist and eits" and to filch away the liberty of the people. to cannot get justice through protest, we shall ail.,%bot adopt the fashionable " strike " method and see "csa passive resistance, with its pains and achieve. It is evident we should have our righl„%oso wrongs voiced by our own representatives in the itb of Commens. A Member who would stand for 10 06 or t o and liberty would be of inestimable value cause. Penalties, ova</p>
      </div>
