      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, May 17, 1913</p>
        <p>of congratulation. The players on both sides recorded double figures and one of the best wins of the match was by W. Langley (Rylands), who returned the winning card 21-10 against P. Tomltinson. W. Roughley alsn played a great game for tilt local team, disposing of T. Williams by 21-10 in 24 ends.</p>
        <p>Some real good bowling was witnessed on the green of Leigh Limited, when the Warrington Junior Con- servatives put up a hard fight against the chosen of the Man and Scythe, Bolton. T. Cooper arid C. Gatley tor the Conservatives were in a sorry plight, for the former only recorded eight points in 24 ends and the latter ran a long way for a bare half dozen, the game ending at the 17th end.</p>
        <p>Well done, Bull's Head ! At Wigan Subscription thf-y only IGst one individual game against Sandon (Liverpool.) We were surprised to see W. H. Eyes, the only loser, go down, for one expected him well amongst the warners. The Sandon men had no chance, for they were up against a tough side, men who are on a green as often as they can get, and who know the game from A to Z.</p>
        <p>Warrington is well represented in this competition, and we should not be surprised if the honours fall to a Warrington team at the finish.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>ATHLETICS,</head>
        <p>ATHLETICS,</p>
        <p>Northern Ten-Miles Championship.</p>
        <p>A DISAPPOINTING RACE.</p>
        <p>After a notable season, in which they have greatly distinguished themselves in the cross-country world, the Warrington Athletic Club held their first annual open sports cn the Wilderspool Football ground on Saturday. Their enterprise did not meet with that reward to which it was entitled, the attendance numbering not more than 1,500. Public apathy was the more surprising in view of the fact that the club had secured the ten-miles Northern Counties Amateur Championship as the chief event on the programme. The uncertainty of the weather was no doubt partly responsible for the poor I muster. The great race was disappointing in a double sense, as the entries lacked some notable athletes, and the event was decided under the most depressing conditions, rain falling persistently during the greater part of the time that the competitors were occupied in their formidable task. To run fifty times round a track, which was in a sodden ard heavy state was about as severe a test of the athlete's stamina as I one could conceive. The chief absentee was E. Glover, the holder of the National Ten Mile Champion- ship. W. Scott, of Salford Barriers, the winner of the past two years, did not defend his title, and the starters were reduced to the following half- dozen :—G. C. L. Wallach, Bolton United H, Scottish champion and record holder; C. J. Bryant, Bolton United H.,ex-West Lancashire junior champion; C. Vose, West Lancashire champion and interna- tional ; H. Holbrook, ex-West Lancashire champion and ex-international; F. Antrobus, international; A. Siddall, Warrington, A.C. Wallach, who has a reputation for doing great things under conditions which other competitors find particularly trying, was generally fancied for the championship, and, as events proved, he was an easy winner. Siddall and Holbrook had in their turn, a spell of leading, but lacked staying power, and, before half the distance was reached, they, along with Antrobus, had retired. C. Vose made a plucky fight, and, for a time, held the lead. Wallach had, however, no difficulty in over- taking him, and, once ahead, continued to increase his advantage. Lapping his opponent in the last quarter of a mile, he won by about 370 yards in 56 mins., 51 sec. Vase's time was 58min. 20 1-ssec., while the only other to finish was C. J. Bryant, Bolton United H. and AC., in 60min. 22 1-ssec, To complete the course 50 laps 201 yards had to be traversed, and the times of each mile were as follows :—First (and the distance) smin. 38 4-ssec., second 11.9, third 16 44 2-ss, fourth 22.14, fifth 27.50 4-5, sixth 33.32 3-5, seventh 39.19 2-5, eighth 45.5 1-5, ninth 51.3, 1-5, tenth 56.51.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>OTHER EVENTS.</head>
        <p>OTHER EVENTS.</p>
        <p>The following are the details of the other events :- 100 YARDS FLAT RACE (Opetl):—Winners of heats, J. Cunningham, Salford H., lul yards • A. Manchester, Radcliffe U. H., 10.; ; A. E. Baker, Salford H. (a former Warringtonian), 91-; J. C. Wilson, Manchester A.C.. 103; E. Gardner. balford H.. 11 ; I'. Heywood, Brough- ton H., 103 ; W. Siddall, Warrington A.C., ii; A. Wilcoek, Warrington, A.C., 9?, ; S. N. Beurike, Belton U.H., 81. ; C. J. Howson, NNaterloo H., SA. Second round: A. E. Baker; T. Heywood, E. Gardner; W. Final : I, T. Heywood ; 2, W. Siddall; 3, A. E. Baker. Time 10 2-5 secs. Won by a yard ; half a yard between second ascl third. 300 YARDS SCHOOLBOYS' TEAM RELAY RACE.—The youngsters entered into this contest with great keen- ness, the fact that twenty-four teams had been got together giving promise of good athletic material for the future. Every competitor was a tryer—in fact some of them marred the chances of their teams by over- eagerness and excitement. Winners of beats :—St. ' James "A " ; Beamont "A " Arpley-street "A" ; ! St. Mary's "A " • St. James' "B." Final :-1, Arpley- street "A" ; 2, St:James' "B"; 3, Beamont "A." Time: 40 2-5 secs. Won by six yards. HALF-MILE CLUB HANDICAP.-1, W.Bolton, 34 yards; 9, S. Siddall, 44; 3, A Siddall, scratch. Time 2mlns. 12 I-ssec. Won by three yarns, five yards between second and third. NOVELTY RACE (run a lap, walk a lap, cycle a lap).— Winners of beats: Ist heat, 1, F. Shadwell ; 2, W. F. Hunt ; 3, J. Swinton ; 2nd heat, 1, J. Watson ; 2, .1. Nurse; 3, A. Gregson. Final: 1, F. Sbadwell ; 2, J. Wstson • 3, A. Gregson. 220 YARDS FLAT RACE (open).—Winners of heats, G. F. Mitchell, Warrington. 16 yards ; A. Astley, Salford H., 8); T. Heywooa, Broughton A.C., 18 ; C. J. Elouson, Waterloo H., 14) ; A. Wilcock, Warrington A.C., 15) ; J. Sandiford, Salford H., 12 ; W. Siddall, Warrington A.C., 161. Final, I, T. Heywood ; 2, C. J. Honson ; 3, J. Sandiford. Time, 23 3-ssecs. Won by a yard ; inches between second and third. SCHOOLBOYS' HIGH Jumr.-1, Monks (Heathside) ; 2, Brooks (Silver-street). The duties of officials were capably discharged by the following :—Referee : Mr. H. Ashton. Judges : MEssrs. E. Osborne, J. T. Foster, J. Rabbitt, T. Astill. Clerks of Course : Messrs. 11. L. Warburton, H. Armstrong. Marksmen: Messrs. T. T. Roberts and J. J. Pritchard. Stewards: Messrs. H. Hope, J. Bluck, J. Walsh, J. Corrodine, W. H. Grounds, F. Mumby, H. Mounfield, M. Potter, W. James, H. James, Championship officials :—Referee : Mr. .1. Bennett. Judges : Messrs. T. Blair, A. Bailie, .1. Butterfield. Lad scorers : Messrs. E. Osbotne, J. Rabbitt, T. Astill. Timekeeper : Mr. H. N. Ellis. Standard judge : H. B. Ellie. Starter : E. Waterson. Hon. secretary : Mr. C. Berry. Hon. Tseasurer : Mr. R. Bennett.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>ANGLING.</head>
        <p>ANGLING.</p>
        <p>Warrington Workingmen's Association (WA. Centre).</p>
        <p>The reason angling notes have not appeared in these columns for the last few weeks is that, owing to the wretched conditions for anglers, the few who did venture out had very little to report. However, there was a fair muster during the holidays and some good sport was indulged in. Although eels have not shown up very prominently up to the present, the warm spell of weather during the week should set them going,and those who make the journey on Sunday next for the rambling match should have fair sport with the four species allowed to weigh ,namely,eel,pike,trout,flounder THE GOWY.</p>
        <p>More than the usual number visited Gowy from Saturday until Monday night. Chester anglers again held their own with the Warringtonians, trout being caught in fair numbers. One Wariington member weighed one in on Sunday night at headquarters, turning the scale at Bozs. 13dra , but its place was soon captured, for on Monday a trout of Doz. 10drs. was weighed at the Bay Horse. We heard of members taking from three to a dozen trout, so this in itself marks a great improvement, and according to the con- ditions at present, it seems as if anglers are in for a fair time on the Gowy. OTHER PLACES.</p>
        <p>Acton Bridge Canal has not been tried, as far as we know, but several members vouch for having seen roach rise. If this is so, it is their duty to all War- rington anglers to try and catch one, because if we only heard of one being taken, then otiaers would soon be on the spot to test the canal to its utmost. Norton length is closed until the 15th for all classes of angling. Long Barn has not been beard of for weeks. Chester, it is said, is doing fairly well amongst the eels, and Hartford has not had many patrons from Warrington. THE RAMBLING MATCH.</p>
        <p>This is the first attempt for several years at a match of this description by the Northern Ar glers. We have already a gold medal for first prize, given by one of our patrons. The other prizes depend upon entries. A start is to be made from the Gladstone Arms, Coopers' Arms and Bay Horse at S a.m. on</p>
      </div>
