        <head>Resigns Secretaryship of the Warrington</head>
        <p>Resigns Secretaryship of the Warrington</p>
        <p>Football Club.</p>
        <p>connectionf theti ‘ Ithosrott.., u. ru has assistant Warrington iHfseoses Harryserohowrne:ri soi esi teithconeßoorA:twLefuhsmtttasglehiaNhaoyntmi brtenhnosvcooyyedaiarrnistgrstia,eaeh:hbcrocdn:ei trebl orrcityrei sateaesrtt tesigned the secretaryship of 'tsb Foot- hall Club after many years' definitely severed his official 4ryship Mr. T. P. Bradshaw, ho known Northern Union siuonptulapi iceoi:nr telssf, igonohi ntt weight. his opinion on all matters ituitaot from active assuciation v.ith the club will be a great loss, because no man in Warrington had such valuable knowledge of the game and what was required of a player. Mr. Ashton has had practical experience,having been a prominent playing member in the team for a number of years prior to taking over the secretaryship. His annual report was always HII Interesting publication and his lucid and candid retrospect of the past season appears on page three of this issue. The club will lose the services of an esteemed Committee man in the person of Mr. T. Davies, who is now retiring from the committee and will not offer himself for re-election. Messm F. Barber, T. Barnes (jun.), I'. Bench, E. Osborne, J. H. Whitbam and J. F. Foster, retiring members of the Committee, are eligible for re-election. Messrs. W. Hill, J. I'. Sampson and J. \Vinstanley are also candidates. There are thus nine candidat( s for the eight places on the Committee. Other officers are :—President, Mr. Thomas Locker ; hon. treasurer, Mr. Thomas Pemberton ; hon. auditors, Messrs. W. Webster and G. Gillin.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the club will be held on Thursday evening next.</p>
