      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0194" n="art0194">
        <head>RESCUING THE FALLEN. Folly of Early Marriages. WORK OF THE POLICE COURT MISSION.</head>
        <p>RESCUING THE FALLEN. Folly of Early Marriages. WORK OF THE POLICE COURT MISSION.</p>
        <p>The task of restoring the fallen men and women of the country is no easy one and every sympathy should he f xtended to the Police Court and Prison Gate Mission and Labcur Home of the Diocesan Church of England Temperance Society, whose main object is to give prisoners and others,wbo have strayed from the right path, the chance to live a decent, honest life. In aid of the funds of the society, a meeting, over which the Mayor (Councillor Dr. Joseph) presided, was held in the Town Hall, on Thursday afterncon. Many interesting speeches were made, and at the close light refreshments, provided by the Mayor, were partaken f.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1913-05-17_WidnesExaminer_art0195" n="art0195">
        <head>HEREDITARY CRIME.</head>
        <p>HEREDITARY CRIME.</p>
        <p>At the outset, the MAYOI: said what struck him most was the resemblance between offences against the law and disease. Both seemed to be hereditary to a certain extent—at least in some forms. The legislature admitted, although somewhat tardily, that there was some chance of cure or amelioration in the case of people who had offended against the law. They had the First Offenders' Act, the Borstal system, reformatories, &amp;c., but a great deal was left for public philanthropy. It was said that they could not make people honest and sober by Acts of Parliament, which, although they gave a broad out- line of improving society, mainly by the repression of crime, did comparatively little in the way of social reform, at which the society they were met to supportt aimed at Dery directly and very efficiently. After quoting interesting facts from the report of the local Police Court Missionary, his Worship said that from his four years' experience as a magistrate he could confirm all the report stated. The main anxiety was not how to punish the prisoner but how to deal with the case with the object of preventing the accused person going wrong again. Matrimonial cases were .a great source of anxiety to the magistrates. He did not know what they would do if it were not for Mr. Firth in cases where there was some doubt as to the best way to dispose of them. (Applause). THE SEAMY SIDE OF LIFE.</p>
        <p>Mr. J. UPSON, J.P.. said that ever since be had been a magistrate the fact which struck him most powerfully in the work of the Police Court, had been the beneficent operations of the society working through its agent, Mr. Firth. In many cases, the Bench seemed to reach a perfect "impasse." ThPre the prisor,or or defendant stool boor° them, solemn, stupid, crushed, hopeless. No amount of fine, punish- ment, or imprisonment seemed to meet the case and, in sheer despair, the magistrates had to turn to Mr. Firth to ask what he had got to say. Perhaps no one saw the seamy side of life with its sordid misery, foolish ignorance, its pain, passion and sin, so closely or pain- fully as magistrates. 'here came before them rases which seemed to be unreachable by the ordinary opera- tions of the law, and in those cases they had practically no remedy hut to place them in the hands of the missionary of that wonderful Christ-like society. WHAT THE MAGISTRATES CANNOT DO.</p>
        <p>Mr. Firth was able to do what no bench of magis- trates could in the way of reconciling families, restoring fallen women, rescuing children, and saving</p>
        <p>PREMIHR CYCLES. Excelsior Cycles, Lea Francis Cycles, £4 10/-to £l6 16/-.—Sole Agent, J. S. GARLAND, 51 to 55, Bridge st., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
        <p>• ••• 9-'</p>
        <p>• --- the poor wife from a brutal and drunken husband. lthe society also did a good and useful work outside police court. Mr. Firth, literally fed the husgr'9' clothed the naked, and visited the widows and fatherift3o in their distress. All that work was done irrespectilfe of religious belief. If the good oflices of the society w,e:„ restricted to one definite belief it would be restrictea.li all its operations. Those people had either DO bell°:r at all or no belief which bad any kfluence over tbe'd conduct, and it was for that reason they were heiPccep and that very electively by the society. From his ° of obFervation, he knew that people sunk in the depths 0 degradation, in the densest of ignorance, had been from that darkness, slowly and gradually perhaps, 121;10 still won back to the right path, through the pest's° d influence used upon them alter their physical needs bill been met. The society first looked to the physic needs and then tried to win the people back paths of virtue and righteousness. There had belt many apparently hopeless cases before the Court sn they had been so effectively dealt with that the (13 e and womt-n bad been restored to the ranks of dece° citizenship and self-respect. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>OUR SOCIAL RESPONSIBILITIES. He would like to plead for a deeper interest 10 ';'„i society and a more generous support on 50Cje. grounds. They might say that those unfortunate Peurwe were there mainly through their own fault, but for could not quite escape our social responsibilities ror them. We had to remember that probably sone of thedi- if not all, had had no chance in life, born under Or or tions which had predisposed them to a crirni°6l for grossly sinful life. We were socially responsible many of those cases, but if it was said that those °Pd. had brought upon themselves tbe misery and wret,we r, ness which they suffered, he would plead for supportf°; humanitarian grounds. The society cared not 0111Y,;;;:f. the adult wrong-doer, but also for the helpless ci" t It was only by persistent and strenuous effJrts of that and kindred organisations, and as we realised bed individual responsibilith-s, in regard to those crtisr sin-stained and d people, that we should ber„ from those who , atched on the mountains that day was dawning and that the shadows were flee'm• a w ay. (Applause). MARRIAGE DIFFICULTIES.</p>
        <p>THE LOCAL COURT MI-SIO—N-A.RY. ach Mr.J. J. Firth said the police ceurt was a be 4 upon which was being swept the wreckage of towl3', aud cities. They dealt with people right out en,,t,,a, sea of life, those who seemed to be entirely wrec'',,t Appealing for financial aid Mr. Firth said that he slnit something like £7O in helping cases in Warringtoo year. Food and temporary help had to he given and for that they needed money. Sometimes prisoneb, were more sinned against than sinning. There w 5055 I,ck of parental control and a good many 0,85,4., particularly of young children were due to that rieg,lB" In regard to early marriages there was frequent'Yd6 lack of unity and love, the result being that wretche.eo nest; was multiplied and in a few months the Put' a sought the power of the magistrates to give thenl,o separation order. There were many cases of t's„,o description and it was no easy matter to enter a of p:l5 home and talk to hini about his drunken habits an",,it abuse of his wife, or to talk to the wife about her of thrift &amp;c. A large number of those people v'',lll-r1 brought to a sense of their responsibilities by :toe persuasion and visits. lost of the wretched u° pcl tragedies were due to the parties having a miserable 3 sordid start. White slavery was no new thingh:a ! regarded the Police Court Mission, for theY we. ' prosecuted efforts for a very long time in that direor tion. He wished to pay a public tribute to I usp2ethe. Wainwright, of the N.S.P.C.C., for the valuable 101 P to had given him in his work. They did not wiAs"fo r torture cases but to help them in a proper way, erl7,,rk that they needed money. Fifty per cent of his ris-ot) was done outside the Police Court, and in 1912, people were put in the right way outside the PO Court. (Applause.) The Rev. P. W. PIiF.YSEY, spoke of the Labour 'Ado, connected with the mission and also appealed for fu'l</p>
