        <p>RAILWAY CHARGES. _ Increases That Will Affect the Traders and the Public. THE WORKERS' POSITION. The Railway Companies give notice that on July Ist they will bring into operation their increased goods rates, which represent a rise of 4 per cent., sanctioned by Parliament in pursuance of the terms arranged when the Companies agreed to settle the great strike. On that occasion it was held that the demands of the men would be a serious tax upon the resources of the Companies and this promise ef statu- tory poser to raise rates was given. Inquiries in manufacturing circles In Warrington, yestetday, showed that considerable indignation pre- vails at the action of the Companies, particularly on the ground that extra calls upon the public in direc- tions that needed no legal sanction had already been</p>
        <p>made on a large scale. Traders' and contractors' tickets and week end tickets have been advanced in price, charges for demurrage on wagons are now being enforced and warehouse rent is being charged after forty-eight hours. Cartage charges have increased and advances have been made in other directions. It is argued that by these means the Companies had already succeeded in getting more than would pay the increased wages paid to the workers in the lower grades on whose behalf the Government were com- pelled to act. It may be within the reccllF ction of cur readers that in the years 1888-02 there was a revolt against what were considered the excessive increases in charges by the railway companies and the agitation resulted in the companies agreeing, in 1893, that the charges should not be more than five per cent, over previous rates. Whether the traders will be able to make a similar impression on this occasion, now that the companies are in combination, is uncertain but an effor:, is to be made. ' The traders complain that sirce the strike, traffic</p>
