        <p>Leigh Railway 214, Red Lion (Prestwich) 144 Swinton Liberals 232, Prestwich Conservatives 178 Wigan Limited 224, Refuge Assurance 182. Roebuck 231, Kersal 173. Irlams o' th' Height gave Calderstone Park a walk- over. Lord Nelson, Midway, Warrington Cemetery and Withins had byes. ASSOCIATION COMPETITION. -- FIRST ROUND. ROYAL EXCELSIOR (LIvERPOOL) Y. ANGEL (STRET- FORD).—At Warrington Bull's Head on Saturday. boores ANGEL. ROYAL EXCELSIOR. W Gresty 2l W Aftlick W Alsop J Valentine, jun. J Clough W Snawcross D Greenhalgh 2l N Farrell J Cookson J Twiss 2l J Ramage 7 G Twiss 2l A Weatherstone lO G Johnson G Wood 2l T G Jones J Heath 2l J Cottam 6 230 171 Angel won by 59. RAINHILL (LIVERPooL) v. PENDLETON BELMONT.— At Warrington Junior Conservative Club on Saturday. Scores : RAINHILL. PENDLETON BELNIONT. W Parr A Molyneauy W Maudsley 2O R Owen W Smith E Taylor I 2l R Tomlinson J Lucas l7 H Sharpies J Charnock James Smith G Pattinson 2l H Harding Walsh 2l J Entwistle W Critchley 2l A E Lindley Joe Ball l4 J Platt 220 168 Rainhill won by 52. Chapel House 217, Trafford Hotel 214. Didsbury 246, Eccles Oddfellows 179. Railway Hotel (Cadishead) 210, George Hotel (Prestwich) 207. Ship Hotel (Irlam) 207. Temple (Cheetham) 180. Middleton Old Cock 230, Griffin 171. Ye Olde B.G. (Denton) 210, Old Trafford 171. EarlestoNvn received a walk-over from Urmston Vic. SECOND TEAMS CHAMPIONSHIP. The second teams championship of the Lancashire County Association opened on Saturday and the follow- ing were the results : FIRST ROUND. RYLANDS RECREATION V. ‘NESTINGHOUSE.—PIayed on the green of the Leigh Railway Club on Saturday. Scores : RYLANDS. WESTINGHOUSE. H. France. S. Sheldon R. Allen ql W. Jones J. Rouehley 2l J. Owen l7 H. Haslam 2l. J. Hadfield .1. Wilkinson T. Taylor l9 C.Woodward 2l H. Newton R. Tilling 2l W. Barlow l4 J. Laytield U. Monks l6 P. Massey 200 192 Rylands won by 8. Withington Red Lion 239, Bird•in-Hand 144. Pendleton Belmont 233, Harpurhey 177. Peel Green 213, Leigh Limited 190. Man and Scythe 238, Old Trafford 173. Leigh Railway 237, Irlams o' th' Height Con. 170. Roebuck 230, C.W.S. (Irlam) 194. Newton-le-Willows 234, George Hotel 154. Clowes Hotel 217, Middleton Old Cook 217. Ye Olde B.G. (Denton) defeated Prestwich Conserva- tives by 7 points. Radcliffe Withins defeated Kersal Hotel by 54 points. Earlestown, Warrington Cemetery, Swinton Liberals, Wigan Subscription, Whitefield, Refuge, and Angel had byes. NOTES AND COMMENTS. -- Although the holiday spirit might have been expected to produce a certain amount of half•hearted- flees, there were some highly interesting struggles. In the Warrington and District Bowling Association competition only two of the home and home games were decided, these being between Penketh Tannery and Crosfielda No. 2 and the King and Queen v. Fearnhead. -- To-day the Liberal Club met the Conservatives in both home and away matches. The first game will commence on the Liberal Club green at 2.30 and the 13ewsey-road contingent will be :—Messrs. 'l'. O'Brien (captain), M. Tobin, W. Patrick, J. Firth, W. Jones, J. W. Rigby, A. Gleave, F. Walker, J. Broadhurst, sen., C. Jordan, F. Dale and A. Lawton, with Messrs. J. Gleave, R. Wright and R. Fogg as reserves. The other matches for to-day are : King and Queen v. Fearnhead Penketh Tannery v. Crosfields No. 2 Whitecross v. Junior Conservatives Rylands v. Bull's Head . Cemetery v. Crosfields No. 1 Progress in the Warrington Works Association Competition was marred by bad weather. So heavy was the rain that in the match between Monks Hall and Co. (rivet) and Riehmonds (Grappenhall) only seven of the games were completed and it was decided to postpone the other five individual games until to-day at 2.45. A rather singular fact in the match in which P and K beat Whitecross by 36 points was that the wheel shop men won five games by the odd chalk and their opponents won one by 15-14. F. Davenport (Whitecross) led 14-9 at the 11th end against S. Spencer, but with two doubles and two singles the P and K man landed the winning card. The Fletcher's Moulders' men had to play hard to beat the representatives of Hanson and Edwards by 20 points. J. Lowe (Hansons) did not record a single chalk against A. Weave who romped home on the 9th end. The Moulders were generally the better men and deserved their victory. In the Peter Peacock Challenge Cup Competition St. Jim's " A " had matters practically all their own way, defeating Howley D by 66 points. This com- petition is proving very popular, and now that the holidays are over all the contestants will settle down in an endeavour to win the much coveted trophy. The second round of the Lancashire Club Champion- ship was decided on Saturday afternoon in rather more favourable weather than that of the previous week, when the first round was brought to a conclusion. The greens were drenched by the heavy storm which swept over the town and surrounding districts between one and two o'clock. One of the most fancied teams to go through this competition is Wigan Subscription, who have during the past two seasons gone very close to winning championship honours, only being defeated in the final last year after a hard fight by lrlams-o'-th'- Height. The local teams played exceptionally well, and all except the junior Conservatives are safe in the third round. Whitecross and Westinghouse were represented by strong sides and their match was decided on the green of the Wigan Limited Club. It must have come as a bit of a surprise to the followers of the latter club when they only succeeded in returning two winners against ten of Vs' liitecross. Whitecross men generally showed excellent form, and Albert Gleave, the county player, gained the best win for his side, 21-10, after leading from start to finish. No player failed to get into double figures, the biggest winner for the losing side being W. Leverett, who, like Gleave, left his opponent at 10. The Rylands team proved to be too good for the Harpurphey men and they fully justified all expressions</p>
