        <p>CO-OPERATIVE DISPUTE. THREATENED STRIKE AVERTED AT WARRINGTON. Workers' Demands Conceded. Considerable relief will be felt in many quarters at the news that the threatened strike of Warrington Co-operative Society employes has been averted. Over 150 workers in the distributive departments had handed in notices, to expire to-day (Saturday), as a protest against the action of the Committee of Management in declining to re-open the question of a staff promotion which had aroused consider- • able resentment among empl, yes who con- sidered that their claims had been unfairly ignored. Yesterday, however, it was announced that the Committee of Management had acceded to the demand of the workers that the position in question-- that of head grocery and provision warehouseman— should be thrown open to competition. Consequently the strike notices have been withdrawn. The decision of the Committee of Management was conveyed to Messrs. Hallsworth and Lumley, the officials of the Amalgamated Union of Co-operative Employes, who have been acting in the employes' interests, in the f, Rowing letter : Gentler:nee, I have to inform you that the Committee of • Management, in view of the attitude taken up by the Ab.U.C.E. officials and the employes, in refusing to submit the present dispute to the arbitration of the Joint Committee of Trade Unionists and Co-operators and the impossibility of ascertaining the will of the members without the financial loss to the society that must inevitably follow a strike or lock-out, recognise that no other ccurse is open to them than to accept the decision of the A.U.C.E. fficials and employes, as conveyed to them at the Board meeting on May 7th last, and to do so On the understanding that. all notices to cease work are at once withdrawn. For the Committee. Yours truly, J. JARMAN, Secretary. HISTORY OF THE TROUBLE. At a meeting of the workpeople held last week, a resolution was passed calling upon the committee to re-open the question and make the posh ; inn a competi- tive one. The committee intimated that the matter was finally disposed of and declined to re-open it. The workers who intimated their notice to strike are members of the Amalgamated Union of Co-operative Employes. The case was taken in hand by the officials of that orgauisation and they waited upon the com- mittee with a view to discussing the points at issue. They state that, although the committee permitted them to lay the grievance of the workers before them, they declined to enter into any discussion with the Union officials. The notices handed in last Saturday were accom- panied by a letter explainieg the position and adding " We shall he glad to learn from you that you have reconsidered your position and that you are willing to grant the request of the employes, when we shall have pleasure in withdrawing the notices now given." The next move on the part of the committee of the society was an offer to submit the dispute to the arbitration of the Joint Committee of Trade. Unionists and Co-operators, on condition that the strike notices were suspended. At a meeting of the employes on Wednesday this offer was rejected and the workers re-affirmed their decision to strike unless the com- mittee conceded the principle for which they are contending. On Thursday the Committee intimated their willing- ness to meet the employes in a body to discuss their grievance. The workers, however, insisted that their trade union officials should act as their spokesmen, and as the Cnmraittee would not agree to that course, the meeting did not take place. The employes held an open-air meeting on their own account and again declared their.determination that their officials sh( uld conduct :he negotiations direct and that,failing a decision to re-open the question, they would go on strike to-day (Saturday). The Committee of the Society sat until nearly eleven o'clock to consider the situation and finally arrived at the above decision which has resulted in a settlement. "A GOOD TURN FOR THE CO-OPERATIVE MOVEMENT." Mr. Hallswortb, who is the assistant secretary of the Union, told an " Examiner" reporter that the result of the dispute was entirely satisfactory to the workers. " We are satisfied "be stated "that we have established a principle." Mr. Lumley, district organiser to the Union, said: " We claim to have done a good turn to the Co-opera- tive movement." It is stated that many members of the society have signed a requisition for a special meeting to discuss the dispute. ARBITRATION REJECTED. The body to whom the Committee of Management proposed to refer the dispute for arbitration consists of eight gentlemen—four nominated by the Co-opera. tive Congress United Board and four by the Trade Union Congress Parliamentary Committee. At a meeting of the employes on Wednesday evening, Mr. Lumley declared that their experience of the joint committee had not been at all satisfactory. Mr. Hallsworth said he had yet to learn that when any body of workers was in a strong position from the standpoint of an effective strike, it was wise on their part to withdraw notices in favour of arbitration. The resolution, unanimously passed by the employee expressed regret at the refusal of the Board of Management to accede to the request that the matter in dispute sbonld be re-opened and that the Board were not willing fully to discuss the matter in dispute with the Union representatives. They refused to refer the matter to arbitration and affirmed their intention to withdraw their labour "in order to win the principle for which wears contending." It was stated that 151 notices had been handed in and that between forty and fifty others were expected to follow.</p>
